1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Bülbül Hatun''' (c. 1452 - c. 1515,[1]) (Bülbül meaning "Nightingale") was the third wife of Sultan Bayezid II.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Life==
2 CONT
2 CONT Little is known of Bülbül’s early life. The Ottoman inscription (vakfiye) describes her as Hātun binti Abdullah (Daughter of Abdullah) which means that her father was possibly a Christian who converted to Islam.[1][3][4] Bayezid m
2 CONC arried her in 1465 at Amasya.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Turkish tradition, all princes were expected to work as provincial governors (Sanjak-bey) as a part of their training.[5] In 1481 Ahmed, Selim's principal rival, was sent to Manisa sanjak (then known as Saruhan) and t
2 CONC hen in 1483 to Amasya, and Bülbül accompanied him.[5] After the death of Şehzade Ahmed, Bülbül Hatun came to Bursa in 1513 when her son was executed by Selim. Bursa was the first Ottoman capital, and the place where, until the con
2 CONC quest of Constantinople, members of the dynastic family were buried.[5] In retirement she occupied herself with pious works[5] She built a "Hatuniye Mosque" at Amasya in 1510. She had already built and endowed a religious college 
2 CONC in Bursa, and then she also built a tomb for Ahmed, in which she was too buried at her death in 1515.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Children==
2 CONT
2 CONT Together with Bayezid Bülbül had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Hatice Sultan (Amasya, c. 1465 - Bursa, c. 1550). married firstly 1479, Damat Kara Mustafa Pasha (killed at Söğüt, 1483), Vizier, married secondly 1484, Damad Faiq Pasha;
2 CONT * Şehzade Ahmet (Amasya, c. 1466 - Yenişehir, 24 April 1513) Governor of Sarihan 1481-1483 and of Amasya 1483-1513, married Bülbül Hatun, daughter of Şehzade Ahmed;
2 CONT * Gevhermülük Sultan (Amasya, c. 1467 - Istanbul, c. 1550), married 1480, Damat Dukakinzade Ahmed Pasha (killed at Amasya, 4 March 1515);
2 CONT * Hundi Sultan (Amasya, c. 1470 - Bursa, c. 1511), married 1481, Damat Hersekzade Ahmed Pasha (Hersek, 1 May 1456 - Maras, 21 July 1517), Grand Vizier 1487, 1497-1516;
2 CONT * Şehzade Mahmud (Amasya, c. 1475 - Manisa], c. 1507), Governor of Kastamonu 1504 and of Sarihan 1504-1507.
2 CONT
