2 CONT
2 CONT Sultan Murad in literature
2 CONT
2 CONT In William Shakespeare's History play Henry V,
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Harry refers to Murad as "Amurath" in Act V Scene 2 when he succeeds his father, King Henry IV, in 1412:
2 CONT
2 CONT Chief Justice. Good morrow, and God save your majesty!
2 CONT
2 CONT King Henry V. This new and gorgeous garment, majesty,
2 CONT
2 CONT Sits not so easy on me as you think.
2 CONT
2 CONT Brothers, you mix your sadness with some fear:
2 CONT
2 CONT This is the English, not the Turkish court;
2 CONT
2 CONT Not Amurath an Amurath succeeds,
2 CONT
2 CONT But Harry Harry!
2 CONT
2 CONT Murad (as "Amurath the First") is the subject of Thomas Goffe's play The Courageous Turk, published in 1632.
2 CONT
