1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Nur Sultan''' (1451–1519) was the wife of Mengli I Giray (r. 1467–1515). She was one of very few women known to have had influence over the affairs of state in the Crimean Khanate. She is also one of few well kn
2 CONC own women of the Giray dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Life=
2 CONT
2 CONT Nur Sultan was the daughter of Prince Timur ibn Mansur, bey of the Manghits. She was the sister of Tevkel.
2 CONT
2 CONT She first married khan Xälil of Kazan (r. 1466-67). After his death, she married his brother Ibrahim of Kazan (r. 1467-1479). She became the mother of Möxämmädämin of Kazan and Ghabdellatif of Kazan. She supported the neutrality p
2 CONC olicy of her spouse against the more aggressive policy of her stepson Ilham Ghali. She was widowed when Ibrahim Khan died in 1479. Her spouse was succeeded by her stepson Ilham Ghali of Kazan. She left Kazan with her two sons and 
2 CONC sought refuge with Ivan III of Moscow. Her brother Tevkel enterred Crimean service in 1503.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1487, she married Mengli I Giray. The marriage was possibly made to give the Crimean khan an influential power base in the power struggle in post- Golden Horde Central Asia. She left for Bakhchisarai on the Crimea in the compan
2 CONC y of her youngest son Ghabdellatif, but left her eldest son in the custody Ivan of Moscow. She became one of the wives of Mengli I Giray. The women of the Giray dynasty, though an Islamic dynasty, did not yet live secluded in a ha
2 CONC rem, since until 1564 the royal women were still allowed to receive male visitors in audience. Nur Sultan herself never lived in seclusion.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nur Sultan was highly politically involved from the start. She wished to support her son's right to the throne of Kazan in the power struggle against their halfbrother, her stepson. She wrote to Ivan II of Moscow and asked him to 
2 CONC intervene in Kazan politics to defend the rights of her son, which gave Ivan III a reason to interfere in Kazan politics. Ivan II joined Mengli I Giray and launched a campaign on Kazan, which resulted in Muhammed Amin being placed
2 CONC  on the throne of Kazan. However, the power struggle in Kazan continued, and Nur Sultan became a long term supporter of the alliance between Moscow and the Crimean Khanate on order to protect the interest of her son in Kazan.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1494-1495 she made a pilgrimage to Mecca with her brother, as well as Egypt. On her return, she gifted an Arab horse to her diplomatic ally Ivan III, who in return promised her that Kazan would always remain the property of her
2 CONC  family. In 1510–1511, Nur Sultan visited her sons in Moscow and Kazan in the company of her stepson Sahib I Giray. During her journey she signed a peace treaty between Crimean Khanate, Moscow and the Kazan Khanate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nur Sultan issued yarliqs (priviligies, grants, orders and appointments, and messages addressed by the khans to their vassals) in her own name despite being a woman and not the reigning khan. In 1515, her spouse died, and was succ
2 CONC eeded by his son Mehmed I Giray.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was one of few examples of the Giray harem who ever had any influence over the affairs of state, alongside Ayse Sultan, wife of Devlet I Giray (r. 1551–1577) and Emine Sultan Biyim, wife of Mehmed IV Giray (1642–44 and 1654–66
2 CONC ), have been historically acknowledged as politically influential.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nur_Sultan_(wife_of_Mengli_I_Giray)
