1 OCCU khan of the Crimean Khanate 3 times: 1466–1467, 1467–1469 and 1475–1476, Князь, Крымский хан
1 TITL khan
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Nur Devlet (Crimean Tatar: Nur Devlet‎, نور دولت) was a khan of the Crimean Khanate (1466–1467, 1467–1469, and 1475–1476) and the son of Hacı I Giray, the founder of the Crimean Khanate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon the death of Haci I Giray in 1466, rivalries broke out between his sons. Nur Devlet, the eldest son, took power in 1567 but was overthrown by Meñli I Giray in 1467, only to regain control. In 1467, he sent an embassy to Polan
2 CONC d, announcing his ascent to the throne and subsequently seeking to establish an alliance on equal terms with his father. Poland replied politely on favorable terms.
2 CONT
2 CONT In a new offensive, Meñli I Giray supported the Genoese, who returned to take power in January 1469. Nur Devlet took refuge in Moscow.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1475, the Ottomans, at the request of the Shirin Beg, captured the Khanate of Meñli I Giray, but his expedition was not directed against the Khan but against the Genoese. The capture of Khan (near the castle of the Seven Towers
2 CONC ) led to the recovery of power by his brother, Nur Devlet, who was recognized by the Turks. But the Ottomans went to war against Moldova, and Nur asked to send a contingent to support the Turks to make a fun, and Nur Devlet could 
2 CONC not do it, perhaps because they had not yet consolidated their authority. Then the sultan was freed in exchange for Meñli I Giray becoming an Ottoman vassal. It is said that he was given an army, and it is unclear whether the goal
2 CONC  was to overthrow Nur Devlet, but there was a reason: this time, he was defeated and expelled from the Crimea in 1476 by Ahmad Khan of the Golden Horde, who named his nephew Janibeg Khan. More likely, it was after the Golden Horde
2 CONC 's conquest of the Crimea that the Ottoman army gave Menlo (1477) and then dominated the Crimea (1478).
2 CONT
2 CONT After being expelled from the Crimean throne for the third time in 1476, he retired with his brother Hayder Giray in Lithuania and later in Russia towards 1480; this year, his son was killed by a Ber Devlet tartar guilty of killin
2 CONC g Nur Devlet with his own hands. Shortly after, Haydar was banished by the great prince in Vologda. Nur Devlet took part in the struggle against the Golden Horde, and while the great prince faced the Tatars, Nur Devlet was fun and
2 CONC  captured the capital; he was likely to reward the great prince, Khan Kasimov Daniya Khan, when he died in 1486.
2 CONT
2 CONT Murtaza Khan of the Golden Horde, sworn enemy of Meñli I Giray, wrote to him to form an alliance among the Great Horde, Kasimov, and Moscow against the Crimean Khan, but the operation failed. Not mentioned again after 1487, he die
2 CONC d but not before 1498 or after 1503, and was succeeded by his son Satilghan Khan.
