1 NOTE  La majeure partie du Kosovo se trouve alors sous la domination du prince serbe Vuk Brankovic.
2 CONT Serbian medieval nobleman, Knez (Prince) of Raska-Kosovo, who ruled from his capital Pristina to Prizren in the West and Skopje in the South of Balkans, during the 14th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT He lost Ohrid, his territories being reduced to a small holding around Drenica.  Taking advantage of the weakness of Marko King of Serbia after his accession and following the battle of Marica River in 1371, Vuk expanded his autho
2 CONC rity over Kosovo and Macedonia, acquiring Skopje and Priština by 1376/77 and Prizren after the death of Djurdje Balšic in Jan 1379[214].  After Lazar Hrebljanovic and Tvrtko Ban of Bosnia defeated Nikola Altomanovic, nominal Knez 
2 CONC of Hum, in 1373 Vuk acquired Sjenica and Zvecan.  He fought with Lazar Prince of Serbia at the battle of Kosovo 1389.  He was depicted in later Serbian epics as a traitor, which Fine suggests was due to an effective propaganda cam
2 CONC paign by the supporters of Regent Milica (whom Vuk later opposed) as, according to Turkish sources, he is reported as having fought well.  Ottoman armies forced him to surrender Skopje in 1392, and accept Ottoman suzerainty.  He w
2 CONC as driven into exile in [1395] by the Ottomans, angered at the alliance he concluded with Venice in 1394.  Vuk may have been captured and died soon after in captivity.
