1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Dukaginzade Ahmed Pasha / Progon Dukagjini''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dukaginzade_Ahmed_Pasha En], [https://tr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dukakino%C4%9Flu_Ahmed_Pa%C5%9Fa Tr]
2 CONT
2 CONT Dukaginzade Ahmed Pasha (Albanian: Ahmed Pashë Dukagjini; Ottoman Turkish: دوقکین زاده أحمد پاشا; Turkish: Dukakinoğlu Ahmed Paşa; died 1515), born Progon Dukagjini, was a high-ranking statesman and military commander of the Ottom
2 CONC an Empire in the early 16th century. He hailed from the Albanian [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dukagjini_family Dukagjini family], one of the strongest in pre-Ottoman medieval Albania. Dukaginzade Ahmed Pasha was the son of Nicho
2 CONC las Paul Dukagjini and Kiranna Arianiti Comnena.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1503, he had become sanjakbey of Ankara and was married to [https://www.geni.com/people/Gevher%C5%9Fah-Han%C4%B1msultan/6000000205086825844 Gevherşah Hanımsultan], daughter [https://www.geni.com/people/Ay%C5%9Fe-Sultan/60000000
2 CONC 29696926961 Ayşe Sultan] (daughter of Sultan [https://www.geni.com/people/Bayezid-II-of-the-Ottoman-Empire/385226639660012698 Bayezid II]) and [https://www.geni.com/people/G%C3%BCveyi-Sinan-Pasha/6000000205086883882 Guneyi Sinan P
2 CONC asha], another Ottoman Albanian general, and had with her a son [https://www.geni.com/people/Dukanginzade-Ibrahim-Pasha/6000000205087505857 Ibrahim Bey] and a daughter [https://www.geni.com/people/Fatma-Han%C4%B1m/6000000205087861
2 CONC 894 Fatma Hanım], while his [https://www.geni.com/people/Dukakinzade-Mehmed-Pasha/6000000205088299827 son] by his first wife married [https://www.geni.com/people/Gevherm%C3%BCl%C3%BCk-Sultan/6000000029696787207 Gevhermüluk Sultan]
2 CONC , a daughter of Sultan [https://www.geni.com/people/Bayezid-II-of-the-Ottoman-Empire/385226639660012698 Bayezid II]. Later, he married [https://www.geni.com/people/Hafsa-Sultan/4874391169070046850 Hafize Sultan], a daughter of Sul
2 CONC tan [https://www.geni.com/people/Selim-I-of-the-Ottoman-Empire/6000000008518541049 Selim I.] Dukaginzade Ahmed Pasha was one of the commanders who supported Prince Selim in the Ottoman succession dispute. In 1511, as a result of t
2 CONC he large revolt of the janissaries, he became beylerbey of Anatolia.[1] In his new position, he played an instrumental role in securing that Selim would be the next Sultan in 1512 and had an important impact in the military victor
2 CONC y against Şehzade Ahmed, the pretender to the Ottoman throne on April 15, 1513, in Yenişehir. Dukaginzade Ahmed Pasha may have been the commander who captured Şehzade Ahmed in the battle.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT By the summer of 1513, he became joined as a vizier (minister) in the Imperial Council (diwan) and was responsible for the negotiations with Venice about possible Ottoman support to Venice against H.R.E. Charles V.[2] In 1514, Sel
2 CONC im I began his campaign against the Safavids which culminated in the Battle of Chaldiran. At the beginning of the campaign, Dukaginzade Ahmed Pasha was at the head of the vanguard of 20,000 sipahi. His activity in the early stages
2 CONC  of the campaign in contemporary sources is unclear, but in the battle of Chaldiran on August 23, 1514, he and the other viziers were at the centre of the battle line next to Selim.[3] Around September 7, when the Ottoman army rea
2 CONC ched Tabriz, the Safavid capital, Dukaginzade was in the delegation which went ahead of the army in order to accept the city's surrender to Selim.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was Grand Vizier of the empire between December 1514 and March 1515. Then he was executed by [https://www.geni.com/people/Selim-I-of-the-Ottoman-Empire/6000000008518541049 Selim I.], who thought that he was involved in the ongo
2 CONC ing revolt of the janissaries. His son, [https://www.geni.com/people/Dukakinzade-Mehmed-Pasha/6000000205088299827 Dukakinzade Mehmed Pasha], was a governor in several regions including Egypt Eyalet. He built the Al-Adiliyah Mosque
2 CONC  complex in Aleppo, Syria where his family was based.[5] The El Adli Dukaginzade are his descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Ottoman sources, Dukaginzade have been used to refer to him
2 CONT
