1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * [https://digitalna.nsk.hr/?pr=iiif.v.a&id=601954&vwopt=%7B%22pages%22%3A%5B75%5D%2C%22pan%22%3A%7B%22x%22%3A0.216%2C%22y%22%3A0.664%7D%2C%22view%22%3A%22thumbnails%22%2C%22zoom%22%3A0.397%7D Znameniti Hrvati Bošn
2 CONC jaci i Hercegovci u Turskoj Carevini/ Safet beg Bašagić str.65]
2 CONT * http://tr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Damat_R%C3%BCstem_Pa%C5%9Fa
2 CONT * http://hr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rustem-pa%C5%A1a_Hrvat
2 CONT Rustem-paša Opuković (otomanski tur. رستم پاشا, tur. Rüstem-paşa) (Skradin, o. 1500. - Carigrad, 1561.), osmanski general i državnik hrvatskog podrijetla iz Bosne[1]. Poznat je i kao Damat Rüstem-paša (damat - mladoženja osmanske 
2 CONC dinastije), te također Hırvat Rüstem-Paşa (Hırvat - "Hrvat" na turskome). Jedan je od poznatih pripadnika zajednice Hrvata u Osmanskom Carstvu, koji je živio i radio na prostoru današnje Turske.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Rüstem Pasha Opuković (Turkish pronunciation: [ɾysˈtem paˈʃa]; Ottoman Turkish: رستم پاشا‎; Croatian: Rustem-Paša Opuković c. 1500 – 10 July 1561) was a Croatian or Serbian-born[1] Ottoman statesman. He served as the grand vizier 
2 CONC of sultan Suleiman the Magnificent. Rüstem Pasha is also known as Damat Rüstem Pasha (the epithet damat meaning "son-in-law" to the Ottoman dynasty) because of his marriage to one of the sultan's daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT The Rüstem Pasha Mosque (center), overlooked by the Süleymaniye Mosque (upper right) dedicated to Suleiman the Magnificent.
2 CONT
2 CONT Of Croatian or Serbian[2] descent, Rüstem Pasha's birthplace was Skradin, a town today located in Croatia. He was taken as a child to Istanbul, where he built a military and bureaucracy career. On 26 November 1539, he married Mihr
2 CONC imah Sultan, the daughter of Suleiman the Magnificent and Hürrem Sultan. Rüstem Pasha held the title Grand Vizier twice, first from 1544–1553 and second from 1555–1561, until his death. As Grand Vizier, he collected vast wealth. H
2 CONC e spent a chunk of his wealth raising public buildings, mosques, and charitable foundations. At the time of his death in Constantinople on 10 July 1561, his personal property included 815 lands in Rumelia and Anatolia, 476 mills, 
2 CONC 1700 slaves, 2,900 war horses, 1,106 camels, 800 Qur'ans, etc.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Rüstem Pasha Mosque (Turkish: Rüstem Paşa Camii) is an Ottoman mosque located in Hasırcılar Çarşısı (Strawmat Weavers Market) in Fatih, Turkey,[3] which was designed by Ottoman imperial architect Mimar Sinan for Grand Vizier R
2 CONC üstem Pasha. It was built between 1561 and 1563.[4] Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Together with Mihrimah Sultan, he had three children; one daughter and two sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Ayşe Hümaşah Hanımsultan
2 CONT     Sultanzade Osman Bey
2 CONT     Sultanzade Mehmed Bey
2 CONT
2 CONT Popular culture
2 CONT
2 CONT In the acclaimed Turkish television series Muhteşem Yüzyıl, he is portrayed by actor Ozan Güven. See also
2 CONT
2 CONT     List of Ottoman Grand Viziers
2 CONT
2 CONT References Footnotes
2 CONT
