1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The Historian's Den
2 CONT Hayreddin Barbarossa, originally named Khizr, was born on the island of Lesbos in the late 15th century. He began his career as a corsair alongside his brother Oruç Reis, targeting Spanish and Portuguese ships in the Mediterranean
2 CONC . Their activities drew the ire of European powers, leading to multiple exiles. In 1516, the brothers captured Algiers from the Spanish, with Oruç declaring himself Sultan. After Oruç's death in 1518, Khizr took on the name Hayred
2 CONC din and continued their mission, securing the city and expanding their influence. Recognizing his prowess, the Ottoman Empire offered him support, appointing him as the admiral of their navy. This partnership allowed Barbarossa to
2 CONC  consolidate his power and establish the Regency of Algiers, which later evolved into modern-day Algeria, Tunisia, and parts of Morocco.
2 CONT
2 CONT Under Barbarossa's leadership, the Regency of Algiers became a formidable maritime power. He successfully defended the region against multiple European incursions, most notably at the Battle of Preveza in 1538, where his fleet dec
2 CONC isively defeated the Holy League, securing Ottoman dominance in the Mediterranean. Barbarossa's naval strategies and alliances, including a notable collaboration with France against the Habsburgs, were instrumental in his success.
2 CONC  His legacy as a pirate-turned-admiral and state-builder remains significant, illustrating the complex interplay of piracy, politics, and empire-building in the 16th century.
2 CONT
