1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00079239&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Nikita Romanovich
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Nikita Romanovich also known as Nikita Romanovich Zakharyin-Yuriev (Russian: Никита Романович, died 23 April 1586) was a Muscovite Boyar in 1563 whose grandson Mikhail Feodorovich founded the Romanov dynasty of Russian tsars. He w
2 CONC as son of the Boyar Roman Yurievich Zakharyin-Yuriev, Okolnichi, who died on 16 February 1543, who gave his name to the Romanov Dynasty of Russian monarchs, and wife Uliana Ivanovna, who died in 1579, and the brother-in-law of Iva
2 CONC n IV of Russia, who had married his sister Anastasia Romanovna.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nikita Romanovich is first recorded in 1547, when, on account of the tsar's wedding with Anastasia Zakharyina, he was promoted to spalnik and stolnik. He participated as a rynda (bodyguard) of the tsar in the unlucky campaigns aga
2 CONC inst the Khanate of Kazan in 1547 and 1548. Later, he was the assistant to the Princes Vasily Serebryany and Andrey Nogtev-Suzdalsky with the rank of okolnichy in the Livonian campaign of 1559.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was granted a boyar dignity in 1562. Four years later, following the death of his brother Daniil Romanovich, he became the governor of Tver. He commanded detachments of the Muscovite army during the winter campaign of 1572 in N
2 CONC ovgorod and against Sweden. He also took part in the Livonian campaigns of 1573 and 1577.
2 CONT
2 CONT On his deathbed Ivan the Terrible left his two sons, Fyodor and Dmitry, to the care of trusted associates. Until the illness incapacitated him in late 1584, Nikita Romanovich led the regency, as the only uncle of the young tsar. H
2 CONC e died on 23 April 1586 and was buried in the Novospassky Monastery.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married twice, to Varvara Ivanovna Khovrina (d. 18 June 1556) and to Princess Evdokia Alexandrovna Gorbataia-Schuiskaya (d. 4 April 1581), a sixth cousin of the future Vasili IV.
2 CONT
2 CONT His children by first marriage were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Fyodor Nikitich Romanov
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna (d. 1585), married to Prince Ivan Fyodorovich Troyekurov (d. 29 May 1621)
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphimia (d. murdered 23 March 1602), married to Prince Ivan Vasilievich Sitski (d. Kozheozero Monastery, 23 March 1608)
2 CONT
2 CONT His children by second marriage were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Mikhail (d. Nyrob, 18 March 1605), Okolnichi
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander (d. murdered in Usolie-Lud 15 March 1605), Boyar (1599), married firstly to Princess Eudoxia Ivanovna Galitsyna (d. 1 August 1597) and secondly to Juliana Semyonovna Pogozhaya (d. 1622), without issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Vasily (d. Pelym, 15 February 1601)
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivan "Kascha" (d. 1640), Boyar (1605), married to Princess Uliana Fyodorovna Litvinova-Massalaskaya (d. 1650), and had:
2 CONT
2 CONT Nikita (c. 1607 – 21 December 1654), Boyar 1645
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrey (d. 25 April 1609)
2 CONT
2 CONT Dmitry (d. 4 November 1611)
2 CONT
2 CONT Irina (d. 10 September 1615)
2 CONT
2 CONT Praskovia (d. 25 October 1622)
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivan (d. 30 July 1625)
2 CONT
2 CONT Uliana (d. 1565)
2 CONT
2 CONT Marfa (d. 1610), married to Prince Boris Kambulatovich Tcherkassky (d. 22 April 1601)
2 CONT
2 CONT Irina (d. 6 June 1636), married in 1602 to Ivan Ivanovich Godunov (d. drowned 1610), Okolnichi (1603), a second cousin of Boris Godunov, and had:
2 CONT
2 CONT Pyotr, Steward, who married and had:
2 CONT
2 CONT Grigory, Steward (1678), married to Marfa Afanasievna, without issue
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]References
2 CONT
