1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''NOTE''': Safiye Sultan was '''NOT''' related to Nurbanu Sultan. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Safiye_Sultan '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Safiye Sultan''' (Ottoman Turkish: صفیه سلطان‎; c. 1550 – 1619[a]), was the consort of Murad III and Valide Sultan of the Ottoman Empire as the mother of Mehmed III. Safiye was also one of the eminent figures during the era kno
2 CONC wn as the Sultanate of Women. She lived in the Ottoman Empire as a courtier during the reigns of seven sultans: Suleiman the Magnificent, Selim II, Murad III, Mehmed III, Ahmed I, Mustafa I, and Osman II.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Background=
2 CONT
2 CONT The identity of Safiye has often been confused with that of her Venetian mother-in-law, Nurbanu,[2] leading some to believe that Safiye was also of Venetian descent, descended from Venetian Governor of Corfu, captured by Muslim pi
2 CONC rates and presented to the Sultan's harem in 1562. However, Safiye was Albanian, from the village of Rezi in the Ducagini mountains.[3][4][5][6]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1563, at the age of 13, she was presented as a slave to the future Murad III by Mihrimah Sultan, daughter of Suleiman the Magnificent and Hürrem Sultan.[7] Given the name Safiye ("the pure one"), she became a concubine of Murad
2 CONC  (then the eldest son of Sultan Selim II). In 1566, she gave birth to Murad's son, the future Mehmed III.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Time as consort=
2 CONT
2 CONT Safiye had been Murad's only concubine prior to his accession, and he continued having a monogamous relationship with her for several years into his sultanate. His mother Nurbanu advised him to take other concubines for the good o
2 CONC f the dynasty,[8] which by 1581 had only one surviving heir, Murad and Safiye's son Mehmed. In 1583, Nurbanu accused Safiye of using witches and sorcerers to render Murad impotent and prevent him from taking new concubines. This r
2 CONC esulted in the imprisonment and torture of Safiye's servants.[9] Murad's sister Esmehan presented him with two beautiful concubines, which he accepted. Cured of his impotence, he went on to father twenty sons and twenty-seven daug
2 CONC hters.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Venetian reports state that after an initial bitterness, Safiye kept her dignity and showed no jealousy of Murad's concubines. She even procured more for him, earning the gratitude of the Sultan, who continued to value her and con
2 CONC sult her on political matters, especially after the death of Nurbanu. During Murad's latter years, Safiye returned to being his only companion.[3] However, it is unlikely that Safiye ever became Murad's wife—though the Ottoman his
2 CONC torian Mustafa Ali refers to her as such, he is contradicted by reports from the Venetian and English ambassadors.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Valide Sultan=
2 CONT
2 CONT When Murad died in 1595, Safiye arranged for her son Mehmed to succeed as sultan, and she became the valide sultan—one of the most powerful in Ottoman history. Until her son's death in 1603, Ottoman politics were determined by a p
2 CONC arty headed by herself and Gazanfer Ağa, chief of the white eunuchs and head of the enderun (the imperial inner palace).[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT Safiye eventually enjoyed an enormous stipend of 3,000 aspers a day during the latter part of her son's reign.[11] When Mehmed III went on the Eger campaign in Hungary in 1596, he gave his mother great power over the empire, leavi
2 CONC ng her in charge of the treasury. During her interim rule she persuaded her son to revoke a political appointment of the judgeship of Istanbul and to reassign to the grand vizierate to Damat Ibrahim Pasha, her son-in-law.[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT During this period, the secretary of the English ambassador reported that while in the palace, Safiye "espied a number of boats upon the river [the Bosphorus] hurrying together. The Queen Mother sent to enquire of the matter [and]
2 CONC  was told that the Vizier did justice upon certain chabies [kahpe], that is, whores. She, taking displeasure, sent word and advised [the Vizier] that her son had left him to govern the city and not to devour the women; [thus] comm
2 CONC anding him to look well to the other business and not to meddle any more with the women till his master's return."[13]
2 CONT
2 CONT The greatest crisis Safiye endured as valide sultan stemmed from her reliance on her kira, Esperanza Malchi. A kira was a non-Muslim woman (typically Jewish) who acted as an intermediary between a secluded woman of the harem and t
2 CONC he outside world, serving as a business agent and secretary. In 1600, the imperial cavalry rose in rebellion at the influence of Malchi and her son, who had amassed over 50 million aspers in wealth. Safiye was held responsible for
2 CONC  this, along with the debased currency the troops were paid with, and nearly suffered the wrath of the soldiers, who brutally killed Malchi and her son. Mehmed III was forced to say "he would counsel his mother and correct his ser
2 CONC vants." To prevent the soldiers from suspecting her influence over the Sultan, Safiye persuaded Mehmed to have his decrees written out by the Grand Vizier, instead of personally signing them.[14]
2 CONT
2 CONT Safiye was instrumental in the execution of her grandson Mahmud in 1602, having intercepted a message sent to his mother by a religious seer, who predicted that Mehmed III would die in six months and be succeeded by his son. Accor
2 CONC ding to the English ambassador, Mahmud was distressed at "how his father was altogether led by the old Sultana his Grandmother & the state went to Ruin, she respecting nothing but her own desire to get money, & often lamented ther
2 CONC eof to his mother," who was "not favored of the Queen mother."[15] The sultan, suspecting a plot and jealous of his son's popularity, had him strangled.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mehmed III was succeeded by his son Ahmed I in 1603. One of his first major decisions was to deprive his grandmother of power—she was banished to the Old Palace in January 1604.[16] When Ahmed I's brother Mustafa I became sultan i
2 CONC n 1617, his mother Halime Sultan received 3,000 aspers as valide sultan although her mother-in-law Safiye was still alive. However, Halime received only 2,000 aspers during her retirement to the Old Palace between her son's two re
2 CONC igns; during the first months of her retirement Safiye was still alive, perhaps a neighbour in the Old Palace, receiving 3,000 aspers a day.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT All succeeding sultans were descended from Safiye.[17]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Foreign relations=
2 CONT
2 CONT Safiye, like Nurbanu, advocated a generally pro-Venetian policy and regularly interceded on behalf of the Venetian ambassadors, one of whom described her to the senate as "a woman of her word, trustworthy, and I call say that in h
2 CONC er alone have I found truth in Constantinople; therefore it will always benefit Your Serenity to promote her gratitude."[18]
2 CONT
2 CONT Safiye also maintained good relations with England. She persuaded Mehmed III to let the English ambassador accompany him on campaign in Hungary.[19] One unique aspect of her career is that she corresponded personally with Queen El
2 CONC izabeth I of England, volunteering to petition the Sultan on Elizabeth's behalf. The two women also exchanged gifts. On one occasion, Safiye received a portrait of Elizabeth in exchange for "two garments of cloth of silver, one gi
2 CONC rdle of cloth of silver, [and] two handkerchiefs wrought with massy gold."[20] In a letter from 1599, Safiye responds to Elizabeth's request for good relations between the empires:
2 CONT
2 CONT >I have received your letter...God-willing, I will take action in accordance with what you have written. Be of good heart in this respect. I constantly admonish my son, the Padishah, to act according to the treaty. I do not neglec
2 CONC t to speak to him in this manner. God-willing, may you not suffer grief in this respect. May you too always be firm in friendship. God-willing, may [our friendship] never die. You have sent me a carriage and it has been delivered.
2 CONC  I accept it with pleasure. And I have sent you a robe, a sash, two large gold-embroidered bath towels, three handkerchiefs, and a ruby and pearl tiara. May you excuse [the unworthiness of the gifts].[21]
2 CONT
2 CONT Safiye had the carriage covered and used it on excursions to town, which was considered scandalous. This exchange of letters and gifts between Safiye and Elizabeth presented an interesting gender dynamic to their political relatio
2 CONC nship. In juxtaposition to the traditional means of exchanging women in order to secure diplomatic, economic, or military alliances, Elizabeth and Safiye's exchange put them in the position of power rather than the objects of exch
2 CONC ange.[22]
2 CONT
2 CONT An unusual occurrence in Safiye's relationship with England was her attraction to Paul Pindar, secretary to English ambassador and deliverer of Elizabeth's coach. According to Thomas Dallam (who presented Elizabeth's gift of an or
2 CONC gan to Mehmed III), "the sultana did take great liking to Mr. Pinder, and afterwards she sent for him to have his private company, but their meeting was crossed."[23]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Public works=
2 CONT
2 CONT Safiye is also famous for starting the construction of Yeni Mosque, the "new mosque" in Eminönü, Istanbul, in 1597. Part of Istanbul's Jewish quarter was razed to make way for the structure, whose massive building costs made Safiy
2 CONC e unpopular with the soldiery, who wanted her exiled. At one point Mehmed III temporarily sent her to the Old Palace.[24] Though she returned, she did not live to see the mosque completed. After Mehmed died, Safiye lost power and 
2 CONC was permanently exiled to the Old Palace. The mosque's construction was halted for decades. It was finally completed in 1665 by another valide sultan, Turhan Hatice, mother of Mehmed IV. The Al-Malika Safiye Mosque in Cairo is nam
2 CONC ed in Safiye's honor.
2 CONT
2 CONT =In popular culture=
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 2015 TV series ''Muhteşem Yüzyıl: Kösem'', Safiye Sultan is portrayed by Turkish actress Hülya Avşar.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Notes=
2 CONT
2 CONT * [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Safiye_Sultan#Notes '''a-24''']
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Alderson, A. D. (1956). The Structure of the Ottoman Dynasty. Oxford: Clarendon.
2 CONT * Andrea, Bernadette (2007). Women and Islam in Early Modern English Literature. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press. ISBN 978-0-521-12176-7.
2 CONT * Börekçi, Günhan (2009). "Ahmed I". In Ágoston, Gábor; Masters, Bruce. Encyclopedia of the Ottoman Empire. New York: Facts on File. ISBN 978-0-8160-6259-1.
2 CONT * Jardine, L. (2004). "Gloriana Rules the Waves: Or, the Advantage of Being Excommunicated (And a Woman)". Transactions of the Royal Historical Society 6 (14): 209. doi:10.1017/S0080440104000234.
2 CONT * Mitchell, Colin P. (2011). New Perspectives on Safavid Iran: Empire and Society. Taylor & Francis. ISBN 978-1-136-99194-3.
2 CONT * Pedani, M. P. (2000). "Safiye's Household and Venetian Diplomacy". Turcica 32: 9. doi:10.2143/TURC.32.0.460.
2 CONT * Peirce, Leslie Penn (1993). The Imperial Harem: Women and Sovereignty in the Ottoman Empire. Studies in Middle Eastern History. New York: Oxford University Press. ISBN 978-0-19-507673-8.
2 CONT * Ruggles, D. Fairchild, ed. (2011). Islamic Art and Visual Culture: An Anthology of Sources. John Wiley & Sons. ISBN 978-1-4051-5401-7.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Safiye Sultan (Ottoman Turkish: صفیه سلطان‎; c. 1550 – 1619[a]), was Ottoman Haseki Sultan as favourite consort of Murad III and Valide Sultan as mother of Mehmed III. Safiye was also one of the prominent figures during the era kn
2 CONC own as the Sultanate of Women. She lived in the Ottoman Empire during the reign of seven sultans, Suleiman the Magnificent, Selim II, Murad III, Mehmed III, Ahmed I, Mustafa I, and Osman II.
2 CONT
2 CONT Background
2 CONT
2 CONT The identity of Safiye has often been confused with that of her Venetian mother-in-law, Nurbanu,[2] leading some to believe that Safiye was also of Venetian descent, descended from Venetian Governor of Corfu, captured by Muslim pi
2 CONC rates and presented to the Sultan's harem in 1562. However, Safiye was of Albanian origin[3] and born in Albania.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1563, at the age of 13, she was presented as a slave to the future Murad III by Hümaşah Sultan, daughter of Şehzade Mehmed, son of Suleiman the Magnificent and Hürrem Sultan.[5] Given the name Safiye ("the pure one"), she becam
2 CONC e a concubine of Murad (then the eldest son of Sultan Selim II). In 26 May 1566, she gave birth to Murad's son, the future Mehmed III, same year when Suleiman the Magnificent died. Haseki Sultan
2 CONT
2 CONT Safiye had been Murad's only concubine prior to his accession, and he continued having a monogamous relationship with her for several years into his sultanate. His mother Nurbanu advised him to take other concubines for the good o
2 CONC f the dynasty,[6] which by 1581 had only one surviving heir, Murad and Safiye's son Mehmed. In 1583, Nurbanu accused Safiye of using witches and sorcerers to render Murad impotent and prevent him from taking new concubines. This r
2 CONC esulted in the imprisonment and torture of Safiye's servants.[7] Murad's sister Esmehan presented him with two beautiful concubines, which he accepted. Cured of his impotence, he went on to father twenty sons and twenty-seven daug
2 CONC hters.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Venetian reports state that after an initial bitterness, Safiye kept her dignity and showed no jealousy of Murad's concubines. She even procured more for him, earning the gratitude of the Sultan, who continued to value her and con
2 CONC sult her on political matters, especially after the death of Nurbanu. During Murad's latter years, Safiye returned to being his only companion.[3] However, it is unlikely that Safiye ever became Murad's wife—though the Ottoman his
2 CONC torian Mustafa Ali refers to her as such, he is contradicted by reports from the Venetian and English ambassadors.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT She was influential as a Haseki, as reported Giovanni Moro in 1590 that with the authority she {Safiye} enjoys as mother of the prince, she intervenes on occasion in affairs of state, although she is much respected in this, and is
2 CONC  listened to by His Majesty who considers her sensible and wise.
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT     Mehmed III (May 26, 1566 – December 22, 1603), became the next sultan succeeding his father Murad III
2 CONT     Şehzade Osman, born at Manisa. He died at Constantinople, 1581.
2 CONT     Ayşe Sultan (1570 – 15 May 1605), married:
2 CONT
2 CONT firstly 20 May 1586, Damad Ibrahim Pasha, Governor-General of Rumelia 1581-1582, of Egypt 1583, 1596-1597 and 1599-1601. secondly 5 April 1602, Damad Yamischi Hasan Pasha, Governor of Baku 1596, Grand Vizier 1601-1603. thirdly 29 
2 CONC June 1604, Damad Guzelce Mahmud Pasha, Governor of Kars 1594, 7th Vizier 1599, 3rd Vizier in 1601.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Fatma Sultan (1580 – 1620), daughter, married:
2 CONT
2 CONT firstly 6 December 1593, Damad Halil Pasha. secondly 29 June 1604, Damad Ja'afar Pasha, Governor-General of Anatolia 1588-1590, Rumelia 1590-1594 and Egypt 1598-1601, Vizier in 1594. thirdly 1610, Damad Hizir Pasha, Governor of Va
2 CONC n 1582, Karaman, and Tamashvar 1592. fourthly 1611, Damad Murad Pasha, Vizier in 1605. Valide Sultan Ottoman Sultan Mehmed III, to whom Sāfiya was a Valide sultan during 1595–1603.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Murad died in 1595, Safiye arranged for her son Mehmed to succeed as sultan, and she became the valide sultan—one of the most powerful in Ottoman history. Until her son's death in 1603, Ottoman politics were determined by a p
2 CONC arty headed by herself and Gazanfer Ağa, chief of the white eunuchs and head of the enderun (the imperial inner palace).[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Safiye eventually enjoyed an enormous stipend of 3,000 aspers a day during the latter part of her son's reign.[9] When Mehmed III went on the Eger campaign in Hungary in 1596, he gave his mother great power over the empire, leavin
2 CONC g her in charge of the treasury. During her interim rule she persuaded her son to revoke a political appointment of the judgeship of Istanbul and to reassign to the grand vizierate to Damat Ibrahim Pasha, her son-in-law.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT During this period, the secretary of the English ambassador reported that while in the palace, Safiye "espied a number of boats upon the river [the Bosphorus] hurrying together. The Queen Mother sent to enquire of the matter [and]
2 CONC  was told that the Vizier did justice upon certain chabies [kahpe], that is, whores. She, taking displeasure, sent word and advised [the Vizier] that her son had left him to govern the city and not to devour the women; [thus] comm
2 CONC anding him to look well to the other business and not to meddle any more with the women till his master's return."[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT The greatest crisis Safiye endured as valide sultan stemmed from her reliance on her kira, Esperanza Malchi. A kira was a non-Muslim woman (typically Jewish) who acted as an intermediary between a secluded woman of the harem and t
2 CONC he outside world, serving as a business agent and secretary. In 1600, the imperial cavalry rose in rebellion at the influence of Malchi and her son, who had amassed over 50 million aspers in wealth. Safiye was held responsible for
2 CONC  this, along with the debased currency the troops were paid with, and nearly suffered the wrath of the soldiers, who brutally killed Malchi and her son. Mehmed III was forced to say "he would counsel his mother and correct his ser
2 CONC vants." To prevent the soldiers from suspecting her influence over the Sultan, Safiye persuaded Mehmed to have his decrees written out by the Grand Vizier, instead of personally signing them.[12] Interior design of Yeni Mosque in 
2 CONC Eminönü, Istanbul. The construction began during Safiye's regency.
2 CONT
2 CONT Safiye was instrumental in the execution of her grandson Mahmud in 1602, having intercepted a message sent to his mother by a religious seer, who predicted that Mehmed III would die in six months and be succeeded by his son. Accor
2 CONC ding to the English ambassador, Mahmud was distressed at "how his father was altogether led by the old Sultana his Grandmother & the state went to Ruin, she respecting nothing but her own desire to get money, & often lamented ther
2 CONC eof to his mother," who was "not favored of the Queen mother."[13] The sultan, suspecting a plot and jealous of his son's popularity, had him strangled.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mehmed III was succeeded by his son Ahmed I in 1603. One of his first major decisions was to deprive his grandmother of power—she was banished to the Old Palace in January 1604.[14] When Ahmed I's brother Mustafa I became sultan i
2 CONC n 1617, his mother Halime Sultan received 3,000 aspers as valide sultan although her mother-in-law Safiye was still alive. However, Halime received only 2,000 aspers during her retirement to the Old Palace between her son's two re
2 CONC igns; during the first months of her retirement Safiye was still alive, perhaps a neighbour in the Old Palace, receiving 3,000 aspers a day[1] while the Haseki Sultan of Ahmed I, Kösem Sultan also living in the Old Palace, receive
2 CONC d 1,000 aspers day.[15]
2 CONT
2 CONT All succeeding sultans were descended from Safiye.[16] The Yeni Mosque in Eminönü, Istanbul, (Her construction was begun by Sāfiya Valida Sultânā and completed during the regency of Turhan Hatice, the mother of Mehmed IV). Foreign
2 CONC  relations
2 CONT
2 CONT Safiye, like Nurbanu, advocated a generally pro-Venetian policy and regularly interceded on behalf of the Venetian ambassadors, one of whom described her to the senate as "a woman of her word, trustworthy, and I call say that in h
2 CONC er alone have I found truth in Constantinople; therefore it will always benefit Your Serenity to promote her gratitude."[17]
2 CONT
2 CONT Safiye also maintained good relations with England. She persuaded Mehmed III to let the English ambassador accompany him on campaign in Hungary.[18] One unique aspect of her career is that she corresponded personally with Queen El
2 CONC izabeth I of England, volunteering to petition the Sultan on Elizabeth's behalf. The two women also exchanged gifts. On one occasion, Safiye received a portrait of Elizabeth in exchange for "two garments of cloth of silver, one gi
2 CONC rdle of cloth of silver, [and] two handkerchiefs wrought with massy gold."[19] In a letter from 1599, Safiye responds to Elizabeth's request for good relations between the empires:
2 CONT
2 CONT     I have received your letter...God-willing, I will take action in accordance with what you have written. Be of good heart in this respect. I constantly admonish my son, the Padishah, to act according to the treaty. I do not neg
2 CONC lect to speak to him in this manner. God-willing, may you not suffer grief in this respect. May you too always be firm in friendship. God-willing, may [our friendship] never die. You have sent me a carriage and it has been deliver
2 CONC ed. I accept it with pleasure. And I have sent you a robe, a sash, two large gold-embroidered bath towels, three handkerchiefs, and a ruby and pearl tiara. May you excuse [the unworthiness of the gifts].[20]
2 CONT
2 CONT Al-Malika Safiyya Mosque, Cairo (Exterior view)
2 CONT
2 CONT Safiye had the carriage covered and used it on excursions to town, which was considered scandalous. This exchange of letters and gifts between Safiye and Elizabeth presented an interesting gender dynamic to their political relatio
2 CONC nship. In juxtaposition to the traditional means of exchanging women in order to secure diplomatic, economic, or military alliances, Elizabeth and Safiye's exchange put them in the position of power rather than the objects of exch
2 CONC ange.[21]
2 CONT
2 CONT An unusual occurrence in Safiye's relationship with England was her attraction to Paul Pindar, secretary to English ambassador and deliverer of Elizabeth's coach. According to Thomas Dallam (who presented Elizabeth's gift of an or
2 CONC gan to Mehmed III), "the sultana did take great liking to Mr. Pinder, and afterwards she sent for him to have his private company, but their meeting was crossed."[22] Public works
2 CONT
2 CONT Safiye is also famous for starting the construction of Yeni Mosque, the "new mosque" in Eminönü, Istanbul, in 1597. Part of Istanbul's Jewish quarter was razed to make way for the structure, whose massive building costs made Safiy
2 CONC e unpopular with the soldiery, who wanted her exiled. At one point Mehmed III temporarily sent her to the Old Palace.[23] Though she returned, she did not live to see the mosque completed. After Mehmed died, Safiye lost power and 
2 CONC was permanently exiled to the Old Palace. The mosque's construction was halted for decades. It was finally completed in 1665 by another valide sultan, Turhan Hatice, mother of Mehmed IV. Masjid al-Malika Safiyya Masjid al-Malika S
2 CONC afiyya, Egypt.[24] (Interior view)
2 CONT
2 CONT The Al-Malika Safiye Mosque in Cairo is named in Safiye's honor. The mosque of al-Malika Safiyya derives its name more by appropriation than by real patronage. It was started by 'Uthman Agha, who held the post of the Agha Dar al-S
2 CONC a'ada, or black eunuch in charge of the harem, as well as the Egyptian waqf estates of the holy places in the Hijaz. 'Uthman Agha was the agent and slave of the noble Venetian beauty Safiya, of the Baffo family, who had been captu
2 CONC red by corsairs and presented to the imperial harem, where she became chief consort of Sultan Murad III (1574-95) and virtual regent for her son, Sultan Mehmed III (1595-1603). 'Uthman died before the mosque was completed, and it 
2 CONC went to Safiya as part of his estate. She endowed the mosque with a deed that provided for thirty-nine custodians including a general supervisor, a preacher, the khatib (orator), two imams, timekeeper, an incense burner, a repairm
2 CONC an, and a gardener.[25] Death The inscription at her husband Murad III's türbe, proving that Safiya Sultan was buried there.
2 CONT
2 CONT Leslie Peirce points out in her book that Safiye was still alive during the first months of her daughter-in-law's retirement in the Old Palace between Mustafa I's two reigns, which means that she was alive at least until 1619 and 
2 CONC died during the reign of her great-grandson Osman II. Safiye was buried in Murad III's tomb. In popular culture
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 2015 TV series Muhteşem Yüzyıl: Kösem, Safiye Sultan is portrayed by Turkish actress Hülya Avşar. See also
2 CONT
