1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''NOTE''': Yahya was a pretender to the Ottoman throne and he was not the biological son of Murad III or Helen of Trebizond, who may be a fictitious character. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sultan_Yahya '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia:
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sultan Yahya''' (1585–1649) was the alleged son of Ottoman Sultan Murad III.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Biography=
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Background==
2 CONT When his father, Murad, became Sultan, he followed the Ottoman custom of executing all of his brothers (potential rival claimants to the Ottoman throne). Yahya's mother, Safiye, was concerned that this could also eventually happen
2 CONC  to him after the death of his father, so he was smuggled out of the empire, first to Greece, and then to present-day Bulgaria. He was then supposedly baptized at an Orthodox Christian monastery, where he lived for the next eight 
2 CONC years of his life.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Battle for Ottoman throne==
2 CONT Eventually, Yahya's two older brothers died, but in 1603, since Yahya had escaped the country to avoid fratricide, his nephew Ahmed I became the Ottoman sultan. Yahya believed that as the next oldest son of Murad III, he was next 
2 CONC in line to be Ottoman Sultan and felt cheated out of his rightful destiny. He would dedicate the rest of his life to gaining the Ottoman throne. However, the standard Ottoman practice at the time for determining the succession was
2 CONC  not birth order of sons; instead the Ottoman laws of succession to the throne stated that after the death of their father, the Ottoman princes would fight among themselves until one emerged triumphant.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1603 on, Yahya made frequent trips to northern and western Europe to gain support for his claim to the throne (visiting Florence, Madrid, Rome, Kraków, Antwerp, Prague, and other cities). At one point he managed to win the su
2 CONC pport of the Tatar Khan Shahin, and of the Cossacks as well.[2] Between 1614 and 1617, he schemed with Serbian Orthodox Christian bishops in the Sanjak of Prizren and Western Roman Catholic bishops and leaders as part of his strat
2 CONC egy to gain the Ottoman throne. A few years later, with the assistance of Russian and Ukrainian cossacks, he led a fleet of 130 ships and unsuccessfully attacked Constantinople. He died in 1649 on the Montenegrin coast, where he w
2 CONC as involved in a rebellion organized by the Roman Catholic bishops of Skodra-and-Bar.
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT # Kosovo, A Short History (1998), Noel Malcolm -- Harper Perennial - pp. 121 - 122 ISBN 978-0-06-097775-7
2 CONT # Faroqhi, Suraiya (December 20, 2005). The Ottoman Empire and the World around it. I. B. Tauris. ISBN 978-0-857-73023-7.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sources=
2 CONT
2 CONT Ostapchuk, Victor (1989). The Ottoman Black Sea Frontier and the Relations of the Porte with the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth and Muscovy: 1622-1628. Harvard University. p. 92.
2 CONT Королёв В. Н. (2007). Босфорская война. М.: Вече. ISBN 978-5-9533-2099-3.(in Russian) Усенко О. Г. (2006). "Ототоманус, или сын турецкого султана" (журнал) (6) (Родина ed.): 45—52. ISSN 0235-7089.(in Russian) Faroqhi S. (2005). Th
2 CONC e Ottoman Empire and the World around it. I. B. Tauris. ISBN 978-0-857-73023-7. Benzoni G. (2004). "JACHIA" (Dizionario Biografico degli Italiani). 61. Romа: Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana. ed. Alberto M. Ghisalberti.Templat
2 CONC e:Ref-it Catualdi V. (1889). Sultan Jahja dell'imperial casa ottomana od altrimenti Alessandro conte di Montenegro ed i suoi discendenti in Italia: Nuovi contributi alla storia della questione orientale. Chiopris.Template:Ref-it C
2 CONC atualdi V. (2013). Sultan Jahja dell'imperial casa ottomana od altrimenti Alessandro conte di Montenegro ed i suoi discendenti in Italia: Nuovi contributi alla storia della questione orientale. BiblioLife. ISBN 1295371448.Template
2 CONC :Ref-it Giammanco A.D. (2015). "(Self) Fashioning of an ottoman cristian prince: Jachia ibn Mehmed in confessional diplomacy of the early seventeenth century" (MA thesis in comparative history) (Central Europian University ed.). L
2 CONC evinsk. A. (1890). "Un adventurier turk au XVII ciecle (Sultan Yahya autrement dit le comte Alexandre de Montenegro)" (Revue bleue: politique et littéraire). XLV(1): 393—400 , 435—442.(in French) Malcolm N. (2002). Kosovo: A Short
2 CONC  History. Pan. ISBN 0330412248. Peirce, Leslie P. (1993). The Imperial Harem: Women and Sovereignty in the Ottoman Empire. Oxford: Oxford University Press. ISBN 0195086775. Setton K.M. (1991). Venice, Austria, and the Turks in the
2 CONC  Seventeenth Century. 192. American Philosophical Society. ISBN 0871691922.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Earlier about me page:
2 CONT
2 CONT Yahya (1585–1649) was the third son of Ottoman Sultan Murad III. His mother Elena was a princess from the Byzantine Komnenós dynasty of Trebizond, a surviving branch of the Byzantine imperial family of the same name from Constanti
2 CONC nople. When his brother, Mehmet, became Sultan, he followed the Ottoman custom of executing all of his brothers (potential rival claimants to the Ottoman throne). Yahya's mother was concerned that this could also eventually happen
2 CONC  to him after the death of his father, so he was smuggled out of the empire, first to Greece, and then to present-day Bulgaria. He was then supposedly baptized at an Orthodox Christian monastery, where he lived for the next eight 
2 CONC years of his life.[1] The father of "Sultan" Yahya, Ottoman Sultan Murad III in his royal robes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Battle for Ottoman throne
2 CONT
2 CONT Eventually, Yahya's two older brothers died, but in 1603, since Yahya had escaped the country to avoid fratricide, his nephew Ahmed I (the fourth-born) became Ottoman sultan. Yahya believed that as the next oldest son of Murat III
2 CONC , he was next in line to be Ottoman Sultan and felt cheated out of his rightful destiny. He would dedicate the rest of his life to gaining the Ottoman throne. However, the standard Ottoman practice at the time for determining the 
2 CONC succession was not birth order of sons; instead the Ottoman laws of succession to the throne stated that after the death of their father, the Ottoman princes would fight among themselves until one emerged triumphant.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1603 on, Yahya made frequent trips to northern and western Europe to gain support for his claim to the throne (visiting Florence, Madrid, Rome, Kraków, Antwerp, Prague, and other cities). Between 1614 and 1617, he schemed wit
2 CONC h Serbian Orthodox Christian bishops in Kosovo and Western Roman Catholic bishops and leaders as part of his strategy to gain the Ottoman throne. A few years later, with the assistance of Russian and Ukrainian cossacks, he led a f
2 CONC leet of 130 ships and unsuccessfully attacked Istanbul. He died in 1649 on the Montenegrin coast, where he was involved in a rebellion organized by the Roman Catholic bishops of Skodra-and-Bar.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and Descendants
2 CONT
2 CONT He married in 1629 the Albanian Princess Anna Katharina a descendant of Skanderbeg and had three Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Prince Maurizio
2 CONT     Prince Alessandro
2 CONT     Princess Elena (named after his Mother)
2 CONT
