1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * * http://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%A4%D0%B8%D0%BB%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B5%D1%82_%28%D0%9F%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%B0%D1%80%D1%85_%D0%9C%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B2%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9%29 
2 CONT * Fyodor Nikitich Romanov (Russian: Фёдор Никитич Романов) (1553 — October 1, 1633) was a Russian boyar who after temporary disgrace rose to become patriarch of Moscow as Filaret (Russian: Филарет), and became de-facto ruler of Ru
2 CONC ssia during the reign of his son, Mikhail Feodorovich.
2 CONT ===Life===
2 CONT * Fyodor was born in Moscow the second son of the prominent boyar Nikita Romanovich and was the first to bear the Romanov surname. During the reign of his first cousin Feodor I (1584-1598), young Feodor Romanov distinguished himse
2 CONC lf both as a soldier and a diplomatist, fighting against the forces of John III of Sweden in 1590, and conducting negotiations with the ambassadors of Rudolf II, Holy Roman Emperor from 1593 to 1594. He was made a Boyar in 1583.
2 CONT * On the death of the childless tsar, he was the popular candidate for the vacant throne; but he acquiesced in the election of Boris Godunov, and shared the disgrace of his too-powerful family three years later, when Boris compell
2 CONC ed both him and his wife, Xenia Shestova, to take monastic vows under the names of Philaret and Martha respectively.
2 CONT * Philaret was kept in the strictest confinement in the Antoniev Monastery of the Russian North, where he was exposed to every conceivable indignity; but when the False Dmitriy I overthrew the Godunovs, he released Philaret and ma
2 CONC de him metropolitan of Rostov (1605).
2 CONT * Philaret Romanov, a 19th-century painting.In 1609 Philaret fell into the hands of False Dmitriy II, who named him Patriarch of all Russia, though his jurisdiction only extended over the very limited area which acknowledged the i
2 CONC mpostor. From 1610 to 1618 he was a prisoner in the hands of the Polish king, Sigismund III Vasa, whom he refused to acknowledge as tsar of Muscovy on being sent on an embassy to the Polish camp in 1610. He was released on the con
2 CONC clusion of the truce of Deulino (February 13, 1619), and on 2 June of the same year was canonically enthroned Patriarch of Moscow and all of Russia.
2 CONT * Henceforth, till his death, the established government of Muscovy was a diarchy. From 1619 to 1633 there were two actual sovereigns, Tsar Michael and his father, the most holy Patriarch Philaret. Theoretically they were co-regen
2 CONC ts, but Philaret frequently transacted affairs of state without consulting the tsar. He replenished the treasury by a more equable and rational system of assessing and collecting the taxes. His most important domestic measure was 
2 CONC the chaining of the peasantry to the soil, a measure directed against the ever increasing migration of the down-trodden serfs to the steppes, where they became freebooters instead of tax-payers. The taxation of the tsar's military
2 CONC  tenants was a first step towards the proportional taxation of the hitherto privileged classes.
2 CONT * Philaret's zeal for the purity of orthodoxy sometimes led him into excesses but he encouraged the publication of theological works, formed the nucleus of the subsequently famous Patriarchal Library, and commanded that every arch
2 CONC bishop should establish a seminary for the clergy, himself setting the example. Another great service rendered by Philaret to his country was the reorganization of the Muscovite army with the help of foreign officers. His death in
2 CONC  October 1633 put an end to the Russo-Polish War (1632-33), withdrawing the strongest prop from a tsar feeble enough even when supported by all the weight of his authority.
2 CONT * '''By his marriage he had:'''
2 CONT ** Boris (d. 20 November/30 November 1592)
2 CONT ** Nikita (d. 29 November/9 December 1593)
2 CONT ** Lev (d. 21 September/1 October 1597)
2 CONT ** Tatiana (d. 4 November/14 November 1611), married to Prince Ivan Mikhailovich Katyrev-Rostovski (d. 1640)
2 CONT ** Mikhail Feodorovich Romanov
2 CONT ** Ivan (d. 7 June/17 June 1599)
2 CONT * '''Patriarch Filaret (Feodor Romanov'''
2 CONT * Fyodor Nikitich Romanov (1553 — October 1, 1633) was a Russian boyar who after temporary disgrace rose to become patriarch of Moscow as Filaret, and became de-facto ruler of Russia during the reign of his son, Mikhail Feodorovic
2 CONC h.
2 CONT * Fyodor was born in Moscow the second son of the prominent boyar Nikita Romanovich and was the first to bear the Romanov surname. During the reign of his first cousin Feodor I (1584-1598), young Feodor Romanov distinguished himse
2 CONC lf both as a soldier and a diplomatist, fighting against the forces of John III of Sweden in 1590, and conducting negotiations with the ambassadors of Rudolf II, Holy Roman Emperor from 1593 to 1594. He was made a Boyar in 1583.
2 CONT * On the death of the childless tsar, he was the popular candidate for the vacant throne; but he acquiesced in the election of Boris Godunov, and shared the disgrace of his too-powerful family three years later, when Boris compell
2 CONC ed both him and his wife, Xenia Shestova, to take monastic vows under the names of Philaret and Martha respectively.
2 CONT * Philaret was kept in the strictest confinement in the Antoniev Monastery of the Russian North, where he was exposed to every conceivable indignity; but when the False Dmitriy I overthrew the Godunovs, he released Philaret and ma
2 CONC de him metropolitan of Rostov (1605).
2 CONT * In 1609 Philaret fell into the hands of False Dmitriy II, who named him Patriarch of all Russia, though his jurisdiction only extended over the very limited area which acknowledged the impostor. From 1610 to 1618 he was a prison
2 CONC er in the hands of the Polish king, Sigismund III Vasa, whom he refused to acknowledge as tsar of Muscovy on being sent on an embassy to the Polish camp in 1610. He was released on the conclusion of the truce of Deulino (February 
2 CONC 13, 1619), and on 2 June of the same year was canonically enthroned Patriarch of Moscow and all of Russia.
2 CONT * Henceforth, till his death, the established government of Muscovy was a diarchy. From 1619 to 1633 there were two actual sovereigns, Tsar Michael and his father, the most holy Patriarch Philaret. Theoretically they were co-regen
2 CONC ts, but Philaret frequently transacted affairs of state without consulting the tsar. He replenished the treasury by a more equable and rational system of assessing and collecting the taxes. His most important domestic measure was 
2 CONC the chaining of the peasantry to the soil, a measure directed against the ever increasing migration of the down-trodden serfs to the steppes, where they became freebooters instead of tax-payers. The taxation of the tsar's military
2 CONC  tenants was a first step towards the proportional taxation of the hitherto privileged classes.
2 CONT * Philaret's zeal for the purity of orthodoxy sometimes led him into excesses but he encouraged the publication of theological works, formed the nucleus of the subsequently famous Patriarchal Library, and commanded that every arch
2 CONC bishop should establish a seminary for the clergy, himself setting the example. Another great service rendered by Philaret to his country was the reorganization of the Muscovite army with the help of foreign officers. His death in
2 CONC  October 1633 put an end to the Russo-Polish War (1632-33), withdrawing the strongest prop from a tsar feeble enough even when supported by all the weight of his authority.
2 CONT
2 CONT By his marriage he had:
2 CONT
2 CONT Boris (d. 20 November/30 November 1592)
2 CONT
2 CONT Nikita (d. 29 November/9 December 1593)
2 CONT
2 CONT Lev (d. 21 September/1 October 1597)
2 CONT
2 CONT Tatiana (d. 4 November/14 November 1611), married to Prince Ivan Mikhailovich Katyrev-Rostovski (d. 1640)
2 CONT
2 CONT Mikhail Feodorovich Romanov
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivan (d. 7 June/17 June 1599)
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Patriarch Filaret (Feodor Romanov)
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Fyodor Nikitich Romanov (Russian: Фёдор Никитич Романов) (1553 — 1 October 1633) was a Russian boyar who after temporary disgrace rose to become patriarch of Moscow as Filaret (Russian: Филарет), and became de-facto ruler of Russi
2 CONC a during the reign of his son, Mikhail Feodorovich.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Fyodor was born in Moscow the second son of the prominent boyar Nikita Romanovich and was the first to bear the Romanov surname. During the reign of his first cousin Feodor I (1584-1598), young Feodor Romanov distinguished himself
2 CONC  both as a soldier and a diplomatist, fighting against the forces of John III of Sweden in 1590, and conducting negotiations with the ambassadors of Rudolf II, Holy Roman Emperor from 1593 to 1594. He was made a Boyar in 1583.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the death of the childless tsar, he was the popular candidate for the vacant throne; but he acquiesced in the election of Boris Godunov, and shared the disgrace of his too-powerful family three years later, when Boris compelled
2 CONC  both him and his wife, Xenia Shestova, to take monastic vows under the names of Filaret and Martha respectively.
2 CONT
2 CONT Filaret was kept in the strictest confinement in the Antoniev Monastery of the Russian North, where he was exposed to every conceivable indignity; but when the False Dmitriy I overthrew the Godunovs, he released Filaret and made h
2 CONC im metropolitan of Rostov (1605).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1609 Filaret fell into the hands of False Dmitriy II, who named him Patriarch of all Russia, though his jurisdiction only extended over the very limited area which acknowledged the impostor. From 1610 to 1618 he was a prisoner 
2 CONC in the hands of the Polish king, Sigismund III Vasa, whom he refused to acknowledge as tsar of Muscovy on being sent on an embassy to the Polish camp in 1610. He was released on the conclusion of the truce of Deulino (13 February 
2 CONC 1619), and on 2 June of the same year was canonically enthroned Patriarch of Moscow and all of Russia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thenceforth, until his death, the established government of Muscovy was a diarchy. From 1619 to 1633 there were two actual sovereigns, Tsar Michael and his father, the most holy Patriarch Filaret. Theoretically they were co-regent
2 CONC s, but Filaret frequently transacted affairs of state without consulting the tsar. He replenished the treasury by a more equable and rational system of assessing and collecting the taxes. His most important domestic measure was th
2 CONC e chaining of the peasantry to the soil, a measure directed against the ever increasing migration of the down-trodden serfs to the steppes, where they became freebooters instead of taxpayers. The taxation of the tsar's military te
2 CONC nants was a first step towards the proportional taxation of the hitherto privileged classes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Filaret's zeal for the purity of orthodoxy sometimes led him into excesses but he encouraged the publication of theological works, formed the nucleus of the subsequently famous Patriarchal Library, and commanded that every archbis
2 CONC hop should establish a seminary for the clergy, himself setting the example. Another great service rendered by Filaret to his country was the reorganization of the Muscovite army with the help of foreign officers. His death in Oct
2 CONC ober 1633 put an end to the Russo-Polish War (1632-33), withdrawing the strongest prop from a tsar feeble enough even when supported by all the weight of his authority.
2 CONT
2 CONT By his marriage he had: [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Boris (d. 20 November/30 November 1592)
2 CONT
2 CONT Nikita (d. 29 November/9 December 1593)
2 CONT
2 CONT Lev (d. 21 September/1 October 1597)
2 CONT
2 CONT Tatiana (d. 4 November/14 November 1611), married to Prince Ivan Mikhailovich Katyrev-Rostovski (d. 1640)
2 CONT
2 CONT Mikhail Feodorovich Romanov
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivan (d. 7 June/17 June 1599)
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ The first date is of Russian Orthodox Calendar.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]References
2 CONT
2 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica, Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Feodor Nikitich Romanov (Russian: Фео́дор Ники́тич Рома́нов; 1553 – 1 October 1633) was a Russian boyar who after temporary disgrace rose to become patriarch of Moscow as Filaret (Russian: Филаре́т), and became de facto ruler of R
2 CONC ussia during the reign of his son, Mikhail Feodorovich.
2 CONT
2 CONT The second son of a prominent boyar Nikita Romanovich Feodor was born in Moscow and was the first to bear the Romanov surname. During the reign of his first cousin Feodor I (1584–1598), young Feodor Romanov distinguished himself b
2 CONC oth as a soldier and a diplomat, fighting against the forces of John III of Sweden in 1590, and conducting negotiations with the ambassadors of Rudolf II, Holy Roman Emperor from 1593 to 1594. He was made a Boyar in 1583.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the death of the childless tsar, he was the popular candidate for the vacant throne; but he acquiesced in the election of Boris Godunov, and shared the disgrace of his too-powerful family three years later, when Boris compelled
2 CONC  both him and his wife, Xenia Shestova, to take monastic vows under the names of Filaret and Martha respectively.
2 CONT
2 CONT Filaret was kept in the strictest confinement in the Antoniev Monastery of the Russian North, where he was exposed to every conceivable indignity; but when the False Dmitriy I overthrew the Godunovs, he released Filaret and made h
2 CONC im metropolitan of Rostov (1605).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1609 Filaret fell into the hands of False Dmitriy II, who named him Patriarch of all Russia, though his jurisdiction only extended over the very limited area which acknowledged the impostor. From 1610 to 1618 he was a prisoner 
2 CONC in the hands of the Polish king, Sigismund III Vasa, whom he refused to acknowledge as tsar of Muscovy on being sent on an embassy to the Polish camp in 1610. He was released on the conclusion of the truce of Deulino (13 February 
2 CONC 1619), and on 2 June of the same year was canonically enthroned Patriarch of Moscow and all of Russia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thenceforth, until his death, the established government of Muscovy was a diarchy. From 1619 to 1633 there were two actual sovereigns, Tsar Michael and his father, the most holy Patriarch Filaret. Theoretically they were co-regent
2 CONC s, but Filaret frequently transacted affairs of state without consulting the tsar. He replenished the treasury by a more equable and rational system of assessing and collecting the taxes. His most important domestic measure was th
2 CONC e chaining of the peasantry to the soil, a measure directed against the ever-increasing migration of the down-trodden serfs to the steppes, where they became freebooters instead of taxpayers. The taxation of the tsar's military te
2 CONC nants was a first step towards the proportional taxation of the hitherto privileged classes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Filaret's zeal for the purity of orthodoxy sometimes led him into excesses but he encouraged the publication of theological works, formed the nucleus of the subsequently famous Patriarchal Library, and commanded that every archbis
2 CONC hop should establish a seminary for the clergy, himself setting the example. Another great service rendered by Filaret to his country was the reorganization of the Muscovite army with the help of foreign officers. His death in Oct
2 CONC ober 1633 put an end to the Russo-Polish War (1632–33), withdrawing the strongest prop from a tsar feeble enough even when supported by all the weight of his authority.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT May 20, 1625 Filaret on the rights of the sovereign issued a royal decree by which the patriarch received the right to judge and be in charge of the spiritual and the patriarchal peasant population in the area of any business, exc
2 CONC ept theft and robbery. Thus, formed as a state within a state. His policy streamlined the management, but also created much more complex structure: Judgment department - was in charge of judicial affairs. Church department - was i
2 CONC n charge of the affairs of the church decorum. Treasury department - was responsible for collecting taxes from the clergy. Palace department- managed the patriarchal estates.
2 CONT
2 CONT Every order sat patriarchal nobleman with the clerks and clerks. Patriarch personally signed the papers. Filaret also conducted a full inventory of the church and monastery property and revision of charters issued monasteries dele
2 CONC gated in their use of land.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1620 he created a new, Diocese of Tobolsk. In 1625, the patriarch got a gift from the Persian king was transferred part of the Lord's robe, which was placed in the ark in the Assumption Cathedral. This orthodox relic is still k
2 CONC ept in the Cathedral of Christ the Savior.
2 CONT
2 CONT By his marriage he had:[2] Boris (d. 20 November/30 November 1592) Nikita (d. 29 November/9 December 1593) Lev (d. 21 September/1 October 1597) Tatiana (d. 4 November/14 November 1611), married to Prince Ivan Mikhailovich Katyrev-
2 CONC Rostovski (d. 1640) Mikhail Feodorovich Romanov Ivan (d. 7 June/17 June 1599)
