2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sophia_Palaiologina
2 CONT
2 CONT Zoe Palaiologina (Greek Ζωή Παλαιολόγου, Russian Софья Фоминична Палеолог, c. 1455 – April 7, 1503), Grand Duchess of Moscow, was a niece of the last Byzantine emperor Constantine XI and second wife of Ivan III of Russia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her father was Thomas Palaeologus, the Despot of Morea. Together with her brothers, she was taken to Rome after conquest of Morea by Mehmed II of the Ottoman Empire in 1460. In Rome, her Greek name Zoe was changed to Sophia. In 14
2 CONC 69, Pope Paul II offered to marry her to the Russian monarch in order to unite the Orthodox and Catholic churches. The widowed Russian prince married Sophia at the Dormition Cathedral on November 12, 1472. The cardinal Johannes Be
2 CONC ssarion, sent by the Pope to Moscow, however, did not succeed in his mission.
2 CONT
2 CONT Over the years, Sophia started to wield great influence on her husband. It is thought that she was the first to introduce the Kremlin to grand Byzantine ceremonies and meticulous etiquette. The idea of Moscow as the Third Rome evi
2 CONC dently pleased her. Shortly before her death she persuaded her husband to pass the throne to her son Vasili, rather than to Ivan's grandson Dmitry, as had been planned earlier. Apart from Vasili III, only her fifth son, Andrey of 
2 CONC Staritsa, left issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT Retrieved from "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sophia_Paleologue"
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Sophia Palaiologina
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Zoe Palaiologina (Greek: Ζωή Παλαιολογίνα, Russian: Софья Фоминична Палеолог, c. 1455 – April 7, 1503), Grand Duchess of Moscow, was a niece of the last Byzantine emperor Constantine XI and second wife of Ivan III of Russia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her father was Thomas Palaeologus, the Despot of Morea. Together with her brothers, she was taken to Rome after the conquest of Morea by Mehmed II of the Ottoman Empire in 1460. In Rome, her Greek name Zoe was changed to Sophia. I
2 CONC n 1469, Pope Paul II offered to marry her to the Russian monarch in order to unite the Orthodox and Catholic churches. The widowed Russian prince married Sophia at the Dormition Cathedral on 12 November 1472. The cardinal Johannes
2 CONC  Bessarion, sent by the Pope to Moscow, however, did not succeed in his mission.
2 CONT
2 CONT Over the years, Sophia started to have great influence in her husband's decision making. It is thought that she was the first to introduce the Kremlin to grand Byzantine ceremonies and meticulous court etiquette], the idea of Mosc
2 CONC ow as a Third Rome evidently pleased her. Shortly before her death she persuaded her husband to pass the throne to her son Vasili, rather than to Ivan's grandson Dmitry, as had been planned earlier. Apart from Vasili III, only her
2 CONC  fifth son, Andrey of Staritsa, left issu
2 CONT
2 CONT nine 9 children.
2 CONT --------------------
