1 OCCU селджукски султан (1246–1260)
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Note:''' This profile has been confused with his brother, Kayqubad II. Kayqubad II and Kaykaus II are BROTHERS, not the same person, so please do not merge their profiles together.
2 CONT
2 CONT * from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kaykaus_II '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Kaykaus II''' or '''Kayka'us II''' (Persian: عز الدين كيكاوس بن كيخسرو‎‎, '''ʿIzz ad-Dīn Kaykāwus bin Kaykhusraw''') was the eldest of three sons of Kaykhusraw II. He was a youth at the time of his father’s death in 1246 and co
2 CONC uld do little to prevent the Mongol conquest of Anatolia. For most of his tenure as the Seljuq Sultan of Rûm, he shared the throne with one or both of his brothers, Kilij Arslan IV and Kayqubad II. His mother was the daughter of a
2 CONC  Greek priest, and it was the Greeks of Nicaea from whom he consistently sought aid throughout his life.[citation needed] Baiju threatened him and warned him of giving his Mongol cavalries new pastures in Anatolia and too late to 
2 CONC pay tax. The Mongols defeated Kaykaus who then fled to the Byzantine Balkan in 1256. The Byzantine court detained him, though, they welcomed him as usual. So Kaykaus's brother Kayqubad appealed to Berke Khan of the Golden Horde. N
2 CONC ogai invaded the Empire in 1265 and released him[citation needed] and his men after Michael detained an envoy from Cairo to Berke. Berke gave Kaykaus appanage in Crimea and had him married the Mongol woman. He died an exile in 127
2 CONC 9 or 1280 in the Crimea.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Legacy=
2 CONT
2 CONT Though deposed and exiled, Kaykaus remained popular among the Turkmen of Anatolia and a threat to the stability of the fragile Seljuq-Mongol relationship. The vizier Fakhr al-Din Ali was imprisoned for a time in 1271 for correspon
2 CONC ding with him. It was from Kaykaus that Karamanoğlu Mehmed Bey in 1276 sought help in his uprising against the Mongols. Since Kaykaus was in no position to help, Mehmed Bey thought it best to have a representative of Kaykaus’ line
2 CONC  on his side, even if only an imposter, and named Jimri as head of the revolt. Kaykaus later dispatched several of his sons from the Crimea as pretenders, one of which, Masud II, was ultimately successful in winning the Seljuq thr
2 CONC one in 1280.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Ottoman period the rebel Sheikh Bedreddin, who drew support largely from Turkmen migrants to the Balkans, claimed descent from Kaykaus II.
2 CONT
2 CONT =See also=
2 CONT
