1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jahan_Shah
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Monarch of the Iran's Kara Koyunlu dynasty, who designated Tabriz as capital of his Empire, which spanned major parts of modern Iran, Azerbaijan, and Turkey. Jahanshah was buried on the southern part of the 'Blue Mosque' of Tabriz
2 CONC  (along with other members of his imediate family).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Muzaffar al-Din Jahan Shah ibn Yusuf'''  or or Abu al-Muzaffar Jahan Shah (died 1467)‎ was the leader of the Kara Koyunlu Turkmen tribal federation in Azerbaijan and Arran who reigned c.1438-1467. During his reign he managed to
2 CONC  expand the Kara Koyunlu’s territory to its largest extent, including Western Anatolia, most of present day Iraq, central Iran, and even eventually Kerman. He also subjugated neighbouring states. He was one of the greatest rulers 
2 CONC of the Kara Koyunlu. He was also allegedly fond of drinking and entertainment. During his reign Jahan Shah had the Gökmedrese and Muzafferiye theological schools constructed in his capital city Tabriz. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jahan Shah comes to power 
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1420 Jahan Shah married the daughter of Alexios IV of Trebizond and Theodora Kantakouzene, part of the agreement being that Alexius would continue paying to the Kara Koyunlu the tribute that Trebizond had formerly paid to T
2 CONC imur. During the reign of his brother Qare Iskandar (1420-36), as a potential rival to the throne, Jahan Shah’s life was not safe and he took refuge with his other brother Ispend who was ruling Baghdad. In 1436 he obtained the hel
2 CONC p of the Timurid ruler Shah Rukh to defeat Qara Iskander and seize the throne for himself. Having been helped to power by Shah Rukh he ruled at first as a vassal of the Timurids. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the year 1462, Abd al-Razzaq described Jahan-shah's rule in the following terms: "Owing to the benevolent administration (husn-i 'inayat va lutf-i atifat) of Mirza Jahan-shah, Azerbaijan was a highly thriving state. That well-m
2 CONC eaning sovereign was anxious to practice justice, to secure prosperity of the country, and to treat his subjects honourably. The capital, Tabriz, by its numerous population and the prevalence of tranquility, emulated Egypt (misr-i
2 CONC  jami). The rumours of the good beaviour of that felicitous king spread throughout the world. The inhabitants of his God-protected kingdom, indifferent to the arrows of events, enjoyed peace" 
2 CONT
2 CONT Campaigns against Georgia 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1440, King Alexander I of Georgia refused to pay tribute to Jahan Shah. In March Jahan Shah responded by invading Georgia with 20,000 troops, destroyed the city of Samshvilde and sacked Tbilisi before returning to Tabriz. He al
2 CONC so mounted a second military expedition against Georgia in 1444. His forces met those of Alexander’s successor, King Vakhtang IV at Akhatsikhe, but the fighting was inconclusive and Jahan Shah returned to Tabriz once more. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Conquest of Baghdad 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jahan Shah’s brother Ispend, who had ruled over Baghdad and its environs for twelve years, died in 1445 and he bequeathed the government of the state to his nephew Elvend since his son Fulad was too young at the time. However most
2 CONC  of the emirs preferred Fulad, as did Jahan Shah. He decided to organise a military expedition against Baghdad with the backing of some of the emirs, who had sought refuge with him. After a siege of seven months, Baghdad was captu
2 CONC red in June 1446 
2 CONT
2 CONT Independence from the Timurid Empire 
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon the death of the Timurid ruler Shah Rukh in 1447, Jahan Shah became an independent ruler of the Kara Koyunlu, and started to use the titles of sultan and khan. At the same time, the Timurid Empire took advantage of the strugg
2 CONC les among the Turkmen princes and captured the cities of Sultaniya and Qazvin. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From around 1447 Jahan Shah was involved in a struggle against the Ak Koyanlu who had always been sworn enemies of the Kara Koyunlu. Jahan Shah wanted to defeat the Ak Koyunlu ruler Uzun Hasan and make him his vassal. This struggl
2 CONC e lasted until 1451, causing both sides terrible casualties and losses. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jahan Shah Grave at Southern Part of Goy Macid 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jahan Shah set out from Tabriz with a great army on 16 May 1466, and came to the basin of Lake Van. While there, he was furious to learn that Uzun Hasan was raiding his lands with 12,000 cavalry. Meanwhile Uzun Hasan, suspecting t
2 CONC hat Jahan Shah was planning to attack him, had carefully guarded the mountain passes. Envoys went back and forth between them, but because of Jahan Shah’s heavy demands, an agreement could not be reached. Having advanced as far as
2 CONC  Mus, Jahan Shah had to postpone his attack because of the onset of winter. As his troops began to complain, he decided to withdraw to a winter residence. Uzun Hasan caught his army by surprise and totally defeated them in a sudde
2 CONC n attack on 11 November 1467. Jahan Shah was killed while trying to flee, and with his death the great era of Kara Koyunlu history came to an end. He was succeeded by his incompetent son Hasan Ali. Jahan Shah had been buried in so
2 CONC uthern part of Goy Macid Tabriz
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married several times. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Known marriages include:
2 CONT * a daughter of Alexios IV of Trebizond[7][32] and 
2 CONT *Jan Begüm (daughter Tajuddin Rajab bin Afridun):
2 CONT
2 CONT Sons
2 CONT
2 CONT * Pirbudag – governor of Isfahan and Fars, then Baghdad. Killed by Mirza Muhammad.
2 CONT * Hasan Ali succeeded his father.
2 CONT * Mirza Muhammad – son-in-law to Jahangir Beg, captured on 11 November 1467 at the Battle of Chapakchur and executed.
2 CONT * Mirza Yusuf – captured on 11 November 1467 at the Battle of Chapakchur and blinded. Executed by Ughurlu Muhammad on 22 October 1469.
2 CONT * Qasim Beg
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughters
2 CONT * Saliha Khatun
2 CONT * Habiba Khatun
2 CONT * Tutuq 'Ismat, married to Sultan Muhammad, Timurid ruler[33]
2 CONT * A daughter married to descendant of Nimatullah Vali
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
