1 NOTE {geni:about_me} (Qara Othman Bayandori) [Bahadori]
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------
2 CONT *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qara_Osman
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Uthman Beg ''' or '''Osman Beg''' (Azerbaijani: Qara Yuluq Osman Bəy; Turkish: '''Kara Yülük Osman Bey'''; 1356 – 1435) was a late 14th and early 15th-century leader of the Turkoman[1] tribal federation of Aq Qoyunlu in what is
2 CONC  now eastern Turkey, Iran, Azerbaijan and Iraq.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT -http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/TURKS.htm#KaraYuluk<br/>
2 CONT i)        '''  KARA YÜLÜK''' (-[Aug/Sep] 1435).  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' --- Komnene, daughter of ALEXIOS IV Emperor at Trebizond & his [wife Theodora Kantakouzene].  Her parentage and marriage are confirmed by the Historia Byzantina of Michælis Ducæ Nepotis which names "Karaiulucus...Alexii Co
2 CONC mneni imperatoris Trapezuntini gener" as ruler in "vicinus Lazis ac Persis"[1067].  Kuršankis dates the marriage to [1422] when he says that Kara Ilük conquered Karahisar, Tercan, Bayburt et Akşehir and besieged Erzincan with the 
2 CONC help of Emperor Alexios IV[1068].  If this dating is correct, her father must have been Emperor Alexios IV.  There is no indication whether this daughter was legitimate or illegitimate.  
2 CONT
2 CONT  Kara Yülük had one child:  
2 CONT --------------
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT The Aq Qoyunlu : Aq Qoyunlu From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Appointed governor of Diyarbekir by Tamerlan. Ruler	  • 1378–1435 Kara Yuluk Osman • 1501–1501 Murad ibn Ya'qub Historical era	Medieval • Established 1378 • Disestablished 1501 Preceded by Kara Koyunlu Succeeded by Safavid dynasty
2 CONC 	 The Aq Qoyunlu or Ak Koyunlu, also called the White Sheep Turkomans (Persian: آق‌ قویونلو‎ Āq Quyūnlū; Turkish: Ak Koyunlu), was a Persianate Sunni Oghuz Turkic tribal confederation that ruled present-day Armenia, Azerbaijan, Ea
2 CONC stern Turkey, most part of Iran, and Iraq from 1378 to 1501. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aq_Qoyunlu
2 CONT
2 CONT Qara Yuluk Osman Bek's ancestry goes back to Oghuz Kaqan emperor... According to Abubakr Al-Tehrani AlIsfahani,
2 CONT
2 CONT Sungur Bek who was the 20th great-great grandfather of Gara-Yuluk Osman, was a contemporary of the prophet Mohammad as well.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had waged a fierce war against the blasphemers on the frontiers around the summer pastures of the Alagoz Mountains and Gokche Lake and perished in one of the skirmishes. Qara-Yuluk Osman Bek’s 14th in-line predecessor, Shektur 
2 CONC Khan and a contemporary of Caliph Haroon Ar-Rashid, Gipchak Khan had also fought against the infidels with great bravery for the release of Alinja Fort. They both had inflicted heavy defeats on the enemies and liberated the Alinja
2 CONC  Fort from long drawn-out besiegement. 
2 CONT
2 CONT  (ref. document in MEDIA - by the Administrative Department of the President of the Republic of Azerbaijan - P R E S I D E N T I A L  L I B R A R Y page 6)
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT In 800/1398 Qara ʿOṯmān revolted against Qāżī Borhān-al-dīn and killed him in battle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Disorders in northern Syria following the deaths of Qāżī Borhān-al-dīn and the Mamluk sultan Barqūq (r. 784-801/1382-99) gave occasion for conflict between the expanding Ottoman power and the westward-moving conqueror Tīmūr. Volun
2 CONC tarily joining Tīmūr in 802/1399-1400, Qara ʿOṯmān led the vanguard in Tīmūr’s campaigns against Sīvās, whose defenders received Ottoman support (802-03/1402), and against the Mamluk dependencies; he also participated in the battl
2 CONC e of Ankara (804/1402), which ended in a crushing defeat for the Ottomans. In recompense, Tīmūr granted him the rank of amir, confirmed his leadership of the Āq Qoyunlū confederation, and made the Bayandor family custodians of the
2 CONC  fortress city of Āmed (Dīār Bakr) which before 796/1394 had been held by the Artuqids, a clan of the Döḡer tribe. This is apparently how the Āq Qoyunlū first came into possession of Āmed, which was to be their capital for almost 
2 CONC seventy years.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Tīmūr’s death in 807/1405, Qara ʿOṯmān maintained good relations with his successors. By contrast, the Qara Qoyunlū who had been forcibly subdued by Tīmūr, shook off Timurid suzerainty. Under Qara Yūsof, their leader from 79
2 CONC 2/1390 to 823/1420, they expelled Tīmūr’s grandson Abū Bakr and killed the latter’s father, Mīrānšāh, the governor of Azerbaijan; they then defeated the Jalayerids to gain control of Baghdad and ʿErāq-e ʿArab (Mesopotamia), conque
2 CONC red parts of Georgia, and penetrated deeper into Iran. The Timurid sultan Šāhroḵ was obliged to launch three campaigns (823-24/1420-21, 832/1429, and 838-39/1434-35) to check their expansion and reimpose his suzerainty. Šāhroḵ’s i
2 CONC nterventions, together with conflicts among the sons of Qara Yūsof, who had died at the time of the first campaign, weakened the position of the Qara Qoyunlū. In the following years they lost large areas in the west to the Āq Qoyu
2 CONC nlū.
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 823/1420 and Qara ʿOṯmān’s death in 839/1435, the Āq Qoyunlū established their authority in Armenia and Dīār Bakr and moved into Dīār Możar and the western part of Dīār Rabīʿa, an expansion which brought them in conflict w
2 CONC ith the formerly friendly Egyptian Mamluk sultanate. Accordingly, the Qara Qoyunlū allied themselves with Egypt while the Āq Qoyunlū joined with the Timurids, who were then contesting Egyptian hegemony in the Red Sea; Qara ʿOṯmān 
2 CONC also provided support for Šāhroḵ’s expeditions into Azerbaijan. During the third campaign, the aging Āq Qoyunlū leader, at Šāhroḵ’s behest, challenged Qara Yūsof’s son Eskandar to battle near Erzurum, but suffered a severe defeat;
2 CONC  he was put to death in Ṣafar, 839/August-September, 1435, and his head was sent by Eskandar to the Mamluk sultan Barsbay (r. 825-41/1422-38) in Cairo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Qara ʿOṯmān was the real founder of the Āq Qoyunlū state. Under his rule, the confederation not only acquired more territory but also gained support through additional tribes drawn to him by his successes. There are indications th
2 CONC at the mainly Christian sedentary inhabitants were not totally excluded from the economic, political, and social activities of the Āq Qoyunlū state and that Qara ʿOṯmān had at his command at least a rudimentary bureaucratic appara
2 CONC tus of the Iranian-Islamic type. Even so, the Turkman military elite clearly remained dominant. From 827/1424 onward, in the hope of counteracting the centrifugal tendencies inherent in a tribal confederation and making the princi
2 CONC pality more cohesive, Qara ʿOṯmān assigned newly conquered territories to his sons rather than to tribal chefs, but this policy did not produce the expected results (Wood, The Aqquyunlu, pp. 66-70).
2 CONT
2 CONT Qara ʿOṯmān’s death was followed by prolonged succession struggles in which not only the Mamluks, who had gained temporary recognition from some of the Āq Qoyunlū chiefs, but also the Ottomans, intervened. Qara ʿOṯmān’s designated
2 CONC  successor, ʿAlī, could not hold his ground against the claims of his brothers, uncles, and cousins, and in 841/1438-39 he abdicated and went into voluntary exile in Egypt. His brother Ḥamza was then the most powerful Āq Qoyunlū c
2 CONC hief, but he died in 848/1444 before he had been able to eliminate all rivals. The struggle for leadership resumed between Shaikh Ḥasan, a son of Qara ʿOṯmān, and ʿAlī’s son Jahāngīr. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://iranica.com/articles/aq-qoyunlu-white-sheep-confederation
2 CONT --------------------
