1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Alessio Spani''' or '''Lekë Shpani''' (1442-1495) was an Albanian nobleman. A member of the Spani family, which ruled [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drisht Drivast], in the mid-15th century he settled in Venice 
2 CONC and served as a diplomat of the republic in the Ottoman Empire.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alessio_Span En]
2 CONT
2 CONT ====[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alessio_Spani#Family Family]====
2 CONT
2 CONT A member of the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spani_family Spani family] he claimed that he was descended from several imperial Byzantine families. [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gjon_Muzaka Gjon Muzaka] in his Breve memoria de l
2 CONC i discendenti de nostra casa Musachi (1510), wrote that Span was descended from the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodosian_dynasty Theodosian dynasty]. Instead, Andreas Angelos Komnenos in Constitutio Ordinis Constantiniani Equ
2 CONC estris, claimed that [https://www.geni.com/people/Alexios-III-byzantine-emperor/6000000000515214098 '''Alexius III Angelos'''] was Span's great-great-great-grandfather. The complete genealogy presented by him is the following: 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/people/Alexios-III-byzantine-emperor/6000000000515214098 '''Alexius III Angelos'''], Byzantine Emperor from March 1195 to 17/18 July 1203 
2 CONT ** [https://www.geni.com/people/Alexios-Andreas-Posthumus-Dux-and-Comes-of-Drivasto/6000000205134389821 '''Alexios Andreas Posthumus'''] (d. 1260), Dux and Comes of Drivasto 
2 CONT *** [https://www.geni.com/people/Michael-Angelos-Comes-of-Drivasto/6000000205133863957 '''Michael Angelos'''] (d. 1318), Comes of Drivasto 
2 CONT **** [https://www.geni.com/people/Andreas-Angelos-Dux-and-Comes-of-Drivasto/6000000205134404821 '''Andreas Angelos'''] (d. 1366), Dux and Comes of Drivasto 
2 CONT ***** [https://www.geni.com/people/Michael/6000000205134200852 '''Michael'''] (d. 1465) 
2 CONT ****** [https://www.geni.com/people/Andreas-Angelos/6000000205134073889 '''Andreas Angelos''']
2 CONT ****** [https://www.geni.com/people/Alessio-Span-lord-of-Drivasto-Polog/6000000041839032060 '''Alessio Span'''] (b. 1442–d. 1495)
2 CONT
2 CONT Alessio acquired the surname Span from his mother [https://www.geni.com/people/Helen-Span/6000000205134015866 Helen Span], who was the second wife of [https://www.geni.com/people/Michael/6000000205134200852 Michael]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In Muzaka's work, [https://www.geni.com/people/Elisabeta-Brankovi%C4%87/6000000205134573850 '''Elisabeta or Miliza Branković'''], daughter of [https://www.geni.com/people/%C4%90ura%C4%91-Brankovi%C4%87/377258157880013634 '''Đurađ 
2 CONC Branković'''] '''(*)''', is mentioned as Span's wife. This was disputed by [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Franz_Babinger Franz Babinger] on the account that Đurađ had only two daughters, Mara and Katarina. Muzaka informs us that S
2 CONC pan had six children: Marco, Biasio, Lucia, Demetria, Angela and Andriana. Karl Hopf also presented a seventh child named Alessandro.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''(*)''' - Incorrect, written above at wikipedia source. '''Correct''': She was not a daughter of [https://www.geni.com/people/%C4%90ura%C4%91-Brankovi%C4%87/377258157880013634 '''Đurađ Branković'''], she was daughter of [https:/
2 CONC /www.geni.com/people/%C4%90or%C4%91e-Brankovi%C4%87-Archbishop-of-Belgrade/5130938345080024520 '''Đorđe Branković, Archbishop of Belgrade'''].
2 CONT
2 CONT ====[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alessio_Spani#Relations_with_Venice Relations with Venice]====
2 CONT
2 CONT Span's activities were closely related to the Venetian ones in Albania. [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gabriele_Trevisano Gabriel Trivisano], proveditor of Venice in Albania, includes Span in the group of the Albanian nobles that 
2 CONC were favourable to Venice and informed him about the political conditions in Albania. Span played an important role as an intermediary during the peace negotiations between Venice and the Ottoman Empire represented by [https://www
2 CONC .geni.com/people/Mahmud-Angelovi%C4%87-13th-Grand-Vizier/6000000205117325825 '''Mahmud Pasha'''] in the late 1460s.[2] For his services he received a stipend of 200 ducats per year and eventually incorporated a castle mentioned as
2 CONC  Chiro into the Venetian area of control (Venetian Albania).[1] 
