1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Handan Sultan (Ottoman Turkish: خندان سلطان; c. 1574[2] – 12 November 1605;[3] Handan meaning "Joyful") was the Ottoman Valide Sultan as mother of the Ottoman Sultan Ahmed I from 22 December 1603 until 12 November 
2 CONC 1605 although according to some sources including Yılmaz Öztuna, she died 27 November 1605.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Origin
2 CONT
2 CONT According to A.D. Alderson, her origin is unknown.[5] Some sources claim that she was ethnically Greek, originally named Helena.[1][self-published source][6][unreliable source]
2 CONT
2 CONT According to an alternative theory, she was from Bosnia and Herzegovina and her original name was Helen, this is supported by Turkish drama Muhteşem Yüzyıl: Kösem.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT She was one of the concubines of Mehmed III when he served as the governor of Manisa and gave birth to Ahmed I in 1590.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prior to Ahmed's accession to the throne, she has not enjoyed any kind of power and wasn't Mehmed III's Haseki Sultan (chief wife). She might have been completely overshadowed by Safiye Sultan, Ahmed I's paternal grandmother who c
2 CONC ontinued to manage the harem.
2 CONT
2 CONT Apparently, Handan couldn't surpass her mother-in-law Safiye Sultan, or her fellow consort Halime Sultan, and later her daughter-in-law Kösem Sultan, and was not an actress of the Sultanate of Women even when she became the Valide
2 CONC  Sultan and acted as the co-regent. During the first two years of the reign of Sultan Ahmed I, she was the Valide Sultan, being his mother, and wielded some kind of power, and tried to manage the government for two years, but as s
2 CONC ome chronicles say, she was totally inexperienced about her role.
2 CONT
2 CONT The contemporary historian Ibrahim Pecevi questioned her wisdom. Leslie Peirce points out that Ahmed tended to disregard her advice but believed that she ruled the Ottoman Empire behind the scenes, acting as the advisor of her you
2 CONC ng son.[7] While respecting the tradition that a mother's right is God's right, he ingeniously circumvented her will. She did not manage to receive either influence or economic funds as had the two previous Valide Sultans, this wa
2 CONC s probably due in a large measure because of the fact that she wasn't Mehmed III's Haseki Sultan and was a simple slave before she became the Valide Sultan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her salary was only 1000 aspers/a day even though she acted as the Valide Sultan during the first two years of the reign of her son Sultan Ahmed I while his grandmother Safiye was receiving 3000 akçes during the same period. At th
2 CONC e opposite, when Mustafa I ascended the throne in 1617, his mother Halime received 3,000 aspers/a day although her mother-in-law Safiye was still alive. Handan was well liked because she didn't interfered into politics the way tha
2 CONC t Safiye did, and was known for her kindness, wisdom and mercy and for her mild character.
2 CONT
2 CONT Handan also pushed her son to become a father and used to send him each night a slave. Ahmed hadn't produced any sons yet, so Mustafa was kept alive for the good of the dynasty probably due to Handan's influence.[citation needed] 
2 CONC Finally, Mahfiruz Hatice Sultan, gave bith to Osman II November 3, 1604.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Leslie Pierce on behalf of her son, Handan was running the state herself and leaded a political party composed of Boşnak Dervish Mehmed Pasha, Haci Mustafa Ağa (chief black eunuch), Ebülmeyamin Mustafa Efendi (the Sha
2 CONC ykh al-Islām of Istanbul) and Cerrah Mehmed Pasha.[citation needed] Since her son became the Sultan, Handan dismissed the most of the harem people and the palace servants who were close and connected to Safiye Sultan.[8] Death
2 CONT
2 CONT Handan died on 12 November 1605 at Topkapi Palace at a young age (30-31) during reign of her son Ahmed and the reason of her death is a matter of controversy. She probably died as a result of a severe stomach upset. They were some
2 CONC  speculations that she was possibly poisoned either by Safiye, Halime, or Kösem or that she committed a suicide or died because of her worry about her son who was facing the Jelali revolts.
2 CONT
2 CONT Handan's early death and the exile of Safiye to the Old Palace is one of the reasons that permitted the rise of Kösem who apparently couldn't rise in the Harem as long as Valide Handan was alive. She was buried near Mehmet III's t
2 CONC omb at the courtyard of Hagia Sophia. The inscription at the entrance of her husband Mehmed III's türbe, on which Handan's name is written. In popular culture
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 2015 TV series Muhteşem Yüzyıl: Kösem, Handan Sultan is portrayed by Turkish actress Tülin Özen.
2 CONT
2 CONT See also
2 CONT
