1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Boyarinya Kseniya Ioannovna (Ivanovna) Shestova (Russian: Ксения Иоанновна (Ивановна)) was a spouse of Fyodor Romanov and a mother of Mikhail Romanov. The origin of Xeniya Ivanovna has been disputed by genealogists
2 CONC  for centuries. It is currently accepted that her surname was Shestova (Шестова; rather than Shastunova, as was previously believed) and that her grandfather was Timofey Gryaznoy, a rich landowner from Uglich.
2 CONT
2 CONT During Boris Godunov's repressions against the Romanovs, she was forced to take a veil, changing her name to Martha (Russian: Marfa). After several years of exile at Tolvuyskiy pogost, she settled with her son in Kostroma. It was 
2 CONC there that the ambassadors arrived to inform Mikhail about his election to the Russian throne in 1613. As the previous tsars had been either killed or disgraced, Marfa at first opposed to bless her son and let him go to Moscow. At
2 CONC  last she gave up and blessed him with the icon Our Lady of St. Theodore, which became the palladium of the Romanov dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the first years of his reign, Marfa (or the "great nun" as she came to be known) exerted great influence on her moribund and listless son. She placed her relatives, the Saltykovs, at the important posts in the government, l
2 CONC eading to widespread corruption. The return of her husband from Poland in 1619 put an end to their (and her own) influence at court. She died on 27 January 1631.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Xenia Shestova
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Boyarinya Kseniya Ioannovna (Ivanovna) Shestova (Russian: Ксения Иоанновна (Ивановна)) was a spouse of Fyodor Romanov and the mother of Mikhail Romanov. The origin of Xeniya Ivanovna has been disputed by genealogists for centuries
2 CONC  [1]. It is currently accepted that her surname was Shestova (Шестова; rather than Shastunova, as was previously believed) and that her grandfather was Timofey Gryaznoy, a rich landowner from Uglich.
2 CONT
2 CONT During Boris Godunov's repressions against the Romanovs, she was forced to take a veil, changing her name to Martha (Russian: Marfa). After several years of exile at Tolvuyskiy pogost, she settled with her son in Kostroma. It was 
2 CONC there that the ambassadors arrived to inform Mikhail about his election to the Russian throne in 1613. As the previous tsars had been either killed or disgraced, Marfa at first opposed to bless her son and let him go to Moscow. At
2 CONC  last she gave up and blessed him with the icon Our Lady of St. Theodore, which became the palladium of the Romanov dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the first years of his reign, Marfa (or the "great nun" as she came to be known) exerted great influence on her moribund and listless son. She placed her relatives, the Saltykovs, at the important posts in the government, l
2 CONC eading to widespread corruption. The return of her husband from Poland in 1619 put an end to their (and her own) influence at court. She died on 27 January 1631.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Some sources alleged a royal origin for Xenia, as the illegitimate daughter of the Tzar Ivan IV of Russia by a mistress called Mayvi. If this fact is true, the Romanov descended in direct line from the House of Rurik.
2 CONT
2 CONT By her marriage she had:
2 CONT
2 CONT Boris (d. 20 November/30 November 1592) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nikita (d. 29 November/9 December 1593) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Lev (d. 21 September/1 October 1597) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tatiana (d. 4 November/14 November 1611), married to Prince Ivan Mikhailovich Katyrev-Rostovski (d. 1640) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mikhail Feodorovich Romanov 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivan (d. 7 June/17 June 1599) 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Boyarinya Kseniya Ioannovna (Ivanovna) Shestova (Russian: Ксения Ивановна Шестова (or Романова); 1560–1631) was a spouse of Fyodor Romanov and the mother of Mikhail Romanov.
2 CONT
2 CONT The origin of Xeniya Ivanovna has been disputed by genealogists for centuries. It is currently accepted that her surname was Shestova (Шестова; rather than Shastunova, as was previously believed) and that her grandfather was Timof
2 CONC ey Gryaznoy, a rich landowner from Uglich.
2 CONT
2 CONT During Boris Godunov's repression of the Romanovs, she was forced to take the veil, changing her name to Martha (Russian: Marfa). After several years of exile at Tolvuyskiy pogost, she settled with her son in Kostroma. It was ther
2 CONC e that the ambassadors arrived to inform Mikhail about his election to the Russian throne in 1613. As the previous tsars had been either killed or disgraced, Marfa at first declined to bless her son and let him go to Moscow.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the first years of his reign, Marfa (or the "great nun" as she came to be known) exerted great influence on her moribund and listless son. She placed her relatives, the Saltykovs, at the important posts in the government, l
2 CONC eading to widespread corruption. The return of her husband from Poland in 1619 put an end to their (and her own) influence at court. She died on 27 January 1631 and was buried in Novospassky Monastery.
