1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vasily_II_of_Moscow
2 CONT
2 CONT ===============
2 CONT
2 CONT Vasily (Vasili)  II Vasiliyevich "Tyomniy" (The Blind - but in Russian vers. - Dark ), Grand Prince of Moscow (Василий II Васильевич Тёмный - in Russian).  The fiftht and the youngest son of Vasily I.
2 CONT Principe de Moscú , de Russia. Born: March 10, 1415  Died: March 27, 1462, Moscow
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Vasily I Mother: Sophia of Lithuania Spouse: Maria Yaroslavna Issue:  Ivan Vasilievich Anna Vasilievna Andrey Bolshoy
2 CONT
2 CONT Predecessor: Vasily I 
2 CONT
2 CONT Successor: Ivan the Great 
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Vasily II was the Grand Prince of Moscow whose long reign (1425-1462) was plagued by the greatest civil war of Old Russian history.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vasily II was the youngest son of Vasily I of Moscow by Sophia of Lithuania, the only daughter of Vytautas the Great, and the only son to survive his father (his elder brother Ivan died in 1417 at the age of 22). On his father's d
2 CONC eath Vasily was proclaimed Grand Duke at the tender age of 10. His mother acted as a regent. His uncle, Yuri of Zvenigorod (Prince of Galich-Mersky), and his two sons, Vasily the Cross-Eyed and Dmitry Shemyaka, seized on the oppor
2 CONC tunity to advance their own claims to the throne. These claims were based on the Testament of Dmitri Donskoi, Yuri's father and Vasily's grandfather, who had stated that if Vasily I died Yuri would succeed his appanage. However, D
2 CONC mitri had written the testament when Vasily I had no children of his own, and it might be argued that this provision had been made only for the case of Vasily's childless death. Vasily's claim was supported by Vytautas, his matern
2 CONC al grandfather. The underlying causes for the great feudal war that followed are disputed. There are reasons to believe that Yuri's family, whose Northern dominions were rich in salt, ore, and other minerals could offer Russia a m
2 CONC ore liberal, prosperous path of development.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the wedding of Vasili II his mother pulls the golden belt from Prince Vasili the Cross-Eyed (1861)Upon Vytautas' death in 1430, Yuri went to the Golden Horde, returning with a license to take the Moscow throne. But the Khan did
2 CONC  not support him any further, largely due to the devices of the Smolensk princeling and Moscow boyarin Ivan Vsevolzhsky. When Yuri assembled an army and attacked Moscow, Vasily, betrayed by Vsevolzhsky, was defeated and captured b
2 CONC y his enemies (1433). Upon being proclaimed Grand Duke of Moscow, Yuri pardoned his nephew and sent him to reign in the town of Kolomna. That proved to be a mistake, as Vasily immediately started to plot against his uncle and gath
2 CONC er all sort of malcontents. Feeling how insecure his throne was, Yuri resigned and then left Moscow for his Northern hometown. When Vasily returned to Moscow, he had Vsevolzhsky blinded as a traitor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile, Yuri's claim was inherited by his sons who decided to continue the fight. They managed to defeat Vasily, who had to seek refuge in the Golden Horde. After Yuri died in 1434, Vasili the Cross-Eyed entered the Kremlin and
2 CONC  was proclaimed new Grand Duke. Dmitry Shemyaka, who had his own plans for the throne, quarreled with his brother and concluded an alliance with Vasily II. Together they managed to banish Vasily the Cross-Eyed from the Kremlin in 
2 CONC 1435. The latter was captured and blinded, which effectively removed him from the contest for the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vasily's reign saw the collapse of the Golden Horde and its break up into smaller Khanates. Now that his throne was relatively secure, he had to deal with the Tatar threat.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1439, Vasily had to flee the capital, when it was besieged by Olug Moxammat, ruler of the nascent Kazan Khanate. Six years later, he personally led his troops against Olug Moxammat, but was defeated and taken prisoner. The Russ
2 CONC ians were forced to gather an enormous ransom for their prince, so that Vasily could be released some five months later.
2 CONT
2 CONT During that time, the control of Moscow passed to Dmitry Shemyaka. Keeping in mind the fate of his own brother, Dmitry had Vasily blinded and exiled him to Uglich (1446). Hence, Vasili's nickname Tyomniy, which stands for "blind" 
2 CONC (or, more accurately, "seeing darkness"). As Vasily still had a number of supporters in Moscow, Dmitry recalled him from exile and gave him Vologda as an appanage. That proved to be a new mistake, as Vasily quickly assembled his s
2 CONC upporters and regained the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vasily's final victory against his cousin came in the 1450s, when he captured Galich-Mersky and poisoned Dmitry. The latter's children managed to escape to Lithuania. These events finally put to rest the principle of collateral su
2 CONC ccession, which was a major cause of medieval internecine struggles.
2 CONT
2 CONT Now that the war was over, Vasily eliminated almost all of the small appanages in Moscow principality, so as to strengthen his sovereign authority. As a result of his military campaigns, republican governments in Novgorod, Pskov a
2 CONC nd Vyatka were forced to acknowledge him as their overlord.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the meantime, Constantinople fell to the Turks, and the Patriarch agreed to acknowledge the supremacy of the Pope in the Council of Florence. Vasily promptly rejected this arrangement. By his order in 1448, bishop Jonah was app
2 CONC ointed metropolitan of Russia, which was tantamount to declaration of independence of the Russian Orthodox Church from the Patriarch of Constantinople. This move further strengthened Russia's reputation among Orthodox states.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his later years the blind prince was greatly helped by Metropolitan Jonah, boyars, and then by his older son Ivan III who was styled co-ruler since the late 1450s. On Vasily's death in 1462 Ivan III succeeded him as Grand Princ
2 CONC e of Moscow. His daughter Anna was married to a prince of Ryazan.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Vasily II of Moscow
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Vasily II Vasiliyevich Tyomniy (Blind) (Василий II Васильевич Тёмный in Russian) (March 10, 1415 – March 27, 1462, Moscow) was the Grand Prince of Moscow whose long reign (1425-1462) was plagued by the greatest civil war of Old Ru
2 CONC ssian history.
2 CONT
2 CONT First ten years of internecine struggle
2 CONT
2 CONT Vasily II was the youngest son of Vasily I of Moscow by Sophia of Lithuania, the only daughter of Vytautas the Great, and the only son to survive his father (his elder brother Ivan died in 1417 at the age of 22). On his father's d
2 CONC eath Vasily was proclaimed Grand Duke at the tender age of 10. His mother acted as a regent. His uncle, Yuri of Zvenigorod (Prince of Galich-Mersky), and his two sons, Vasily the Cross-Eyed and Dmitry Shemyaka, seized on the oppor
2 CONC tunity to advance their own claims to the throne. These claims were based on the Testament of Dmitri Donskoi, Yuri's father and Vasily's grandfather, who had stated that if Vasily I died Yuri would succeed his appanage. However, D
2 CONC mitri had written the testament when Vasily I had no children of his own, and it might be argued that this provision had been made only for the case of Vasily's childless death. Vasily's claim was supported by Vytautas, his matern
2 CONC al grandfather. The underlying causes for the great feudal war that followed are disputed. There are reasons to believe that Yuri's family, whose Northern dominions were rich in salt, ore, and other minerals could offer Russia a m
2 CONC ore liberal, prosperous path of development.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon Vytautas' death in 1430, Yuri went to the Golden Horde, returning with a license to take the Moscow throne. But the Khan did not support him any further, largely due to the devices of the Smolensk princeling and Moscow boyari
2 CONC n Ivan Vsevolzhsky. When Yuri assembled an army and attacked Moscow, Vasily, betrayed by Vsevolzhsky, was defeated and captured by his enemies (1433). Upon being proclaimed Grand Duke of Moscow, Yuri pardoned his nephew and sent h
2 CONC im to reign in the town of Kolomna. That proved to be a mistake, as Vasily immediately started to plot against his uncle and gather all sort of malcontents. Feeling how insecure his throne was, Yuri resigned and then left Moscow f
2 CONC or his Northern hometown. When Vasily returned to Moscow, he had Vsevolzhsky blinded as a traitor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile, Yuri's claim was inherited by his sons who decided to continue the fight. They managed to defeat Vasily, who had to seek refuge in the Golden Horde. After Yuri died in 1434, Vasili the Cross-Eyed entered the Kremlin and
2 CONC  was proclaimed new Grand Duke. Dmitry Shemyaka, who had his own plans for the throne, quarreled with his brother and concluded an alliance with Vasily II. Together they managed to banish Vasily the Cross-Eyed from the Kremlin in 
2 CONC 1435. The latter was captured and blinded, which effectively removed him from the contest for the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Kazan and Shemyaka
2 CONT
2 CONT Vasily's reign saw the collapse of the Golden Horde and its break up into smaller Khanates. Now that his throne was relatively secure, he had to deal with the Tatar threat.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1439, Vasily had to flee the capital, when it was besieged by Olug Moxammat, ruler of the nascent Kazan Khanate. Six years later, he personally led his troops against Olug Moxammat, but was defeated and taken prisoner. The Russ
2 CONC ians were forced to gather an enormous ransom for their prince, so that Vasily could be released some five months later.
2 CONT
2 CONT During that time, the control of Moscow passed to Dmitry Shemyaka. Keeping in mind the fate of his own brother, Dmitry had Vasily blinded and exiled him to Uglich (1446). Hence, Vasili's nickname 'the blind' (Tyomniy, literally 'd
2 CONC ark'). As Vasily still had a number of supporters in Moscow, Dmitry recalled him from exile and gave him Vologda as an appanage. That proved to be a new mistake, as Vasily quickly assembled his supporters and regained the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vasily's final victory against his cousin came in the 1450s, when he captured Galich-Mersky and poisoned Dmitry. The latter's children managed to escape to Lithuania. These events finally put to rest the principle of collateral su
2 CONC ccession, which was a major cause of medieval internecine struggles.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Later reign and policies
2 CONT
2 CONT Now that the war was over, Vasily eliminated almost all of the small appanages in Moscow principality, so as to strengthen his sovereign authority. His military campaigns of 1441-60 increased Moscow's hold over Suzdal, the Vyatka 
2 CONC lands and the republican governments of Novgorod and Pskov.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the meantime, Constantinople fell to the Turks, and the Patriarch agreed to acknowledge the supremacy of the Pope in the Council of Florence. Vasily promptly rejected this arrangement. By his order in 1448, bishop Jonah was app
2 CONC ointed metropolitan of Russia, which was tantamount to declaration of independence of the Russian Orthodox Church from the Patriarch of Constantinople. This move further strengthened Russia's reputation among Orthodox states.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his later years the blind prince was greatly helped by Metropolitan Jonah, boyars, and then by his older son Ivan III who was styled co-ruler since the late 1450s. On Vasily's death in 1462 Ivan III succeeded him as Grand Princ
2 CONC e of Moscow. His daughter Anna was married to a prince of Ryazan.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vasily_II_of_Russia
