1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Kösem Sultan (Turkish pronunciation: [cøˈsem sulˈtan]) (c. 1590 – 2 September 1651) – also known as Mahpeyker Sultan[4] (Turkish pronunciation: [mahpejˈkeɾ sulˈtan]) – was one of the most powerful women in Ottoman 
2 CONC history.[4][5][6][7] Kösem achieved power and influenced the politics of the Ottoman Empire when she became Haseki Sultan as favourite consort and later legal wife of Ottoman Sultan Ahmed I (r. 1603–1617) and Valide Sultan[4] as m
2 CONC other of Murad IV (r. 1623–1640) and Ibrahim (r. 1640–1648), and grandmother of Mehmed IV (r. 1648–1687). She was one of the prominent figures during the Sultanate of Women.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kösem lived in the Ottoman Empire during the reign of six sultans, Ahmed I, Mustafa I, Osman II, Murad IV, Ibrahim, and Mehmed IV. After her death, she was known by the names "Valide-i Maktule" (murdered mother), and "Valide-i Șeh
2 CONC ide" (martyred mother).[8] She and her daughter-in-law Turhan Hatice Sultan are the only women that ruled the Ottoman Empire solely.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT Kösem was of Greek origin;[9][10][11][12] she is generally said to be the daughter of a priest on the island of Tinos whose maiden name was Anastasia[13][14][15][15][16][17] but these views do not seem reliable.[12] She was bought
2 CONC  as a slave by the Bosnian governor, and sent, at the age of fifteen, to the harem of Sultan Ahmed I. Upon her conversion to Islam, her name was changed to Mahpeyker (Moon-Shaped), and later by Sultan Ahmed I to Kösem.[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT Haseki Sultan
2 CONT
2 CONT In early reign of Ahmed, there was a change in the imperial harem hierarchy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Safiye Sultan, Ahmed's powerful grandmother who continued to manage the harem was deprived of power and retired in Old Palace in January 1604.
2 CONT
2 CONT One year later, Handan Sultan, Ahmed's mother who became chief of imperial harem as Valide Sultan died in November 1605. This condition permitted the rise of Kösem who was Haseki Sultan in top of imperial harem hierarchy.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a Haseki Sultan to Ahmed I, she had a long career as guardian of şehzades. It is possible that the significant modifications in the pattern of succession to the throne during Ahmed's time owed something to her efforts. She must
2 CONC  have realized the personal gain that might stem from the transition to seniority, coupled with the fact that she was no longer haseki but had son "in waiting". According to Venetian ambassador, Kösem "lobbied to spare Mustafa the
2 CONC  fate of fratricide with the ulterior goal of saving her own son from the same fate."[18] During her career as haseki sultan, Kösem received 1,000 aspers a day.[19]
2 CONT
2 CONT Venetian ambassador Contarini reported the beating of a woman who had irritated Kösem, ordered by the sultan, in 1612, which may be identified to her fellow consort Mahfiruz Hatice Sultan.[20] She also made efforts to keep her bro
2 CONC ther-in-law, Mustafa, safe from execution, and saw an obstacle in Mahfiruz who was lobbying in favor of her son.[20]
2 CONT
2 CONT Like his father Mehmed III and mother Handan, Ahmed died at young age (27 years) on 22 November 1617. This made Kösem lose her position in Topkapi Palace and she retired in Old Palace during the reign of her brother-in-law Mustafa
2 CONC  I and step-son Osman II. Valide Sultan First tenure
2 CONT
2 CONT Kösem came back in power when her son ascended to the throne on 10 September 1623 as Murad IV. She was appointed not only as Valide Sultan but also, as her son was a minor, as official regent (naib-i-sultanat) during his minority 
2 CONC from her son's ascension on 10 September 1623 until 18 May 1632.[21] During most of the reign of Murad IV she essentially ruled through him and effectively ran the empire, attending meetings of the divan (cabinet) from behind a cu
2 CONC rtain, even after 1632, when she was no longer regent.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the early years the Empire fell into anarchy; the Safavid Empire invaded Iraq almost immediately, Northern Anatolia erupted in revolts, and in 1631 the Janissaries stormed the palace and killed the Grand Vizier, among other
2 CONC s. Murad feared suffering the same fate as his elder brother, Osman II, and decided to assert his power. He later tried to quell the corruption that had grown during the reigns of previous Sultans, and that had not been checked wh
2 CONC ile his mother was ruling through proxy. His absolute rule started around 1632, when he took the authority and repressed all the tyrants, and he re-established the supremacy of Sultan. Second tenure
2 CONT
2 CONT Kösem's other son, Ibrahim, lived in terror of being the next of his brothers to die by his brother's order. His life was only saved by the intercession of his mother Kösem Sultan.[12] After Murad's death, Ibrahim was left the sol
2 CONC e surviving prince of the dynasty. Upon being asked by Grand Vizier Kemankeş Kara Mustafa Pasha to assume the Sultanate, Ibrahim suspected Murad was still alive and plotting to trap him. It took the combined persuasion of Kösem an
2 CONC d the Grand Vizier, and personal examination of his brother's dead body, to make Ibrahim accept the throne. When Ibrahim succeeded his brother in 1640, he proved too mentally unstable to rule. This enabled Kösem to continue in pow
2 CONC er. Since Ibrahim was the only surviving male member of the Ottoman dynasty, he was encouraged by his mother to distract himself with harem girls. The distractions of the harem allowed Kösem to gain power and rule in his name, yet
2 CONC  even she fell victim to the Sultan's disfavor and left the Imperial Palace.[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ibrahim's behaviour sparked talks of deposing the sultan. In 1647, the Grand Vizier Salih Pasha, Kösem Sultan, and the şeyhülislam Abdürrahim Efendi unsuccessfully plotted to depose the sultan and replace him with one of his sons.
2 CONC  Salih Pasha was executed and Kösem Sultan was exiled from the harem.[22] The next year the Janissaries and members of the ulema revolted. On August 8, 1648, Ibrahim was dethroned, seized and imprisoned in Topkapı Palace.[12][23] 
2 CONC Kösem gave consent to her son's fall, saying "In the end he will leave neither you nor me alive. We will lose control of the government. The whole society is in ruins. Have him removed from the throne immediately."[24] The new Gra
2 CONC nd Vizier, Ṣofu Meḥmed Pasha, petitioned the Sheikh ul-Islam for a fatwā sanctioning Ibrahim's execution. It was granted, with the message "if there are two Caliphs, kill one of them." Kösem also gave her consent. Two executioners
2 CONC  were sent.[25] Ibrahim was strangled on August 18, 1648. Third tenure
2 CONT
2 CONT Eventually Kösem presented her seven-year-old grandson Mehmed IV to the divan with the words "Here he is!, see what you can do with him!" Thus, she declared herself regent for the second time, and ruled openly again between 1648 a
2 CONC nd 1651. At the head of the Ottoman Empire stood the child sultan, Mehmed IV. With Mehmed's ascendancy, the position of Valide Sultan ("mother of the reigning sultan") should have gone to his mother Turhan Hatice Sultan. However, 
2 CONC Turhan was overlooked due to her youth and inexperience. Instead Kösem Sultan was reinstated to this high position. Kösem Sultan was a valide (mother) under two sons, thus having the more experience of the two women.[26] Death
2 CONT
2 CONT It was Mehmed IV's mother, Turhan Hatice, who proved to be Kösem's nemesis. When she was about 12 years old, Turhan was sent to the Topkapı Palace as a gift, from the Khan of Crimea, to Kösem Sultan.[27][28] It was probably Kösem 
2 CONC Sultan who gave Turhan Hatice to Ibrahim as a concubine. Turhan turned out to be too ambitious a woman to lose such a high position without a fight. In her struggle to become Valide Sultan, Turhan was supported by the chief black 
2 CONC eunuch in her household and the grand vizier, while Kösem was supported by the Janissary Corps. Although, Kösem’s position as Valide was seen as the best for the government, the people resented the influence of the Janissaries on 
2 CONC the government.[29]
2 CONT
2 CONT In this power struggle, Kösem planned to dethrone Mehmed and replace him with another young grandson. According to one historian, this switching had more to do with replacing an ambitious daughter-in-law with one who was more easi
2 CONC ly controlled. The plan was unsuccessful as it was reported to Turhan by Melek Hatun, one of Kösem’s slaves, that Kösem was said to be plotting Mehmed's removal and replacement by another grandson with a more pliant mother.[29] Wh
2 CONC ether Turhan sanctioned it or not, Kösem Sultan was murdered three years after becoming regent for her young grandson. It is rumoured that Turhan ordered Kösem's assassination. Furthermore, some have speculated that Kösem was stra
2 CONC ngled with a curtain by the chief black eunuch of the harem, Tall Suleiman. The Ottoman renegade Bobovi, relying on an informant in the harem, states that Kösem was strangled with her own hair.[30]
2 CONT
2 CONT After her death her body was taken from Topkapi to the Old Palace (Eski Sarayı) and then buried in the mausoleum of her husband Ahmed I.[31] Kösem was renowned for her charity work and for freeing her slaves after 3 years of servi
2 CONC ce. When she died the people of Constantinople observed three days of mourning. Issue Kösem Sultan's sons who were Sultans of the Ottoman Empire. Left: Murad IV (ca. 1612–1640) Right: Ibrahim (ca. 1615–1648) Sons
2 CONT
2 CONT     Şehzade Mehmed (8 March 1605 – murdered 12 January 1621) (according to a conjecture by Baki Tezcan, though he is generally not believed to be Kösem's son[32])
2 CONT     Murad IV (26/27 July 1612 – 8 February 1640). Became 17th Ottoman Sultan from 20 January 1623 until his death.[1][2]
2 CONT     Şehzade Kasim (early 1614 – 17 February 1638), Crown Prince since 1635.[1][2]
2 CONT     Şehzade Suleiman (1615 – murdered 27 July 1635).[1]
2 CONT     Ibrahim (5 November 1615 – 18 August 1648). Became 18th Ottoman Sultan from 9 February 1640 until 12 August 1648.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughters
2 CONT
2 CONT     Ayşe Sultan (1605 – May 1657).[1]
2 CONT     Fatma Sultan (1606 – 1670).[1][3]
2 CONT     Gevherhan Sultan (1608 – 1660).[1][3]
2 CONT     Hanzade Sultan (1609 – 21 September 1650).[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT In popular culture
2 CONT
2 CONT     Genç Osman ve Sultan Murat Han (1962) movie, starring Muhterem Nur as Kösem Sultan
2 CONT     IV. Murat (1980)TV series, starring Ayten Gökçer as Kösem Sultan
2 CONT     İstanbul Kanatlarımın Altında (1996) movie, starring Zuhal Olcay as Kösem Sultan
2 CONT     Ankara Theatre (2013-2014 season) Özlem Ersönmez as Kösem Sultan.
2 CONT     Mahpeyker: Kösem Sultan (2010), starring Damla Sönmez (as young Kösem) and Selda Alkor (as old Kösem[33][34]
2 CONT     Tims Production is producing a drama serial following Muhteşem Yüzyıl titled Muhteşem Yüzyıl: Kösem, starring Nezihe Güneş Ersoy (as child Kösem) Anastasia Tsilimpiou (as young Kösem) and Beren Saat (as adult Kösem). The show 
2 CONC started on 12 November 2015.
2 CONT
2 CONT See also
2 CONT
2 CONT     List of mothers of the Ottoman sultans
2 CONT     List of consorts of the Ottoman sultans
2 CONT
