1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murad_IV
2 CONT
2 CONT Murad IV (Ottoman Turkish: مراد رابع Murād-ı Rābi‘‎; July 26/27, 1612 – February 8, 1640) was the Sultan of the Ottoman Empire from 1623 to 1640, known both for restoring the authority of the state and for the brutality of his met
2 CONC hods. Murad IV was born in Istanbul, the son of Sultan Ahmed I (r. 1603–17) and the ethnic Greek[1][2][3] Valide Kösem Sultan. Brought to power by a palace conspiracy in 1623, he succeeded his uncle Mustafa I (r. 1617–18, 1622–23)
2 CONC . He was only 11 when he took the throne. His reign is most notable for the Ottoman–Safavid War (1623–39), of which the outcome would permanently part the Caucasus between the two Imperial powers for around two centuries, while it
2 CONC  also roughly laid the foundation for the current Turkey - Iran - Iraq borders.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT In the early years of Murad's reign, he was under the control of his relatives. During this period, peace and harmony in the Ottoman lands were completely lost, and tyrants took control of the cities. His absolute rule started aro
2 CONC und 1632, when he took the authority and repressed all the tyrants, and he re-etablished the supremacy of Sultan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early reign (1623–32)
2 CONT
2 CONT Murad IV was for a long time under the control of his relatives and during his early years as Sultan, his mother, Kösem Sultan, essentially ruled through him. The Empire fell into anarchy; the Safavid Empire invaded Iraq almost im
2 CONC mediately, Northern Anatolia erupted in revolts, and in 1631 the Janissaries stormed the palace and killed the Grand Vizier, among others. Murad IV feared suffering the fate of his elder brother, Osman II (1618–22), and decided to
2 CONC  assert his power.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the age of 16 in 1628, he had his brother-in-law (his sister Fatma Sultan's husband) and the former governor of Egypt Kara Mustafa Pasha executed for a claimed action "against the law of God".[4] Absolute rule and imperial poli
2 CONC cies (1632–40)
2 CONT
2 CONT Murad IV tried to quell the corruption that had grown during the reigns of previous Sultans, and that had not been checked while his mother was ruling through proxy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Murad IV also banned alcohol, tobacco, and coffee in Constantinople.[5] He ordered execution for breaking this ban.[6] He would reportedly patrol the streets and the lowest taverns of Istanbul in civilian clothes at night, policin
2 CONC g the enforcement of his command by casting off his disguise on the spot and beheading the offender with his own hands.[7] Rivaling the exploits of Selim the Grim, he would sit in a kiosk by the water near his Seraglio Palace[7] a
2 CONC nd shoot arrows at any boat man who rowed too close to his imperial compound. He restored the judicial regulations by very strict punishments, including execution, he once strangled a grand vizier for the reason that the official 
2 CONC had beaten his mother-in-law.[7] Historians including Halil İnalcık as well as primary sources report that even though he was a ruthless supporter of alcohol prohibition, Murad IV was a habitual drinker himself.[8][9]
2 CONT
2 CONT War against Safavid Iran
2 CONT
2 CONT Murad IV's reign is most notable for the Ottoman–Safavid War (1623–39) against Persia in which Ottoman forces managed to conquer Azerbaijan, occupying Tabriz, Hamadan, and capturing Baghdad in 1638. Murad IV himself commanded the 
2 CONC invasion of Mesopotamia and proved to be an outstanding field commander. By the Treaty of Zuhab which followed after the war, it roughly comprised and confirmed the borders as per the Peace of Amasya, with Eastern Armenia, Eastern
2 CONC  Georgia, Azerbaijan, and Dagestan staying Persian, while Western Armenia, and Western Georgia staying Ottoman.[10] Mesopotamia was irrevocably lost for the Persians.[11] the borders per the outcome of the war is more or less the 
2 CONC present border line between Turkey - Iraq and Iran.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the siege of Baghdad, the city withstood the siege for forty days, but was compelled to surrender, and the bulk of the population were butchered by the conquerors, in spite of the promises which they had made to spare them.
2 CONC  It is said that the officers of Murad arranged a sort of tableau, in which the heads were struck off one thousand captives by one thousand headsmen at the same moment, and that Murad IV enjoyed the sight. The sultan had a famous 
2 CONC quote about the fall of Baghdad: ("Trying to conquer Baghdad, was almost more beautiful than Baghdad itself.").[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT Murad IV himself commanded the Ottoman army in the last years of the war, and proved to be an outstanding field commander. He was the second Ottoman Sultan to command an army on the battlefield since the death of Suleiman the Magn
2 CONC ificent in 1566. During his campaign to Persia, he annihilated all rebels in Anatolia and restored order in the Empire.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT After his return to Constantinople, he ordered respected statesmen of the Empire to prepare a new economic and political project to return the Empire to the old successful days.
2 CONT
2 CONT Physical power
2 CONT
2 CONT Murad IV was the last Warrior Sultan who led campaigns in front of his army and fought on the battlefield. His physical strength was phenomenal, which is described in detail on the books of Evliya Çelebi. He was especially known f
2 CONC or his exceptional strength in wrestling - capable of fighting several opponents at the same time. His favorite weapon was a huge mace, which, according to legend, he wielded effortlessly with a single hand despite it reportedly w
2 CONC eighed 60 kilograms (132 lbs).[13] Among his other favorite weapons are a longbow and a large two-handed broadsword apparently weighing more than 50 kilograms (110 lbs), despite the fact that even the largest known swords (used fo
2 CONC r combat) rarely weigh more than 10 lbs, and ceremonial parade swords weighing more than 15 lbs are exceedingly rare.[14] His weapons are today displayed at the Topkapı Palace Museum in Fatih, intact and well preserved.[citation n
2 CONC eeded]
2 CONT
2 CONT Architecture
2 CONT
2 CONT Sultan Murad IV put emphasis on architecture and in his period many monuments were erected. Some of them are Meydanı Mosque, Bayram Pasha Dervish Lodge, Tomb, Fountain, Primary School, Konya Serefeddin Mosque.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Mughal Emperor Shah Jahan had exchanged ambassadors with the Ottoman Sultan Murad IV, it was through these exchanges that he received Isa Muhammad Effendi and Ismail Effendi, two Turkish architects and students of the famous K
2 CONC oca Mimar Sinan Agha. Both of them later comprised among the Mughal team that would design and build the Taj Mahal. Relations with the Mughal Empire
2 CONT
2 CONT In the year 1626, the Mughal Emperor Jahangir began to contemplate an alliance between the Ottomans, Mughals and Uzbeks against the Safavids, who had defeated the Mughals at Kandahar. He even wrote a letter to the Ottoman Sultan M
2 CONC urad IV, Jahangir's ambition however did not materialize due to his death in 1627. However, Jahangir's son and successor Shah Jahan pursued the goal of alliance with the Ottoman Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT While he was encamped in Baghdad, Murad IV is known to have met the Mughal Emperor Shah Jahan's ambassadors: Mir Zarif and Mir Baraka, who presented 1000 pieces of finely embroidered cloth and even armor. Murad IV gave them the fi
2 CONC nest weapons, saddles and Kaftans and ordered his forces to accompany the Mughals to the port of Basra, where they set sail to Thatta and finally Surat.[15] Marriages and Progeny
2 CONT
2 CONT Consorts
2 CONT
2 CONT Very little is known about the concubines of Murad IV, principally because he didn't left sons who survived his death to reach the throne but, privy purse registers record the presence of a single haseki, Ayşe Haseki Sultan, until
2 CONC  the very end of Murad's seventeen-year reign, when a second haseki appears.[16]
2 CONT
2 CONT It is possible that Murad had only a single concubine until the advent of the second, or that he had a number of concubines but singled out only one as haseki.[17]
2 CONT
2 CONT If Ayse was his only concubine, it is possible that it was fear of lack of male issue that prompted the sultan to take another, for his sons all died in infancy.[18] Sons
2 CONT
2 CONT     Şehzade Ahmed (21 December 1627–1628)
2 CONT     Şehzade Numan (1628–1629)
2 CONT     Şehzade Orhan (1629–1629)
2 CONT     Şehzade Hasan (March 1631 – 1632)
2 CONT     Şehzade Suleiman (2 February 1632 – 1635)
2 CONT     Şehzade Mehmed (8 August 1633 – 1637)
2 CONT     Şehzade Osman (9 February 1634 – February 1634)
2 CONT     Şehzade Alaeddin (26 August 1635 – 1637)
2 CONT     Şehzade Selim (1637–1640)
2 CONT     Şehzade Abdul Hamid (15 May 1638 – 1638)
2 CONT     Şehzade Mahmud (May 1640 – 1647)
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughters
2 CONT
2 CONT     İsmihan Sultan (1630–1630)
2 CONT     Gevherhan Sultan (February 1630 – ?), married 1645, Damat Haseki Mehmed Pasha, sometime Fifth Vizier;
2 CONT     Hanzade Sultan (1630–1675), married August 1645, Damat Nakkaş Hasan Pasha, Vizier of Egypt 1640-1642;
2 CONT     Kaya Sultan (1633 – 28 February 1659), married August 1644, Damat Abaza Melek Ahmed Pasha, Vizier 1638 and 1650–1651;
2 CONT     Gülbahar Sultan (1634-1645)
2 CONT     Safiye Sultan, married 1659, Damat Abaza Husein Pasha, Vizier 1674–1675, son of Abaza Siyavuş Pasha;
2 CONT     Fatma Sultan (1636–1640)
2 CONT     Rabia Sultan (1636–1639/1690)
2 CONT     Rukiye Sultan (1640 – January 1690), married January 1663, Şeytan Divrikli Ibrahim Pasha, Vizier.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death
2 CONT
2 CONT Murad IV died from cirrhosis in İstanbul at the age of 27 in 1640.[19]
2 CONT
2 CONT Rumours had circulated that on his deathbed, Murad IV ordered the execution of his mentally disabled brother, Ibrahim I (reigned 1640–48), which would have meant the end of the Ottoman line. However, the order was not carried out.
2 CONC [20] See also
