1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Vasili IV of Russia (Basil IV, Russian: Василий IV Иванович Шуйский, Vasíliy Ivánovich Shúyskiy, other transliterations: Vasiliy, Vasily, Vasilii)
2 CONT
2 CONT * Father: Ivan Andreyevich, Prince Shuisky
2 CONT
2 CONT * Mother: Marfa Feodorovna
2 CONT
2 CONT Predecessor: http://www.geni.com/people/False-Dmitriy-I-of-Russia/6000000010503212851#/tab/overview
2 CONT
2 CONT Successor:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vasili_IV_of_Russia
2 CONT
2 CONT Vasili IV of Russia (Russian: Василий IV Иванович Шуйский, Vasíliy Ivánovich Shúyskiy, other transliterations: Vasiliy, Vasily, Vasilii) (22 September 1552 – 12 September 1612) was Tsar of Russia between 1606 and 1610 after the mu
2 CONC rder of False Dmitriy I. His reign fell during the Time of Troubles.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born Prince Vasily Ivanovich Shuisky, he was descended from sovereign princes of Nizhny Novgorod and a 20th generation male line descendant of Rurik the Viking. He was one of the leading boyars of Tsardom of Russia during the reig
2 CONC ns of Feodor I and Boris Godunov. In all the court intrigues of the Time of Troubles, Vasily and his younger brother Dmitry Shuisky usually acted together and fought as one.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was he who, in obedience to the secret orders of Tsar Boris, went to Uglich to inquire into the cause of the death of the Tsarevich Dmitry Ivanovich, the youngest son of Ivan the Terrible, who had perished there in mysterious c
2 CONC ircumstances. Shuisky reported that it was a case of suicide, though rumors abounded that the Tsarevich had been assassinated on the orders of the regent Boris Godunov. Some suspected that Dmitry escaped the assassination and that
2 CONC  another boy was killed in his place, providing impetus for the repeated appearance of impostors (See False Dmitry I, False Dmitry II, and False Dmitry III). On the death of Boris, who had become tsar, and the accession of his son
2 CONC  Feodor II, Shuisky went back upon his own words in order to gain favour with the pretender False Dmitriy I, who was attempting to gain the throne by impersonating the dead Tsarevich. Shuisky recognized the pretender as the "real"
2 CONC  Dmitry despite having earlier determined the boy had committed suicide, thus bringing about the assassination of the young Feodor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shuisky then conspired against the false Dmitriy and brought about his death (May 1606). After stating publicly that the real Dmitriy had indeed been slain and that the reigning tsar was an impostor, Shuisky's adherents thereupon 
2 CONC proclaimed him tsar on 19 May 1606. He reigned until 19 July 1610, but was never generally recognized. Even in Moscow itself he had little or no authority, and he only avoided deposition by the dominant boyars because they had no 
2 CONC one to replace him with.
2 CONT
2 CONT The popularity of his cousin, Prince Mikhail Skopin-Shuisky, who commanded an army, and mercenaries from Sweden, whose loyalty he purchased with the cession of Russian territory, allowed Shuisky, for a time, to remain on his unsta
2 CONC ble throne. In 1610, he was deposed by his former adherents Princes Vorotynsky and Mstislavsky. He was made a monk and eventually transported to Warsaw by the Polish hetman Stanislaus Zolkiewski. He died a prisoner in the castle o
2 CONC f Gostynin, near Warsaw, in 1612.
