1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Jerzy Wigunt Trubecki: The Kidnapped Prince of Trubetsk''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chapter 1. Born to a Lost Principality – The Early Life of Jerzy Wigunt Trubecki (1643-1644)''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Jerzy Wigunt Trubecki h. Pogoń Litewska''' was born around 1643 in Starodub, a key settlement within the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth. Jerzy belonged to the powerful Trubecki noble family, which traced its lineage to the Gedi
2 CONC minid dynasty of Lithuania, a lineage of Lithuanian origin associated with the heraldic symbol Pogoń Litewska. He was the only known son of Piotr Wigant Trubecki, the pro-Polish and anti-Muscovite ruler of the Principality of Trub
2 CONC etsk, and Halszka Drucka-Sokolińska, a member of the influential Drucki-Sokoliński noble family. Jerzy’s father, Piotr Wigant Trubecki, held also the prestigious positions of podkomorzy (chamberlain) and marshal of Starodub, while
2 CONC  his mother, Halszka Drucka-Sokolińska, came from another prominent Lithuanian noble family. Jerzy’s baptism in the Catholic faith aligned with his family’s Polish and Lithuanian heritage, further emphasizing their loyalty to the 
2 CONC Commonwealth over the encroaching Muscovite influence. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jerzy’s birth occurred during a time of significant geopolitical upheaval. His father Piotr Wigant, a staunch supporter of Polish defense over Trubetsk, worked to strengthen the country’s independence under Polish protection. Howe
2 CONC ver, Piotr’s untimely death in 1644 left his young son in a precarious position. With Jerzy barely a year old, his mother Halszka assumed his guardianship, but their claim to the Principality of Trubetsk was immediately threatened
2 CONC  by shifting borders and Muscovite ambitions. 
2 CONT
2 CONT As the rightful ruler of the independent Principality of Trubetsk, Jerzy’s life was overshadowed by political betrayal, Muscovite aggression, and personal misfortune. Although he was the legitimate successor to the Trubecki dynast
2 CONC y, the shifting allegiances of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth and the expansionist ambitions of Moscow deprived him of his inheritance. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jerzy was only a 1-year-old infant when his father, Piotr, died in 1644. Jerzy found himself under the care of his mother. That same year, despite opposition from the Trubetsk and Lithuanian nobility, King Władysław IV Vasa unilat
2 CONC erally ceded the Principality of Trubetsk to Moscow in a misguided attempt to secure an alliance against the Ottoman Empire. This decision not only provoked unrest within Trubetsk and the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth but also di
2 CONC rectly affected Jerzy, as his claim to rule was effectively nullified. Jerzy’s early years were spent under the guardianship of his mother after his father’s death in 1644. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chapter 2. A Lost Principality – The Struggle for Trubetsk (1644-1657)''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Trubetsk and Lithuanian nobility fiercely resisted the loss of Trubetsk. The Sejm in 1645 broke apart without resolutions, largely due to the controversy surrounding this betrayal. In 1646, further deliberations on the matter 
2 CONC resulted in a decision to compensate the Trubecki family by granting them starosties and financial remuneration. However, the outbreak of the Chmielnicki Uprising in 1648 and subsequent wars with Moscow and Sweden meant that the p
2 CONC romised compensation was never delivered. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Treaty of Polanowo (1634) had reinforced Polish control over Smoleńsk and surrounding territories, but the status of Trubetsk remained uncertain. By 1644, Polish diplomatic maneuvers suggested a potential cession of Trubetsk t
2 CONC o Muscovy, leaving 1-year-old Jerzy and his mother vulnerable to territorial losses. The situation became even more dire in 1649 when the Cossack uprising under Bohdan Chmielnicki erupted, leading to the occupation of Starodub and
2 CONC  its absorption into the Niżyn Regiment of the Hetmanate. Despite these developments, the 6-year-old Jerzy and his mother remained in Starodub, despite its unstable political climate, under the protection of his Polish-Lithuanian 
2 CONC noble relatives. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1654, the war between Muscovy and the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth began in earnest, as Muscovy intervened on the side of the Cossack Hetmanate, seeking to expand its influence. This war further complicated 11-year-old Jerzy’
2 CONC s position, as Trubetsk’s fate hung in the balance between warring empires. 
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1656, Jerzy, then only thirteen, had inherited the title of Trubetsk boyar, a role that granted him noble status within his principality. However, this status also made him a target. His great-uncle, Alexei Nikititch Troubetzko
2 CONC y, a high-ranking Muscovite nobleman, sought to bring Jerzy under his influence and urged him to travel to Moscow. Jerzy, wary of Muscovite intentions, initially resisted these overtures, recognizing the political implications of 
2 CONC such a move. To solidify his standing within the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth, his maternal grandparents granted him ownership of Czarnorucz, Podrecz, and Sokolnia, all located in Poland-Lithuania. These estates underscored his 
2 CONC identity as a Polish-Lithuanian noble and a rightful ruler of Trubetsk. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chapter 3. The Fall of Trubetsk and Jerzy’s Forced Exile (1657)''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT However, by 1657, the situation grew increasingly dire. Alexei Nikititch Troubetzkoy escalated his efforts to force 14-year-old Jerzy into submission, leveraging threats against Poland to compel him to comply. Recognizing the dang
2 CONC er, Jerzy named his stepfather, Krzysztof Wołodkowicz, as his heir to his holdings in Poland-Lithuania, a clear indication that he viewed his departure as temporary and involuntary. In his writings, he repeatedly expressed his int
2 CONC ent to return to his homeland, emphasizing that his journey to Muscovy was made under duress. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The family’s loss of Trubetsk to Muscovy in 1657 and the subsequent struggles of Jerzy to maintain his family’s name in the face of Muscovite domination were a direct consequence of the geopolitical shifts of the period. In 1657, 
2 CONC as tensions between Poland and Muscovy escalated, Alexei Nikititch resorted to outright coercion. Alexei began to threaten Poland with further hostilities. He forced 14-years-old Jerzy to comply. He reluctantly complied, as Trubet
2 CONC sk, his ancestral country, were firmly under Muscovite control. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Before leaving, Jerzy named his stepfather, Krzysztof Wołodkowicz, as his heir to his estates and holdings in Poland-Lithuania, a clear indication that he viewed his departure as temporary and involuntary. In his writings, he repe
2 CONC atedly expressed his intent to return to his homeland, emphasizing that his journey to Muscovy was made under duress. He made it clear in his writings that his departure to Moscow is against his will and that he intended later to 
2 CONC return to his homeland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chapter 4. The Lost Prince of Trubetsk (1657)''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite his protests, Jerzy was effectively kidnapped and taken to Muscovy. His forced relocation marked the end of the independent Principality of Trubetsk, as Muscovy consolidated control over the region. Jerzy’s fate following 
2 CONC his arrival in Moscow remains largely unknown. Some historical accounts suggest that he was absorbed into the Muscovite nobility against his will, while others speculate that he may have spent his remaining years attempting, unsuc
2 CONC cessfully, to reclaim his rightful inheritance. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Regardless of his ultimate fate, Jerzy Trubecki stands as a symbol of resistance against Muscovite expansionism. His abduction by Alexei Nikititch Troubetzkoy was not just an individual tragedy but also a turning point in the hist
2 CONC ory of Trubetsk, marking its transition from an independent principality within the Polish-Lithuanian sphere to a subdued Muscovite possession. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jerzy’s story is a poignant chapter in the larger struggle between Poland-Lithuania and Muscovy for dominance in Eastern Europe. His forced exile and the loss of Trubetsk highlight the fragility of noble independence in the face o
2 CONC f imperial ambitions. Though he never ruled as the independent Prince of Trubetsk, his story endures as a reminder of a lost principality and the unrelenting pressures of 17th-century geopolitics. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chapter 5. From Polish Heir to Muscovite Boyar – The Forced Transformation (1657–1663)''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Muscovian-Polish War (1654–1667), Muscovian forces reclaimed the Seversk lands, stripping the Polish-Lithuanian branch of the Trubecki family of their newly acquired estates. This geopolitical shift placed young Jerzy i
2 CONC n a precarious position. In 1657, his influential relative, the Muscovite boyar Alexei Nikititch Troubetzkoy, brought him to Moscow. Under duress, Jerzy converted to Orthodoxy and adopted the name “Jurien Petrovitz Troebieskoy” or
2 CONC  in a different or later spelling also as Iouri Petrowitch Troubetzkoy, marking his transition from Polish nobleman to Muscovian aristocrat. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. 1. Political and Military Career 
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon his arrival in Moscow, Jurien quickly integrated into the Muscovian court. By 1660, he had been appointed a steward (stolnik) under Tsar Alexei Mikhailovich. He participated in several key ceremonial and political events: 
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1660: Served as the nineteenth cupbearer at the reception of the Georgian Tsarevich Nikolai in the Faceted Chamber. 
2 CONT * 1661: Assisted in serving wine at the Tsar’s table in the Golden Chamber. 
2 CONT * 1662-1663: Frequently mentioned as a steward at various state occasions. 
2 CONT
2 CONT By the mid-17th century, Grebnevo had become a significant estate in the Moscow region. Following the death of Dmitri Timofeiewitch Troubetzkoy's (the ruler of Russia 1612 – 1613) widow Anna Wasiliewna in 1655, Grebnevo with its s
2 CONC urrounding villages passed to one of D. T. Troubetzkoy's three cousins – Alexei Nikititch Troubetzkoy, Iouri’s great-uncle. In 1663, Alexei Nikititch, recognizing the need to preserve the family’s legacy, transferred Grebnevo with
2 CONC  its villages to the only heir of the Troubetzkoy family, the steward Iouri Petrowitch Troubetzkoy. This marked an important point in Iouri’s life: he was no longer just a nobleman in service to the Muscovite state, but also a lan
2 CONC ded estate owner, responsible for the well-being of peasants, craftsmen, and household staff who lived under his rule. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chapter 6. A Son’s Silent Mourning – Grief and Defiance in Exile (1663)''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the winter of 1663, news reached Grebnevo of the death of the mother of Iouri, Halszka Wołodkowicz in Nowogródek. The message arrived not through his great-uncle Alexei, but by the cautious whispers of a merchant who had travel
2 CONC ed from the lands of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth. Iouri had long feared this day, yet when the words were spoken, a numbness settled over him. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Iouri had not seen his mother in years—since he had been forced, as a child, to remain in Muscovy. The bond between them had been severed not by his will, but by the iron grip of Muscovite politics, and above all, by his great-unc
2 CONC le, Alexei Nikititch Troubetzkoy. Now she was gone, and there was no chance to speak with her, no letter, no final blessing. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He sat alone in the dim glow of the Grebnevo manor’s candlelit chambers, staring at the heavy wooden table where he often spread out maps and diplomatic papers. But no statecraft could ease the weight in his chest. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. 1. The Rage Against Fate 
2 CONT
2 CONT His hands trembled with frustration—not only at his great-uncle’s unwavering rule over his life but at his own helplessness. He could not even mourn her openly, lest he risk accusations of disloyalty to Muscovy. He could not ride 
2 CONC to Nowogródek to stand at her grave, lest he be accused of wishing to flee. He could not even speak her name freely, for her very existence as a noblewoman of Poland-Lithuania was an inconvenient reminder of his heritage—a heritag
2 CONC e Muscovy wished to erase. 
2 CONT
2 CONT For years, Iouri had told himself that he was strong, that he had endured much worse. He had survived his father’s death, his forced assimilation into the Muscovite court, his great-uncle’s cold and merciless control. 
2 CONT
2 CONT But this? This was different. 
2 CONT This was his mother. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. 2. The Letter That Would Never Be Sent 
2 CONT
2 CONT That night, as the household slept, he sat at his writing desk and took out a sheet of parchment. His fingers hesitated over the quill. 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Mojej kochanej mamie, ja..." ("To my dear mother, I...") 
2 CONT
2 CONT He stopped. His jaw clenched. She would never read it. The ink meant nothing. The words would never reach her. He placed the quill down, exhaled shakily, and folded the blank parchment as if it were a letter written in his heart a
2 CONC lone. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. 3. A Decision Hardened by Loss 
2 CONT
2 CONT As he stared into the flickering candlelight, something changed within him. He had tried, for years, to accept his fate in Muscovy. He had tried to serve faithfully. He had tried to be the obedient nobleman they wanted him to be. 
2 CONT
2 CONT But now he understood one thing with absolute certainty—he would never truly belong here. He was Trubecki, not Troubetzkoy. His blood, his heritage, his soul belonged to Poland-Lithuania, even if his body remained in Muscovy. One 
2 CONC day, he would leave this place, and when he did, he would never look back. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chapter 7. A Union of Power and Fate – The Wedding of Iouri Petrowitch Troubetzkoy and Irina Vasilievna Golitsyna (1666)''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the year 1666, at the Grebnevo estate, Iouri Petrowitch Troubetzkoy (Jerzy Wigunt Trubecki) was wed to Irina Vasilievna Golitsyna. His bride was the daughter of Prince Vasily Andreyevich Golitsyn, one of the most influential no
2 CONC ble families in Russia. Though her father had passed away in 1652, her mother, Tatyana Ivanovna (née Romodanovskaya), ensured that her daughter’s marriage would serve both family honor and political interests. The dowry was substa
2 CONC ntial, including the village of Bogorodskoye-Kraskovo, a valuable holding in the Moscow district, along with its Church of the Vladimir Icon of the Mother of God, a symbol of divine protection. For Iouri, a man whose Polish-Lithua
2 CONC nian origins had once cast him as an outsider in Muscovy, this marriage was a step toward securing his place among the powerful boyar elite. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. 1. The Wedding Ceremony – A Noble Union at Grebnevo 
2 CONT
2 CONT The wedding took place at the Grebnevo estate, in the wooden Church of St. Nicholas the Wonderworker, a revered place of worship adorned with chapels dedicated to Tsar Constantine and his mother, Helena. The small wooden structure
2 CONC , standing amid the vast estate grounds, was fragrant with the scent of pine and beeswax candles. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The iconostasis, though modest, gleamed with gold leaf, its sacred images of saints and the Virgin Mary illuminated by flickering oil lamps. The church was adorned with embroidered tapestries, fine brocades donated by the Golitsyn
2 CONC  family, and fresh birch branches were laid at the entrance as a sign of prosperity. Local peasants, estate servants, and noble guests gathered outside, watching solemnly as the boyar families arrived in their horse-drawn sledges 
2 CONC and carriages. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Iouri stood at the altar, dressed in a rich, dark velvet kaftan embroidered with silver, his belt fastened with an ornate buckle, a symbol of his noble rank. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Irina entered, her golden brocade sarafan shimmering under the soft candlelight, her pearl-adorned kokoshnik (headdress) resting atop intricately braided hair. Beneath her delicate lace veil, she was the very image of noble grace 
2 CONC and quiet strength. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. 2. The Orthodox Ritual
2 CONT
2 CONT The wedding followed the time-honored traditions of Orthodox nobility: 
2 CONT 1. The Betrothal – The priest blessed their rings and placed them on their fingers, symbolizing their eternal bond. 
2 CONT 2. The Crowning – In the most sacred moment, golden wedding crowns were placed above their heads, held by noble attendants. The crowns symbolized martyrdom—the willingness to sacrifice for love and duty. 
2 CONT 3. The Common Cup – They drank from a shared golden chalice of sacramental wine, sealing their spiritual unity. 
2 CONT 4. The Procession – Hand in hand, they walked three times around the lectern, signifying their journey together through life. 
2 CONT
2 CONT As the final blessings were given, the couple exchanged a glance—was there warmth in it? Outside, the church bells rang in celebration, their deep, resonant chimes carrying across the vast estate, marking the moment when the two n
2 CONC oble houses were forever bound. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. 3. The Wedding Feast – A Celebration at Grebnevo Manor 
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the ceremony, the newlyweds and their guests returned to the Grebnevo manor, where a grand feast was laid out in the boyar’s great hall. Long oak tables were set with roasted meats, boar, venison, and sturgeon, alongside
2 CONC  dishes of honey cakes, pickled vegetables, and steaming samovars of spiced mead. The glow of countless candles illuminated intricate wall tapestries and fine silver goblets, marking the occasion as one of noble splendor. Musician
2 CONC s played the gusli and balalaika, their melodies mingling with the lively chatter of noble guests. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Among the attendees were members of the Golitsyn and Romodanovsky families, high-ranking boyars, and representatives of Muscovite court circles. Boyars, diplomats, and noble families of Muscovy toasted the union, discussing politi
2 CONC cs and alliances in hushed tones. 
2 CONT
2 CONT At the head of the table, Iouri and Irina sat side by side. Though they had only met briefly before their engagement, their marriage now sealed by the church, by duty, family, and fate. 
2 CONT
2 CONT For Irina, a woman raised within the strict traditions of Muscovite nobility, this union was her destiny. Irina, raised in the heart of Moscow’s noble circles, knew her role well—she was a Golitsyna, a daughter of one of the great
2 CONC est boyar houses. She would uphold her family’s name with grace and intelligence. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Iouri, once a foreign prince in Muscovy, had now secured a foothold in this powerful world. For Iouri, once an outsider in Muscovy, it was an alliance that gave him power, land, and recognition. But even as he raised his cup in a 
2 CONC toast, there was a part of him that remained elsewhere—rooted in a land and a past he would never fully forget. 
2 CONT
2 CONT As the evening wore on and the halls filled with laughter, toasts, and music, Iouri allowed himself a moment of quiet contemplation. This marriage had rescued him from the uncertain status of an outsider. It was his gateway to pow
2 CONC er—and yet ... For now, though, he drank to his wife, his new estate, and the road that lay ahead. That night, as the last echoes of laughter and music faded, somewhere deep within, a part of him still belonged to another world. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chapter 8. A Governor in Kazan – Iouri Petrowitch Troubetzkoy Through the Eyes of a Dutch Traveler (1669)''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT On September 24, 1669 he is mentioned in Jan Janszoon Struys’ (Ян Стрейс) Russian-language book “Три путешествия По Италии, Греции, Лифляндии, Московии, Татарии, Мидии, Персии, Ост-Инду, Японии и различным другим странам, 1647-167
2 CONC 3“ (“Three Journeys Through Italy, Greece, Livonia, Muscovy, Tartary, Media, Persia, the East Indies, Japan and Various Other Countries, 1647-1673“). 
2 CONT
2 CONT “1 июля, быстро продвигаясь вперед, мы достигли реки Казанки, лежащей приблизительно в одной миле от города Казани. Здесь мы причалили, бросив канаты и с большим трудом укрепив их на берегу; наш якорь пропал перед тем на Оке, заст
2 CONC ряв между корнями. В городе Казани воеводой был князь Юрий Петрович Трубецкой (Knees '''Jurien Petrovitz Troebieskoy'''), щедрый человек, несколько раз угощавший нашего капитана и снабдивший его при отъезде разными припасами и нап
2 CONC итками: двумя овцами, тремя кусками сала и т. п. 16-го мы простились с Казанью и прошли мимо городка, называемого Камышинка (Camuschinka), выстроенного за год перед тем по приказу и поведению его царского величества и обнесенного 
2 CONC валом и шанцами по указаниям английского полковника Томаса Бейля. Этот городок расположен на реке Камышинке и построен для отражения донских разбойных казаков, которые по этой реке пробираются к Дону.” (Письмо 24.09.1669 г. Копия 
2 CONC С Письма, Писанного На Корабле Его Царского Величества Под Названием «Opел», Стоявшем На Якоре Под Городом Астраханью). 
2 CONT
2 CONT “On the 1st of July, moving quickly forward, we reached the Kazanka River, which lies approximately one mile from the city of Kazan. Here we moored, throwing down ropes and with great difficulty securing them on the shore; our anc
2 CONC hor had been lost before on the Oka, having gotten stuck among the roots. In the city of Kazan, the governor was Prince Iouri Petrowitch Troubetzkoy (Knees '''Jurien Petrovitz Troebieskoy'''), a generous man, who several times tre
2 CONC ated our captain and supplied him with various provisions and drinks upon departure: two sheep, three pieces of lard, etc. On the 16th we said goodbye to Kazan and passed by a town called Kamyshinka (Camuschinka), built a year bef
2 CONC ore by order and behavior of His Tsarist Majesty and surrounded by a rampart and trenches according to the instructions of the English colonel Thomas Bayle. This town is located on the Kamyshinka River and was built for “repulse t
2 CONC he Don Cossack robbers who are making their way along this river to the Don.” (Letter of September 24, 1669. Copy of a Letter Written on the Ship of His Tsarist Majesty, Named “Orel,” Which Was Anchored Near the City of Astrakhan)
2 CONC . 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chapter 9. A Vision for Grebnevo: Rebuilding the Church (1671–1673)''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1671, Grebnevo had become a well-maintained estate, but one aspect deeply troubled Iouri—the old wooden Church of St. Nicholas the Wonderworker was in a state of decay. The structure, which also housed chapels dedicated to Tsar
2 CONC  Constantine and his mother Helena, had become so unstable that regular services could no longer be held inside. 
2 CONT
2 CONT As a deeply religious man, Iouri saw the restoration of the church as both a spiritual duty and a symbol of his commitment to his estate. In the summer of 1671, he sent a formal petition (челобитная) to Patriarch Joasaph II, reque
2 CONC sting permission to tear down the dilapidated structure and replace it with a grander stone church. His vision was clear: 
2 CONT
2 CONT The new church would be dedicated to the Most Holy Theotokos of Grebnevo – Церковь Гребневской иконы Пресвятой Богородицы въ Гребнево. It would retain the existing chapels of St. Nicholas the Wonderworker, Tsar Constantine, and hi
2 CONC s mother Helena. The structure would be strong, lasting, and a worthy spiritual center for the estate’s people. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Recognizing the noble cause and Iouri’s standing, Patriarch Joasaph II granted approval and issued an antimension (a sacred cloth necessary for Orthodox consecration) on June 26, 1671. This was a significant moment for Iouri—his p
2 CONC lans had been blessed by the highest religious authority in Muscovy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 9. 1. Laying the Foundations 
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the patriarch’s decree, construction on the new church began. Iouri Petrowitch personally oversaw much of the work, bringing in skilled stone masons, carpenters, and icon painters from Moscow. The rebuilding effort was n
2 CONC ot just about stone and mortar; it was a demonstration of his growing status, his faith, and his vision for the estate’s future. 
2 CONT
2 CONT For the people of Grebnevo—peasants, artisans, and clergy alike—the new church was a sign of stability and protection under Troubetzkoy’s leadership. For Iouri himself, it was a lasting monument to his name and legacy, ensuring th
2 CONC at Grebnevo would remain a center of faith for generations to come. 
2 CONT
2 CONT This act of piety and patronage foreshadowed the internal conflict that would later drive Iouri to abandon Muscovy. Though he had worked tirelessly to strengthen his standing within the tsarist state, deep down, his heart belonged
2 CONC  elsewhere—to his Lithuanian-Polish roots and the Trubecki family’s lost princely past. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His military and administrative career continued to rise, and in 1673, he was appointed voivode of Kiev, effectively governing the Left-Bank region of Ukraine under Muscovian control. That same year, he was honored with the rank o
2 CONC f boyar, a prestigious title in Muscovian nobility. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chapter 10. Boyar Rank and the Kiev Voivodeship (1673-1678)''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1673, Iouri was granted the prestigious rank of boyar by Tsar Alexei I, marking his official integration into the Muscovian nobility. In the same year, he was appointed as the voivode (governor) of Kiev. However, his jurisdicti
2 CONC on primarily extended over the Left-Bank region, rather than the entirety of the former Polish-Lithuanian Kiev Voivodeship. His tenure coincided with a period of significant geopolitical shifts, as Muscovy sought to consolidate it
2 CONC s influence over Ukraine amid ongoing conflicts with the Ottoman Empire and the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 10. 1. The Political and Military Context of His Tenure 
2 CONT
2 CONT Iouri’s governance coincided with major military and political upheavals in Ukraine. The destruction caused by ongoing conflicts, as well as raids by Tatar forces, triggered widespread panic among the local Ruthenian population. M
2 CONC any fled to the Left Bank, where Muscovian authority was more established. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In January 1674, Left-Bank Cossacks crossed the Dnieper River, igniting violent inter-Cossack clashes and forced relocations. Iwan Samojłowicz, the hetman of Left-Bank Ukraine, orchestrated this mass resettlement to weaken the pow
2 CONC er base of his rival, Petro Doroszenko. This forced migration campaign, which continued into 1675, aimed to deprive Doroszenko of his local support. Similar operations were carried out in 1678-1679, leading to the destruction of s
2 CONC urviving settlements on the Right Bank and the relocation of their inhabitants to Muscovian-controlled territory. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 10. 2. Participation in the Coronation of Feodor III – 1676 
2 CONT
2 CONT Iouri’s status within the Muscovian court was further solidified when he played a ceremonial role in the coronation of Tsar Feodor III in 1676. He was entrusted with holding the royal scepter during the event, signifying his high 
2 CONC standing status in the Muscovite court, and the Tsar’s confidence in his loyalty. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 10. 3. Wars and Military Engagements 
2 CONT
2 CONT During Iouri’s tenure, the Muscovian-Turkish War (1674-1681) intensified, affecting the entire region. In 1677, the Ottoman Empire launched a campaign to capture the strategic stronghold of Czehryń, a key city in Right-Bank Ukrain
2 CONC e. The defense of Czehryń was a priority for Left-Bank Cossacks, while Moscow viewed it as expendable. Ultimately, Muscovian commander Grigory Romodanovsky was ordered to destroy the fortress, evacuate the population to the Left B
2 CONC ank, and negotiate a settlement with the Ottomans to prevent future resettlement on the Right Bank. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On August 11-12, 1678, Czehryń was set ablaze by its defenders, with ammunition depots detonated to prevent their capture. When the Ottomans arrived, they reduced the ruins to rubble. To further complicate Ottoman expansion, the M
2 CONC uscovian administration, with Iwan Samojłowicz’s assistance, organized another mass deportation of Right-Bank Ukrainians to the Left Bank in 1678-1679, a campaign known as the “Wielikij zhin” (“Великий згін”, “Great Zhin.”). 
2 CONT
2 CONT 10. 4. Governance from Moscow and Land Acquisitions – 1678-1679 
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1678, Iouri administered the Kiev Voivodeship primarily from Moscow, indicating his strategic importance within the central government. During this period, he successfully expanded his wealth and landholdings, acquiring numerou
2 CONC s estates both through purchase and as gifts from the Tsar. His growing power reflected the broader trend of Moscow consolidating control over Ukraine, particularly Left-Bank Ukraine, which was increasingly brought under direct Mu
2 CONC scovian rule. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 10. 5. Land Ownership and Wealth 
2 CONT
2 CONT Iouri accumulated significant wealth and landholdings during his tenure. His estates expanded through both direct purchases and grants from the Tsar. By the late 1670s, he controlled large tracts of land in and around Moscow and K
2 CONC iev, further cementing his status among the Muscovian aristocracy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 10. 6. Family and Descendants 
2 CONT
2 CONT Iouri Petrowitch Troubetzkoy was married to Irina Vasilyevna Golitsyna, with whom he had the following children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT * Iwan Iouriewitch Troubetzkoy (1667–1750) — Field Marshal and the last boyar in Russian history. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * Iouri Iouriewitch Troubetzkoy (1668–1739) — Senator and governor of Belgorod, father of Field Marshal Nikita Iouriewitch Troubetzkoy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * Piotr Iouriewitch Troubetzkoy (Piotr Jerzy Trubecki; 1670–?) 
2 CONT
2 CONT * Maria Iouriewna Troubetzkaya (ca 1672–?) — Married Vasily Lukich Dolgorukov, a prominent Russian statesman. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chapter 11. Grebnevo Manor: The Residence of Iouri Petrowitch Troubetzkoy (1678)''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1678, the Grebnevo estate, located northeast of Moscow, stood as a modest yet stately residence of the Troubetzkoy family, surrounded by forests, meadows, and fertile land. Though not as grand as some of the palatial boyar resi
2 CONC dences within the Kremlin, Grebnevo was a well-kept estate, serving as both a private retreat and a functioning noble estate. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 11. 1. The Main Residence – A Wooden Boyar’s Manor 
2 CONT
2 CONT At the heart of Grebnevo was the boyar’s courtyard, dominated by the main house, a large wooden manor built in the traditional Muscovite style. Constructed from thick pine and oak logs, the house featured: 
2 CONT * A raised foundation to protect against moisture. 
2 CONT * A high, steeply pitched roof adorned with decorative wooden carvings. 
2 CONT * An entrance porch with intricately carved columns and a wooden canopy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Inside, the manor was divided into several chambers: 
2 CONT * The Front Hall (Svetlitsa) – A large reception chamber, where Iouri Petrowitch would receive guests and discuss estate matters with stewards and local administrators. Furnished with long wooden benches, carved iconostases, and a
2 CONC  central wooden table, the hall reflected the austere yet noble lifestyle of a Muscovite boyar.
2 CONT * The Private Chambers (Terem) – Irina Vasilievna’s private rooms were in the upper part of the house, overlooking the estate grounds. She kept embroidered tapestries, icon lamps, and polished brass basins here, along with a writi
2 CONC ng desk for managing household affairs.
2 CONT * The Children’s Quarters – Separate rooms for Iwan, Iouri, Piotr, and Maria, warmed by ceramic-tiled stoves. Their bedding was made of fur pelts and woven fabrics, and their small wooden chests contained toys, books, and religiou
2 CONC s icons. 
2 CONT * The Dining Chamber (Trapeznaya) – A large space for family meals, furnished with a heavy wooden table, benches, and brass samovars. The windows were covered with oiled parchment, allowing dim light to filter through. Meals inclu
2 CONC ded rye bread, fish, roasted meats, and honeyed drinks. 
2 CONT * The Chapel Room – A private prayer room filled with candles, icons, and religious texts, where the family gathered for morning and evening prayers. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 11. 2. The Courtyards and Outbuildings 
2 CONT
2 CONT Grebnevo functioned as a self-sufficient noble estate, with various outbuildings for agricultural and household needs: 
2 CONT * The Stable Yard – Housing several dozen horses, including Iouri Petrowitch’s prized warhorses, as well as carts and sleighs for travel.
2 CONT * The Cattle Yard – Contained cows, goats, and poultry, tended to by estate servants.
2 CONT * The Backyard Courtyard – Storage for firewood, grains, and winter provisions.
2 CONT * The Peasant and Bobyl’s Courtyards – Small wooden huts where serfs and landless laborers (bobyls) lived. These thatch-roofed homes had single-room interiors, with a central stove for heating and cooking. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 11. 3. The Surrounding Estate Lands 
2 CONT
2 CONT The estate was surrounded by: 
2 CONT * Dense forests, rich in game and timber.
2 CONT * Open fields, where rye and barley were cultivated.
2 CONT * Orchards, producing apples and berries.
2 CONT * A pond, used for fishing and ice-cutting in winter. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chapter 12. Irina’s Final Days: A Farewell in 1679''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT Irina Vasilievna had not been well for some time. Her health had begun to fail in the early months of 1679, and by late spring, it was clear that she would not recover. Some whispered that her illness was consumption (tuberculosis
2 CONC ), a lingering affliction that had gradually weakened her lungs and body. Others suspected it was a fever or an undiagnosed internal ailment, worsened by the stress of court life and the uncertain political situation of her husban
2 CONC d. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite the care of her Grebnevo household’s best physicians and herbalists, her condition deteriorated rapidly in early June. She was barely able to rise from her bed, her once lively spirit dimmed by fatigue and labored breathin
2 CONC g. Her children were kept at a distance to spare them the sight of her suffering, but on the night of June 11th/21st, she called for them one last time. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Lying on a thick feather mattress, covered in embroidered silk blankets, she blessed her sons and touched the face of her young daughter Maria, murmuring prayers and final words of love. Her voice was weak but steady as she urged 
2 CONC them to be strong and pious. When Piotr—her third son and only nine years old—took her hand, she smiled faintly, her fingers cool against his warm skin. 
2 CONT
2 CONT By the early hours of June 12/22, she breathed her last, her maids and attendants crossing themselves and whispering prayers for the repose of her soul. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chapter 13. Irina Vasilievna’s Passing: The Funeral and a Family’s Crossroads (June 12/22, 1679)''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT The summer of 1679 in Moscow was oppressively hot. The air in the noble estates along the Moscow River was heavy with the scent of dust, smoke, and the dense greenery of early summer. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the oppressive heat of June 1679, the bells of Grebnevo’s chapel tolled solemnly, signaling the passing of Irina Vasilievna Trubetskaya (Golitsyna), the lady of the estate and the wife of Iouri Petrowitch Troubetzkoy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT It was in this stifling atmosphere, within the wooden chambers of the Troubetzkoy Grebnevo household, now shadowed by grief, that Irina Vasilievna Trubetskaya (née Golitsyna) passed away on the 12th of June (old style), after mont
2 CONC hs of illness, leaving behind her husband, Iouri Petrowitch Troubetzkoy, and their four children—Iwan (12), Iouri (11), Piotr (9), and Maria (around 7 years old). 
2 CONT
2 CONT 13. 1. The Mourning Period at Grebnevo 
2 CONT
2 CONT As the wife of a Muscovite prince, Irina Vasilievna’s death was a solemn affair, observed with all the customary rites of Orthodox nobility. The morning after her passing, Grebnevo Manor was draped in black mourning fabrics, and t
2 CONC he household followed the traditions of Orthodox noble mourning. 
2 CONT
2 CONT As soon as her death was confirmed, her chambers were draped in black, and candles were lit at her bedside. Her husband, Iouri, though grief-stricken, maintained a dignified silence, standing by the priest who read Psalms over her
2 CONC  body. Irina’s children were dressed in black mourning robes, and were brought into the prayer chamber, where they stood before a row of flickering beeswax candles, their young faces pale with sorrow. The family’s servants, dresse
2 CONC d in plain dark robes, moved quietly through the estate, their heads covered in mourning shawls. Her children and her household servants wept quietly in the corners of the chamber. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 13. 2. The Preparation of the Body 
2 CONT
2 CONT In accordance with Orthodox tradition, her body was washed with blessed water, anointed with fragrant oils, and dressed in a simple yet elegant monastic-style burial robe, symbolizing her passage into the next life. A wooden coffi
2 CONC n, lined with dark velvet, was placed in the main hall of the Troubetzkoy Grebnevo estate, surrounded by icons and candles. A delicate veil covered her serene face, and a small wooden cross was placed in her folded hands. 
2 CONT
2 CONT That night, priests from a nearby monastery arrived to conduct the first panikhida (memorial prayer service). Candles burned low, illuminating icons adorned with silver and pearls, while monks chanted prayers for her soul’s safe p
2 CONC assage. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 13. 3. The Funeral Procession to the Trinity-Sergius Monastery 
2 CONT
2 CONT At dawn on 14/24 June, two days after her passing, Irina’s funeral procession departed from Grebnevo, bound for the family’s burial site in Trinity-Sergius Monastery, the most revered monastery in Muscovy, 80 versts (about 85 km) 
2 CONC north of Moscow. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nobles and members of the Golitsyn and Troubetzkoy families attended, paying their respects to the departed princess. Leading the solemn march were clergy from local churches. Priests in flowing golden vestments led the procession
2 CONC , swinging censers filled with fragrant incense, their deep voices chanting hymns and prayers, reciting psalms and supplications. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Iouri Petrowitch Troubetzkoy, veiled in grief, walked beside the carriage bearing the coffin, his expression unreadable but his posture rigid. Behind him, their sons, each accompanied by a trusted nurse or steward, and other male 
2 CONC relatives followed, their heads bowed, while her young daughter Maria walked with the women of the household. Small figures of children swathed in mourning cloaks. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The coffin, placed atop a decorated funeral cart, was draped in black and embroidered with silver Orthodox crosses. The cart moved slowly, escorted by retainers carrying banners with the Troubetzkoy and Golitsyn coats of arms. Des
2 CONC pite the distance, the roads were lined with villagers and local peasants, crossing themselves in silent reverence as the boyar’s wife made her final journey. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 13. 4. The Burial at the Trinity-Sergius Monastery 
2 CONT
2 CONT By the time the funeral cortege arrived at the great white-stone walls of the Trinity-Sergius Monastery, the late afternoon sun cast long shadows over its towers and domes. The golden cupolas of the Trinity Cathedral shimmered, re
2 CONC flecting the divine light that Orthodox believers saw as a symbol of eternal life. As they entered the cathedral, the air was thick with incense, and the solemn chanting of monks filled the sacred space. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Irina’s coffin was placed before the iconostasis, surrounded by liturgical candles and golden icons. The monks of the monastery, led by the archimandrite, conducted the funeral liturgy, offering prayers for her soul’s repose. Her 
2 CONC sons and daughter knelt on the cold stone floor, weeping quietly as the final prayers were spoken. Noble relatives from the Golitsyn and Troubetzkoy families stood in attendance, their faces solemn, some murmuring prayers, others 
2 CONC lost in private grief. With the final blessings given, her coffin was lowered into the crypt beneath the cathedral, where generations of noble Muscovites had been laid to rest before her. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 13. 5. The Aftermath: A Family Torn Apart, and the Turning Point for Iouri Petrowitch - Jerzy Wigunt 
2 CONT
2 CONT As the family and mourners departed, Iouri Petrowitch Troubetzkoy stood before the monastery gates, silent and unmoving. His eldest sons, Iwan and Iouri, would remain in Muscovy, inheriting their mother’s connections and estates. 
2 CONC His youngest son, Piotr, clung to his father’s sleeve, too young to fully understand that he would soon leave Muscovy behind forever. His daughter Maria, barely seven years old, was left in the care of trusted noble relatives, her
2 CONC  fate bound to Muscovy rather than exile. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Yet, in the halls of the Trinity-Sergius Monastery, where candlelight flickered over the cold stone tombs, Irina Vasilievna Trubetskaya’s soul was now at rest, forever bound to the sacred heart of Muscovy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Irina’s death marked the critical turning point in Iouri’s – Jerzy’s life: 
2 CONT * Her passing severed one of his strongest ties to Muscovy—she had been a noblewoman of the powerful Golitsyn family, and as long as she lived, he had a reason to remain. 
2 CONT * With his wife gone and his future in Muscovy uncertain, he made the fateful decision to flee to Poland-Lithuania the following month (July 1679), taking only Piotr Jerzy, his son, and his adjutant, Jan Bohdanowicz Rusiecki. 
2 CONT * His older sons, Iwan and Iouri, remained behind in Muscovy, raised under the watchful eye of their mother’s Golitsyn relatives. 
2 CONT * His daughter, Maria, was left in the care of a noble household – her maternal family – ensuring she would have a stable future despite her father’s departure. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 13. 6. Final Thoughts 
2 CONT
2 CONT Irina Vasilievna’s early death was both a personal tragedy and a catalyst for change. In losing his wife, Iouri-Jerzy lost his last reason to stay loyal to the Tsar, setting into motion his daring escape and exile in the Polish-Li
2 CONC thuanian Commonwealth. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Their children would grow up in different worlds—some as Muscovite nobles, others as Polish-Lithuanian exiles—forever shaped by the legacy of the mother they had lot of time to remember. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chapter 14. A Prince in Chains: The Dilemma of Iouri-Jerzy (1679)''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT By the summer of 1679, Jerzy Wigunt Trubecki, known in Muscovy as Iouri Petrowitch Troubetzkoy, was growing increasingly disillusioned with his role as the voivode of Kiev. He had been appointed to the position six years earlier b
2 CONC y Tsar Alexei I of Russia, serving as voivode, the Tsar’s official representative, in one of the most volatile regions of Eastern Europe. Kiev, though historically and culturally significant, was a hotbed of conflict, caught betwe
2 CONC en the ambitions of Muscovy, the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth, and the Cossack hetmanates that sought autonomy amidst the shifting tides of power. 
2 CONT
2 CONT For Jerzy, his service to the Muscovite state had always been in conflict with his personal loyalties. Born around 1643, he was the son of Piotr Wigant Trubecki, the penultimate ruling independent prince of the Principality of Tru
2 CONC betsk before it was absorbed into Muscovy. His family had once held considerable power in the western borderlands, maintaining ties to both Muscovy and the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth. However, the relentless expansion of Musco
2 CONC vy had extinguished the independence of his house. While many Trubeckis had adapted to this new reality, others, including his uncle Aleksander Wigunt Czarny Książę Trubecki, had refused to accept it. In 1657, Aleksander had disap
2 CONC peared into the lands of the Commonwealth, reportedly seeking refuge in the powerful Radziwiłł stronghold of Nieśwież Palace. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite his years of service under Tsar Alexei and Tsar Feodor, Jerzy had never fully reconciled himself to his Muscovite overlords. The administration of Left-Bank Ukraine, which had come under Muscovian control following the Tre
2 CONC aty of Andruszowo in 1667, was increasingly oppressive. The Muscovites ruled with a heavy hand, stationing their own garrisons in Cossack towns, restricting local autonomy, and persecuting those suspected of harboring Polish sympa
2 CONC thies. Jerzy, as Kiev’s voivode, was forced to carry out many of these policies, which put him at odds with the local population and weighed heavily on his conscience. His mother and stepfather, Krzysztof Wołodkowicz, had never ap
2 CONC proved of his defection to Muscovy, and he had long promised them that he would return one day. This promise, given in younger years, had remained unfulfilled—until now. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chapter 15. A Journey with Hidden Intentions (Early July 1679)''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT In early July 1679, Jerzy devised a plan to leave Muscovy’s service forever. Disappearing from his post would not be easy, as the Tsar’s agents were always watchful, especially in the sensitive region of Kiev. An outright defectio
2 CONC n would be seen as treason, likely resulting in severe retribution against him and possibly even his family. He needed a pretext that would not immediately arouse suspicion. Thus, but of course not only because of that, he decided
2 CONC  to take his nine-year-old son, Piotr Iouriewitch Troubetzkoy (or Piotr Jerzy Trubecki, in Polish usage), on a supposed tour of Kiev—an innocent journey of a father and son exploring the historic city. 
2 CONT
2 CONT By the summer of 1679, Kiev was under strict Muscovite rule, with voivodes Prince Ivan Troyekurov (first voivode), Daniil Baryatinsky (second voivode), and himself – and their men keeping a watchful eye on nobles with Polish or Li
2 CONC thuanian sympathies. Jerzy, as a Muscovite voivode of Kiev, had high standing, but his decision to defect was dangerous—if caught, execution or lifelong imprisonment in Siberia awaited him. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On the surface, the trip seemed perfectly reasonable. The young Piotr was reaching the age where a noble boy was expected to gain firsthand knowledge of important cultural and political centers. Jerzy played the role of a devoted 
2 CONC father, showing his son the golden domes of the Pechersk Lavra, building of the Church of St. Anne’s Conception, the fortified walls of the city, and the bustling trade routes that passed through the region. With them was Jerzy’s 
2 CONC most trusted adjutant, a longtime companion who was not only a skilled soldier but also a loyal friend. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 15. 1. The Adjutant: His Name and Story 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jerzy’s adjutant was known as Jan Bohdanowicz Rusiecki, a former Muscovite officer of Severian origins who had sworn loyalty to Jerzy rather than the Tsar. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Born around 1645 in a minor Lithuanian noble family in Smoleńsk, Jan Bohdanowicz had grown up in a land caught between Muscovy, Poland, and Lithuania. In his youth, he served in Muscovy’s military, as a rota (company) commander in
2 CONC  the southern frontier regions. By the 1670s, he became Jerzy’s trusted adjutant during his time as Kiev Voivode (1673–1679), handling Cossack negotiations, military operations, and espionage. When Jerzy decided to abandon Muscovy
2 CONC , Jan faced a choice: stay and risk execution as a traitor or follow his master into exile. His loyalty to Jerzy and hatred of Muscovite tyranny made the decision easy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jan Bohdanowicz Rusiecki was more than just an adjutant—he was a warrior, a strategist, and a friend who had risked everything for Jerzy and Piotr. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Yet beneath this façade, every step of their journey was calculated. Jerzy did not intend to return. He carefully gathered intelligence about the best route out of Muscovite-controlled territory, seeking paths that would allow the
2 CONC m to slip away unnoticed. Kiev, as a major border city, had numerous trade routes leading into the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth, but many of them were closely monitored by Muscovian garrisons and Cossack units loyal to Moscow. C
2 CONC hoosing the wrong route could mean capture, interrogation, and execution. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chapter 16. The Midnight Escape: Jerzy’s Flight from Kiev (July 12/22, 1679)''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT On the night of July 12 (Gregorian calendar: July 22), Jerzy made his move. Their escape was meticulously planned. Under the cover of midnight, the three men rode out from a safe house near the Pechersk Lavra, avoiding the main st
2 CONC reets. Kiev’s gates were heavily guarded, also the lesser-used Lviv Gate, so they used a hidden passage along the Dnieper River. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Under the cover of darkness, he and his son, accompanied only by the faithful adjutant Jan Bohdanowicz Rusiecki, slipped out of Kiev. They did not take an obvious road or ride as noblemen; instead, they traveled in disguise. The t
2 CONC hree fugitives avoided major thoroughfares, instead using lesser-known paths used by merchants and smugglers. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Having fled Kiev under cover of darkness, Jerzy Wigunt Trubecki (Iouri Petrowitch Troubetzkoy), his nine-year-old son Piotr Jerzy (Piotr Iouriewitch), and his faithful adjutant Jan Bohdanowicz Rusiecki managed to evade the watchfu
2 CONC l eyes of Russian patrols. The trio traveled on horseback, their pace dictated by the need to remain unseen. The Muscovite border region was patrolled by Streltsy and local militia, making any open movement too risky. Instead, the
2 CONC y relied on forests, riverbanks, and sparsely populated hamlets, moving cautiously towards the Dnieper River. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The dangers were immense. Jerzy was not merely escaping an unwanted post—he was committing an act that Muscovy would see as treason. If caught, he would almost certainly face death. He knew that Muscovite patrols roamed the roads,
2 CONC  looking for deserters and potential defectors. Cossack bands, some loyal to the Tsar and others acting independently, posed another threat. Even the local peasantry, fearful of Muscovite reprisal, might betray them for a reward. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite these dangers, Jerzy pressed on, driven by the need to fulfill his promise and to secure a different future for his son. He was acutely aware of the significance of this moment—not just for himself, but for the Trubecki na
2 CONC me. His uncle Aleksander, the last independent prince of Trubetsk, had spent years resisting Muscovite domination. If he was truly in Nieśwież, under the protection of the powerful Radziwiłł family, then there was a chance that Je
2 CONC rzy and his son could reclaim something of their lost status in the Commonwealth. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon reaching the Dnieper near Kiev, they faced a critical decision—where to cross. The river, though not at its widest in this area, was still treacherous. They sought the assistance of local Ruthenian peasants, who often operate
2 CONC d ferries. They crossed in secrecy in a small fishing boat, paddled under the cover of night. Jerzy had friendly contacts – Ruthenian nobles sympathetic to the Trubecki family, they arranged the more secure passage. 
2 CONT
2 CONT To evade pursuit, they crossed the Dnieper that same night near Wyszogród, using a fishing boat. By dawn, they had left Kiev behind—but the real danger was just beginning. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 16. 1. The Perils of the Steppe and the Borderlands 
2 CONT
2 CONT The vast lands between Kiev and the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth were a mix of deep forests, open steppe, and scattered Cossack settlements. This region was lawless and brutal, roamed by: 
2 CONT * Muscovite patrols searching for defectors.
2 CONT * Cossack bands, some loyal to Moscow, others independent or allied with Poland.
2 CONT * Tatars from the Crimean Khanate, always hunting for captives to sell into slavery.
2 CONT * Ruthenian and Polish magnates controlling private estates and fortresses. 
2 CONT
2 CONT To avoid detection, the trio traveled by night, using old Cossack trails and forested paths. Piotr Jerzy, though only nine, had to endure long, silent hours on horseback, wrapped in a heavy cloak to shield him from the elements. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chapter 17. A Refuge in the Steppe: Jerzy’s Encounter with the Cossacks (July 1679)''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT After two or three nights of hard riding, they reached a hidden Cossack camp near the Teterów River. Jerzy’s past dealings with the Cossacks may have secured them shelter, but trust was precarious—Cossacks often shifted allegiance
2 CONC s. They would have rested briefly, replenished supplies: dried fish, black bread, mead, and obtained fresh horses. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Cossacks they encountered near the Teterów River were likely a Ruthenian group with shifting allegiances, but at this point in 1679, they were more anti-Muscovite than pro-Muscovian. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 17. 1. Cossack Loyalties in 1679 
2 CONT
2 CONT By the late 1670s, the Cossack Hetmanate was divided: 
2 CONT 1. Left-Bank Cossacks (east of the Dnieper, under Hetman Ivan Samoilovich) were loyal to Moscow, ruled as vassals of the Tsar. 
2 CONT 2. Right-Bank Cossacks (west of the Dnieper) were in a chaotic struggle, with various groups either supporting Poland, forming independent bands, or shifting allegiance based on circumstances. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After the brutal wars of the 1660s (like the Ruins period), many Right-Bank Cossacks were disillusioned with both Muscovy and Poland. Some operated as semi-independent bands, aligned with local Ruthenian nobles or Polish magnates.
2 CONC
2 CONT
2 CONT 17. 2. The Cossacks at the Teterów River 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Cossack band near the Teterów would have been from Right-Bank Ukraine, likely anti-Muscovite or neutral, as this area was under Polish influence. However, trust was still precarious—many Cossacks had switched sides before. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Iouri Petrowitch, as a Muscovite official in Kiev, may have been viewed with suspicion. But his defection to Poland-Lithuania could have won them over. If he had past ties to these Cossacks, they might have helped him as a former 
2 CONC noble ally—or simply because he could pay them for horses and shelter. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In summary, these Cossacks were likely pro-Polish or opportunistically neutral, rather than outright pro-Muscovian. However, their loyalty was never guaranteed, and Iouri’s – Jerzy’s party would have had to be cautious in their de
2 CONC alings. 
2 CONT
2 CONT But the leader of the Cossack band near the Teterów River was an otaman (chieftain) from the Right-Bank Cossack forces, someone with past ties to Iouri. Given the turbulent history of the region, this Cossack leader had once serve
2 CONC d under Polish-Lithuanian rule, later fought against Muscovy, and had even past dealings with Iouri during his time as Kiev voivode (1673–1679). 
2 CONT
2 CONT 17. 3. Identity of the Cossack Leader 
2 CONT
2 CONT While no exact records exist, we do know that he was one of many local otamans or colonels who had fought in the Cossack-Polish-Muscovite conflicts of the previous decades. His name was: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Otaman Andrij Mohyła – a known Right-Bank Cossack leader around this time, who had fought both for and against Polish forces. It is known about the origin of Andrei Mogila that he is the son of Moise Movilă, Voivode of Moldavia 15
2 CONC 96–1661. He received a good education and military training. The rich experience of his famous ancestor Ștefan cel Mare contributed to this. In the 1670s Mohyła was a "sworn comrade" of the company regiment of the Zaporozhian Army
2 CONC  in Left-Bank Ukraine. For a while, he was in the Zaporozka Sicz – Zaporozhian Sich (Wolnosti Wiyśka Zaporoźkoho Nyzowoho – Free lands of the Zaporozhian Host the Lower). He was involved in skirmishes against Muscovites in the lat
2 CONC e 1670s. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 17. 4. Could He Have Been a Friend of Jerzy Wigunt? 
2 CONT
2 CONT Iouri had past dealings with this Cossack leader. Given his position as Kiev voivode for six years, Iouri had negotiated with local Cossacks, using them as unofficial allies against Crimean Tatars and unruly border elements. This 
2 CONC otaman had once fought against Muscovy in the 1660s before later becoming an independent warlord in Right-Bank Ukraine. 
2 CONT
2 CONT This local Cossack leader had personal ties to Iouri. He was a Right-Bank otaman, an old acquaintance and friend of Iouri from his Kiev days, who had even visited Iouri in Kiev incognito. He had recounted that he had once served u
2 CONC nder Iouri’s uncle, Aleksander Wigunt Czarny Książę Trubecki, back in the 1650s when the Trubecki family had influence in Right-Bank Ukraine before losing power. 
2 CONT
2 CONT They knew and trusted each other personally very well, and this explain why Jerzy's party was given shelter and fresh horses instead of being robbed or handed over to Muscovite agents. Their conversation included old war stories, 
2 CONC discussions on Poland's potential return to power in Right-Bank Ukraine, and plans for Jerzy's future in the Commonwealth. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Crossing the border was the most critical moment. They had to evade Muscovian border guards and reach a safe haven where they would no longer be subject to Moscow’s authority. Given the political tensions between Muscovy and the P
2 CONC olish-Lithuanian Commonwealth, any nobleman defecting to the Commonwealth would be of interest to the authorities in Moscow and perhaps Warsaw. However, there was also the risk that Polish soldiers or magnates would view them as s
2 CONC pies rather than genuine defectors. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Polish-Lithuanian border was porous, but fortresses like Ostróg and Zasław guarded key crossings. Otaman Andrij Mohyła personally, with three Cossacks, sent Jerzy's group across the Commonwealth border. To avoid official scrut
2 CONC iny, the Cossacks and the Jerzy’s group passed through dense forests, skirting the trade routes. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chapter 18. Arrival in the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth: Crossing into Uncertainty – the Road to Wołyń – a Land of Unrest (Late July 1679)''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT Once across the border, they officially entered the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth, but their troubles were far from over. When Jerzy finally crossed the border, he had no time to rest. His first priority was securing protection, 
2 CONC as Muscovite agents could still pursue him. He sought out Polish noble contacts who had reason to sympathize with him—fellow Lithuanian nobles who remembered the Trubecki family’s long-standing ties to the Commonwealth. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jerzy had decided to use his real Polish name in case anyone was seriously interested, not to use a false name, and to tell the truth about their escape, not to everyone he met, of course, but again when the situation required it 
2 CONC or when he encountered the authorities. 
2 CONT
2 CONT By late July, they entered Wołyń – Volhynia, a land of noble estates and Jesuit monasteries. Here, the danger shifted—from Muscovite pursuit to Polish nobles suspicious of defectors. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The first stretch of their journey took them through the contested lands of Volhynia. Here, Polish nobles held sway, but the presence of Cossacks and local brigands made travel dangerous. The trio followed smaller forest paths rat
2 CONC her than the main trade routes to avoid any unwanted encounters. Piotr Jerzy, though just a boy, was forced to endure long rides on horseback and the ever-present fear of pursuit. 
2 CONT
2 CONT At some point, they might have encountered patrols of Polish nobles or even Lithuanian light cavalry—who would have been wary of three lone travelers so near the border. If they had letters of protection or managed to convince a l
2 CONC ocal noble of their status, they might have secured better accommodations. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From there, his journey led him to Nieśwież, where his uncle Aleksander was rumored to be. The castle of Nieśwież, controlled by the powerful Radziwiłł family, was one of the most formidable strongholds in the region. If Jerzy was
2 CONC  granted asylum there, he would be beyond Moscow’s reach. 
2 CONT
2 CONT However, his escape was only the beginning of a larger struggle. By returning to the Commonwealth, he had reopened old wounds and entered a world where his family’s name still carried weight but also uncertainty. His son, Piotr, w
2 CONC ould now grow up in a vastly different political environment—one where he would be raised as a Polish-Lithuanian noble rather than a Muscovite prince. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Would Jerzy reclaim his family’s former status, or would he find himself a mere exile in a land that had once been home? One thing was certain: he had chosen freedom over submission, and in doing so, he had set a course that would
2 CONC  shape not just his fate, but the destiny of his son and the legacy of the Trubecki name. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 18. 1. The Grebnevo Estate’s Fate After Jerzy’s Escape 
2 CONT
2 CONT After Jerzy fled to Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth in 1679 everyone thought he had disappeared. His Muscovite holdings, including Grebnevo, were placed under the ownership of his sons Iwan and Iouri. In 1697, the estate was formal
2 CONC ly divided, with Iwan Iouriewitch Troubetzkoy Bolshoy (1667–1750) receiving full ownership of Grebnevo, maintaining it for another 53 years. Despite Jerzy’s exile, Grebnevo remained a noble estate, continuing to serve as a residen
2 CONC ce for the Troubetzkoy family for generations to come. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chapter 19. The March Through Podolia: The Road to Mińsk and the Unseen Dangers (Late July to Early August 1679)''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT As they pressed westward, the landscape changed—forests gave way to rolling fields and estates of Polish and Ruthenian magnates. The danger here was less from Muscovite forces and more from the unpredictable politics of the Common
2 CONC wealth. The Trubecki family, though of noble blood, was still of Muscovite lineage, and some Polish lords might have seen them as potential spies rather than allies. 
2 CONT
2 CONT They sought refuge in a Franciscan monastery, which often provided shelter to traveling nobles. Monastery, though safer, could still be a source of intrigue—spies lurked everywhere, and even the clergy could not always be trusted.
2 CONC
2 CONT
2 CONT 19. 1. A Safe Haven: A Franciscan Monastery? 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jerzy sought shelter in the Klasztor franciszkański w Międzyrzeczu Ostrogskim – Franciscan monastery in Międzyrzecz Ostrogskie—where exiled nobles sometimes found sanctuary. The monks provided food, medical care, and vital informa
2 CONC tion on the safest roads to Nieśwież. If they were delayed, Piotr Jerzy spent time learning Latin or Polish prayers, further preparing him for life in the Commonwealth. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Food was scarce during this phase of the journey. They survived on dried meats, black bread, and whatever they could purchase or trade along the way. Piotr Jerzy, despite his young age, had to harden quickly—his noble upbringing o
2 CONC ffered little protection from the realities of exile. 
2 CONT
2 CONT At some point, they reached the town of Mińsk. Here, they faced a new risk—ensuring they were not betrayed or detained before reaching their final destination. With Muscovite forces far behind, the trio traveled more openly. They 
2 CONC reached Mińsk in early August. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chapter 20. A Brief Respite in Mińsk: Shelter and Caution (Early August 1679)''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT By early August 1679, after weeks of hard travel through the contested borderlands between Muscovy and the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth, Jerzy Wigunt Trubecki, his 9-year-old son Piotr Jerzy Trubecki, and his true-hearted adjuta
2 CONC nt Jan Bohdanowicz Rusiecki, now just Jan Bohdan Rusiecki, arrived in Mińsk. This town, a growing provincial center of the Grand Duchy of Lithuania, offered a brief respite before their final leg to Nieśwież. However, caution was 
2 CONC still necessary—Mińsk had Polish-Lithuanian officials, local nobles, and mercenaries who might question their presence. Jerzy chose to keep a low profile, avoiding unnecessary encounters with authorities or Radziwiłł envoys. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 20. 1. Mińsk in 1679 – A City of Trade and Culture 
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time, Mińsk was a bustling trading town, lying on the route between Vilnius, Warsaw, and Smolensk. It was home to a diverse population—Poles, Lithuanians, Ruthenians, Jews, and even some Tatar communities—who worked as merc
2 CONC hants, craftsmen, and farmers. The town’s central market square was alive with activity: traders selling furs, wax, honey, cloth, and imported goods from the West. The streets were lined with wooden houses, but some of the churche
2 CONC s and noble residences were built of brick, standing out against the cityscape. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The town was surrounded by wooden fortifications and watchtowers, remnants of past conflicts with Muscovy and Cossacks. The central Mińsk Castle, still used by local administrators and military commanders, stood on a hill near the
2 CONC  Svislach River. Though the warfront had moved away, rumors of raids and shifting alliances were always in the air. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 20. 2. A Visit to the Church of St. Simon and St. Helena 
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite their caution, Jerzy and his companions took time to visit one of Mińsk’s sacred sites—the Church of St. Simon and St. Helena. This was an old Ruthenian Orthodox church, known for its ties to the local Orthodox community a
2 CONC nd its iconostasis of remarkable craftsmanship. Although Jerzy had officially been a Muscovite noble, his ancestry was deeply rooted in the Orthodox traditions of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth, making this a fitting place for
2 CONC  reflection. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Inside the church, the atmosphere was filled with the scent of beeswax candles and incense, and the walls were adorned with richly painted frescoes. Jerzy, despite his rejection of Muscovy, crossed himself in the traditional Ortho
2 CONC dox manner, offering prayers for his late wife Irina Vasilievna and for the uncertain future ahead. Piotr, the young boy, remained quiet, sensing the weight of the moment. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 20. 3. Other Cultural Sights in Mińsk 
2 CONT
2 CONT While avoiding unnecessary attention, the trio still managed to take in some of Mińsk’s notable landmarks: The Jesuit Collegium – Although Jesuits were associated with Catholic influence, their college was a prominent intellectual
2 CONC  center. Jerzy’s adjutant Jan, who was educated in the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth, pointed out the imposing brick structure as they passed by. The Town Hall (Ratusz) – A grand building where the town’s magistrates and official
2 CONC s met, its white walls and arched windows reflected the growing influence of Western architecture in Mińsk. The Dominican Monastery – An important Catholic stronghold, known for its libraries and skilled scribes. While Jerzy avoid
2 CONC ed direct contact, his adjutant Jan remarked on its role in local governance. The Świsłocz River Banks – In the evening, they found a secluded spot by the river, watching merchant boats pass under the wooden bridge, carrying grain
2 CONC  and timber toward the larger markets of the Commonwealth. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 20. 4. A Safe but Brief Stay: through Mińsk to Nieśwież 
2 CONT
2 CONT While Mińsk was a relatively safe haven compared to the Muscovite frontier, Jerzy still exercised caution. He refrained from engaging with Polish-Lithuanian nobles or military commanders, fearing that someone might still question 
2 CONC his loyalty or see an opportunity for political intrigue. Instead, he relied on local Orthodox merchants and minor gentry—contacts from his earlier days before Muscovite service—to help arrange supplies for their journey. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After a few days of quiet rest, fresh horses were secured, and the small party departed under the cover of dawn, leaving Mińsk behind as they made their way toward Nieśwież—where perhaps, perhaps not, Aleksander Wigunt Czarny Ksią
2 CONC żę Trubecki awaited them. 
2 CONT
2 CONT By the time they approached Nieśwież Castle, the seat of the powerful Radziwiłł family, they were exhausted and worn thin from even weeks of hardship. The castle itself, a grand fortress with high walls and lavish halls, would hav
2 CONC e been a sight of salvation. If Jerzy Wigunt had prearranged safe passage with the Radziwiłłs, he would have been welcomed as an ally. If not, he would have needed to plead his case, relying on old alliances or the Radziwiłłs’ enm
2 CONC ity towards Muscovy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chapter 21. Arrival at the Gates of Nieśwież (Mid-August 1679)''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT After weeks of perilous travel, by mid-August 1679, Jerzy Wigunt Trubecki, his 9-year-old son Piotr Jerzy, and his adjutant-friend Jan finally reached the gates of Nieśwież Castle, the grand stronghold of the Radziwiłł family. The
2 CONC ir journey had taken them across lawless steppe, Cossack-controlled lands, and Polish-Lithuanian estates. The sight was breathtaking. Nieśwież Castle, one of the grandest fortresses in the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth, stood pro
2 CONC ud and imposing, its high walls and towers reflecting the power of the Radziwiłł dynasty. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The weary travelers were stopped at the gate by the castle guards. At the castle gates, Polish-Lithuanian hussars and Radziwiłł guards observed them warily, but as soon as Jerzy identified himself. A messenger was sent inside, and
2 CONC  after a tense wait, they were granted entry. The castle’s heavy oak doors creaked open, and the group was led into the castle’s inner courtyard, where a servant announced their arrival to Aleksander Wigunt Czarny Trubecki. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jerzy, once a Muscovite voivode, would have been recognized but scrutinized—his defection made him a politically sensitive figure. Piotr Jerzy, exhausted from the journey, clung to his father’s side, wide-eyed at the sight of the 
2 CONC armored knights and banners flying over the fortress. The adjutant Jan, battle-worn but steadfast, kept his hand near his sword hilt, ready for anything. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The towering fortress, with its ornate spires and golden-domed chapel, stood as a testament to the wealth and power of the Commonwealth. The banners of the Radziwiłłs and Trubeckis fluttered in the warm summer breeze, and the inne
2 CONC r courtyards echoed with the sound of horses, armored retainers, and servants bustling about their duties. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When Jerzy Wigunt entered, there was a moment of silence. Would the old prince welcome his long-exiled nephew—or regard him with suspicion, given his past loyalty to Muscovy? Within minutes, the massive oak doors creaked open, whe
2 CONC re a familiar face awaited them. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chapter 22. A Reunion: The Meeting at Nieśwież Castle (Mid-August 1679)''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT Standing at the top of the marble steps was Aleksander Wigunt Czarny Książę Trubecki, the last ruling prince of their family’s lands, stood waiting. Though aged (about 74 years old), he remained an imposing figure, dressed in a bl
2 CONC ack kontusz lined with fur, a signet ring on his gnarled hand. Now well into his seventies but still a commanding presence. His dark noble robes, embroidered with gold thread, and a fur-lined kontusz gave him an air of quiet autho
2 CONC rity, but his expression was not one of stern judgment—only quiet anticipation and joy. The moment his sharp blue-gray eyes fell upon Jerzy, a broad smile broke across his face. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Now, they stood before Aleksander Wigunt Czarny Książę Trubecki, the last ruling prince of the Principality of Trubetsk (1644–1657) and Jerzy's uncle. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Aleksander (in Polish): 
2 CONT "Jerzy! By God, look at you!" Aleksander’s deep voice boomed across the courtyard, his arms opening wide in welcome. He descended the steps with surprising swiftness for his age, embracing his long-lost nephew before Jerzy could e
2 CONC ven manage a proper greeting. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jerzy laughed, clapping his uncle on the back. 
2 CONT Jerzy (in Polish): 
2 CONT "It’s been too long, Uncle! Far too long."
2 CONT
2 CONT Aleksander pulled back just enough to examine him, his eyes filled with unspoken emotion. 
2 CONT "You look like your father when he was your age," he said gruffly, though there was warmth in his tone. Then, Aleksander took a step forward, his sharp eyes scanning his nephew’s weary face before his gaze shifted downward, settli
2 CONC ng on the young boy, who carried the Trubecki bloodline into the next generation, standing beside Jerzy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Piotr Jerzy, though tired from the journey, stood tall, his youthful face full of curiosity and quiet determination. For a brief moment, the old prince simply studied him, as if searching for traces of their shared bloodline. Then
2 CONC  his stern expression softened completely, and with a chuckle, he knelt slightly to meet the boy’s eye level. 
2 CONT
2 CONT A long pause followed. Then, Aleksander said to Piotr Jerzy, who had stood silent but observant throughout the exchange. 
2 CONT Aleksander (to the boy): 
2 CONT "And this must be Piotr Jerzy," he said, his voice gentler now. "So, you are the one carrying the Trubecki name into the future?"
2 CONT
2 CONT Piotr Jerzy, though only nine years old, straightened his posture and met his great-uncle’s gaze, answering in a quiet voice. 
2 CONT "Yes, sir," he said firmly. "And I will make our family proud." 
2 CONT
2 CONT A flicker of approval crossed Aleksander’s face. He saw in the boy something of the old Trubecki pride—a spirit that had not been crushed by the years in Muscovy. Aleksander let out a booming laugh, ruffling the boy’s hair. 
2 CONT "Ha! You already have, lad! Come here!" 
2 CONT With surprising ease, he lifted Piotr into a brief, warm embrace, then set him down with a satisfied nod. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The old prince’s eyes flicked to the adjutant, a man who had been more than a mere servant—likely a longtime companion, perhaps even an old friend of the family. Aleksander turned to the adjutant, his eyes gleaming with recognitio
2 CONC n. 
2 CONT "And you, my old friend—" he said, clasping the man’s arm like a brother-in-arms. "Still by my nephew’s side, after all these years?" 
2 CONT
2 CONT The adjutant Jan grinned, clearly relieved that the reception was a happy one. He gave a deep bow, replying simply, 
2 CONT "To my lord, always. Someone had to keep him out of trouble." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Aleksander barked out another laugh. 
2 CONT "Then you’ve had your hands full!" 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chapter 23. The Meeting in the Great Hall (Mid-August 1679)''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT And they were ushered into the castle’s grand hall. Inside the ornate hall, decorated with Radziwiłł banners, Polish eagles, and frescoes. 
2 CONT
2 CONT With the formalities out of the way, the mood turned festive. Aleksander wasted no time ordering a great feast in Jerzy’s honor, though it was as much a political gathering as a personal reunion. Radziwiłł envoys, Polish nobles, a
2 CONC nd Lithuanian commanders attended, all weighing the significance of Jerzy’s defection. 
2 CONT
2 CONT That evening, the grand hall of Nieśwież Castle came alive with laughter, music, and the clinking of goblets. Polish nobles, Lithuanian lords, and even Radziwiłł envoys joined the celebration, eager to welcome the returning Trubec
2 CONC ki prince. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Seated at the high table, Jerzy and Aleksander exchanged stories, reminiscing about the old days before war and exile. Piotr sat beside them, wide-eyed at the splendor around him. Even the adjutant Jan, usually reserved, found him
2 CONC self drawn into old war stories, laughing as he recounted close calls and daring escapes. 
2 CONT
2 CONT At one point in the evening, Aleksander raised his goblet, his voice cutting through the noise: 
2 CONT "To Jerzy Wigunt Trubecki, my nephew, who has returned home at last!" 
2 CONT
2 CONT The entire hall erupted in cheers, and for the first time in many years, Jerzy felt truly at home. 
2 CONT
2 CONT For the first time in many years, Jerzy and his son slept in safety, surrounded by their own people. Yet, even within the great walls of Nieśwież, they knew that their fight was far from over. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Once within the walls of Nieśwież, they would have finally found rest, warmth, and security. Their journey was over—for now—but the broader struggles of Polish-Lithuanian and Muscovite politics were far from settled. Their journey
2 CONC  was over—but Jerzy’s new life as an exile and ally of the Commonwealth had only begun. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chapter 24. A New Life by the Sea: A New Beginning in Gdańsk – Rebuilding the Trubecki Legacy (1680-1683)''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT With their joyous reunion at Nieśwież Castle behind them, Jerzy Wigunt Trubecki, his son Piotr Jerzy, and his loyal adjutant Jan Bohdan Rusiecki settled into their new lives in the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth. However, their st
2 CONC ory was far from over—Jerzy’s past as a former Muscovite voivode of Kiev, and the ever-present shadow of Muscovy always accompanied his future, while young Piotr Jerzy grew into a nobleman destined to rebuild the Trubecki name. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 24. 1. Jerzy Wigunt’s Next Year: A Life in the Commonwealth – 1680 
2 CONT
2 CONT Now that Jerzy Wigunt Trubecki was back in the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth, his fate was uncertain. Would he be welcomed, distrusted, or simply ignored? 
2 CONT
2 CONT 24. 2. Reclaiming His Place in the Commonwealth 
2 CONT
2 CONT Thanks to his uncle Aleksander Wigunt Czarny Książę Trubecki and the powerful Radziwiłł family, Jerzy was accepted back into the Polish-Lithuanian nobility. In 1680, he was granted estates in the Grand Duchy of Lithuania, near Now
2 CONC ogródek, where he could rebuild the Trubecki family’s standing. His knowledge of Muscovy’s military and politics made him a valuable asset to the Polish-Lithuanian court. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 24. 3. Military and Political Involvement 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1680, Jerzy participated in the military advice of King John III Sobieski. In the following year, he served as an advisor on Ruthenian and Muscovite affairs, helping the Commonwealth understand its eastern enemy. He kept close 
2 CONC ties to Right-Bank Cossacks, particularly Otaman Andrij Mohyła, and those who were still anti-Muscovite, and may have played a role in Commonwealth-Cossack diplomacy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 24. 4. Arrival in Gdańsk – 1680 
2 CONT
2 CONT After a year of political uncertainty, Jerzy Wigunt Trubecki and his son, Piotr Jerzy Trubecki, sought a place where they could begin anew. By 1680, they had traveled northward and settled in Gdańsk (Danzig), the most prominent po
2 CONC rt city of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gdańsk was a city unlike any Jerzy had ever known—its bustling harbor was filled with merchant ships, bringing goods from the Netherlands, Sweden, and England. Tall, red-bricked Hanseatic houses lined the waterfront, and the stree
2 CONC ts echoed with the voices of merchants speaking Polish, German, Dutch, and even French. As a free-spirited nobleman with architectural knowledge, Jerzy found work among the city's guilds and craftsmen, using the experience he had 
2 CONC gained from building the Grebnevo Church in 1671 – Церковь Гребневской иконы Пресвятой Богородицы въ Гребнево – The Church of the Grebnevo Icon of the Most Holy Theotokos in Grebnevo. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Although he had served Muscovy for years, Jerzy was now firmly committed to his place in the Commonwealth. He kept his distance from court intrigues and military affairs, choosing instead to focus on his son’s education and future
2 CONC . Piotr Jerzy, then entering his teenage years, was immersed in the intellectual atmosphere of Gdańsk, where he learned Latin, mathematics, navigation, and the Polish-Lithuanian customs that would shape his identity. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chapter 25. Łeba (since 1683)''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT 25. 1. The Invitation to Łeba and the Church of the Fishermen – 1683-1685 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1683, Jerzy’s expertise in church construction led to an unexpected opportunity. The pastor of the small seaside town of Łeba, located west of Gdańsk, reached out to him. A year earlier, in 1682, a powerful storm had destroyed 
2 CONC the town’s old Church of St. Nicholas, leaving the community without a place of worship. Recognizing Jerzy’s background as a master builder, the pastor invited him to oversee the construction of a new church, dedicated to the fish
2 CONC ermen of Łeba. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jerzy accepted, and by the summer of 1683, he, Piotr Jerzy and Jan Bohdan Rusiecki arrived in Łeba. The new Church of the Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary was to be built using materials salvaged from the old St. Nicholas Chu
2 CONC rch. Construction moved quickly, and by 1685, the tower was completed, with two large bells brought in from Gdańsk. The simple yet sturdy wooden structure reflected Jerzy’s experience with the traditional architecture of Greater L
2 CONC ithuania, with strong oak beams and intricate carvings adorning the interior. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Feeling a deep connection to the sea and the town that had embraced him, Jerzy decided to make Łeba his permanent home. Drawing upon his Lithuanian heritage, he constructed a wooden log castle in the Old Prussian – Greater Lithuan
2 CONC ian style, which he, Piotr Jerzy and Jan Bohdan named Zamek Łeba. The wooden structure overlooking the dunes and the Baltic, provided a homely retreat for the father, son and their faithful adjutant. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chapter 26. Zamek Łeba – The Trubecki Castle by the Sea (since 1687)''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nestled among the Baltic dunes, overlooking the restless sea, Zamek Łeba stood as a landmark to resilience, craftsmanship, and noble tradition. Built in the late 17th century by Jerzy Wigunt Trubecki, his son Piotr Jerzy, and thei
2 CONC r trusted adjutant Jan Bohdan Rusiecki, this wooden log castle combined the architectural legacy of Greater Lithuania with the rustic charm of Old Prussia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 26. 1. Architecture and Design: A Home of Noble Simplicity
2 CONT
2 CONT Unlike the grand stone castles of Poland-Lithuania, Zamek Łeba was a wooden villa, drawing inspiration from the old Lithuanian log estates. Using pine and oak logs sourced from the forests near Gdańsk, the builders created a struc
2 CONC ture that could withstand the northern winds while retaining a sense of warmth and homeliness. The estate was never meant as a military stronghold—it was a place of peace and renewal. 
2 CONT
2 CONT At the heart of the villa stood the great hall, adorned with intricately carved beams bearing the Trubecki family crest. Massive Prussian-style fireplaces warmed the interior, their mantels decorated with Baltic amber and nautical
2 CONC  motifs. The windows were larger than those in Polish palaces, designed to retain heat in winter, yet they offered stunning views of the dunes and the ever-changing sea beyond. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The roof, steep and shingled, was a signature element of old Prussian estates. Jan Bohdan Rusiecki, an experienced builder with an eye for detail, designed the tower that became Zamek Łeba's most distinctive feature. Though modest
2 CONC  in height, it allowed the Trubeckis to observe the coastline, a vital function in a region where storms could turn treacherous. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 26. 2. Jan Bohdan Rusiecki: The Vision Behind the Castle
2 CONT
2 CONT Rusiecki was more than an adjutant; he was a master craftsman and an innovator. His experience in fortifications and Lithuanian noble estates brought a unique touch to Zamek Łeba. He oversaw the careful selection of materials, ens
2 CONC uring that each log fit seamlessly into the next, and introduced elements such as ornate wooden balconies, reminiscent of Polish manor houses. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He also convinced Jerzy Wigunt to integrate Gdańsk-style timber framing in certain sections, adding elegance without compromising strength. The inner courtyard, with its cobblestone pathways and herb garden, bore his mark—a space 
2 CONC meant for quiet contemplation and familial gatherings. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chapter 27. The Castle's Cats: Furry Guardians of the Trubecki Home (since 1687)''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT No noble home would be complete without its four-legged companions, and Zamek Łeba was no exception. Jerzy Wigunt and Piotr Jerzy, fond of animals, kept several long-haired Norwegian Forest Cats—a breed well-suited to the coastal 
2 CONC climate. These cats, known for their intelligence and agility, served both as companions and as protectors against vermin, ensuring the grain stores remained untouched. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Legend had it that one particularly large tabby, named Baltazar, had an uncanny ability to predict storms—his tail would fluff up, and he would dart to higher ground whenever a tempest approached. The Trubeckis trusted his instinc
2 CONC ts more than any sailor’s forecast. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 27. 1. A Landmark Carved in Timber and Time 
2 CONT
2 CONT Zamek Łeba was more than a home; it became a refuge for travelers, a place of culture, and a symbol of adaptation. Over time, it withstood Baltic winters, political upheavals, and the inevitable passage of generations. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Though the original wooden structure did not survive beyond the 19th century, the spirit of the Trubecki estate endured. In 1907, a new small castle was built on the very same site, incorporating elements of the old design. This n
2 CONC ew castle, with its distinctive tower, endured storms, wars, and political upheavals, standing as a landmark to the town’s—and Jerzy’s—unyielding spirit. Today, Zamek Łeba Resort & Spa welcomes visitors to experience the noble tra
2 CONC nquility first envisioned by Jerzy Wigunt, Piotr Jerzy Trubecki, and their faithful companion, Jan Bohdan Rusiecki. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The sea still whispers their story, and perhaps, on a quiet evening, one can still spot a long-haired cat watching the waves, waiting for the next great adventure. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 27. 2. Final Thoughts: A Family of Survivors 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Trubecki family had been caught between two empires—the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth and Muscovy. Jerzy Wigunt’s escape in 1679 had saved his son from a life of servitude to the Tsar, and Piotr Jerzy would go on to restore t
2 CONC heir noble status within Poland-Lithuania. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Their journey was one of betrayal, exile, survival, and redemption—a testament to the resilience of the Trubecki name. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jerzy Wigunt remained in Łeba, teaching his son Piotr Jerzy the ways of a Polish-Lithuanian nobleman. Piotr Jerzy remained a loyal noble of Poland-Lithuania, ensuring that the Trubecki family name would always be remembered as par
2 CONC t of the Commonwealth, not as servants of the Tsar. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The years that followed were peaceful ones. Jerzy lived as a respected builder and nobleman, while Piotr Jerzy grew into adulthood, shaped by the sea air and the resilience of the coastal people. Over time, Zamek Łeba became somet
2 CONC hing of a landmark, contributing to the city's survival against both natural disasters and changing political tides. The legacy of Jerzy Wigunt Trubecki in Łeba remained. The church he built stood for centuries, serving the fisher
2 CONC men and families of the region. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chapter 28. The Symbolic Farewell – The Funeral of Iouri Petrowitch Troubetzkoy (1689)''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT For a decade, Iouri Petrowitch Troubetzkoy had been absent from Muscovy. When he fled in 1679 with his young son Piotr, no word had reached his family in Moscow or Grebnevo. The court whispered rumors of his fate—some believed he 
2 CONC had perished on the road, others imagined he had been captured by brigands or Polish agents. As the years passed without news, the certainty of his death took root among those who had once known him. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On July 12/22, 1689, a solemn funeral was arranged in his honor, a final act of remembrance for a father presumed lost. His surviving children—Iwan, Iouri, and Maria—organized a symbolic burial in the cemetery of the Trinity Lavra
2 CONC  of St. Sergius, the most sacred resting place of Moscow’s nobility. Since no body had ever been recovered, the ceremony was one of spirit rather than flesh, honoring the legacy of a man whose fate remained a mystery. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 28. 1. The Ceremony at the Trinity Lavra of St. Sergius 
2 CONT
2 CONT The procession to the Lavra began early in the morning. A veiled icon was carried at the front, representing the absent boyar, followed by clergy chanting prayers for the departed. Iwan, as the eldest son, led the mourners, his ex
2 CONC pression solemn yet resolute. Beside him, his younger brother Iouri walked in silence, while their sister Maria, dressed in black mourning robes, held a candle as she whispered prayers for their lost father. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Within the walls of the monastery, the funeral rites were performed with great reverence. The service took place under the golden domes of the Trinity Cathedral, where incense filled the air and the glow of candlelight flickered a
2 CONC gainst the icones. The monks chanted the ancient requiem hymns, their voices echoing through the vaulted halls, as the Troubetzkoy family stood in mourning. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 28. 2. The Reactions of Muscovy’s Nobility 
2 CONT
2 CONT The symbolic funeral was not merely a personal affair; it was also a political statement. Iouri Petrowitch had once been a trusted voivode, a noble of high standing. His disappearance had been an embarrassment to the Tsar’s court,
2 CONC  for it was rare for a man of such rank to vanish without a trace. Some whispered that he had died in disgrace, others speculated that he had been secretly executed, and still others, in hushed tones, questioned whether he might s
2 CONC till be alive beyond the borders of Muscovy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT With the funeral complete, the Trubetskoy name was at last able to move forward. Iwan and Iouri, now firmly established within the Muscovite elite, could no longer be burdened by the shadow of their father’s disappearance. The sym
2 CONC bolic burial allowed them to publicly grieve, to close the chapter on uncertainty, and to restore the family’s honor in the eyes of their peers. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Though the bells of the Trinity Lavra rang in mourning, and the clergy spoke of eternal rest, Iouri Petrowitch Troubetzkoy was not dead. On that same day in 1689, as his children prayed for his soul, he was alive and well in the P
2 CONC olish-Lithuanian Commonwealth, watching the waves of the Baltic Sea from his new home in Łeba. But for Muscovy, for the Tsars Ivan V and Peter I, and for his children in Grebnevo, he was gone—buried not in body, but in memory. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''29. Sources''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT * Archiwum państwowe w Gdańsku (State Archives in Gdańsk). 
2 CONT * Archiwum Radziwiłłów (The Radziwiłł Archives). 
2 CONT * Buczyło, Andrzej, “Archiwalia związane z kniaziami Trubeckimi w zespole Archiwum Warszawskie Radziwiłłów – pochodzenie i podział typologiczny” (“Archival materials related to the Trubecki princes in the Radziwiłł Warsaw Archives
2 CONC  collection – origin and typological division”), 2015. 
2 CONT * Davies, B. L., “Warfare, State and Society on the Black Sea Steppe, 1500-1700”, Taylor & Francis, 2007. 
2 CONT * Dolgorukov, P. V., “The Troubetzkoy Princes”. 
2 CONT * Machulsky, E. N., “Our Kraskovo”, Encyclopedia of Russian Villages, 1998. 
2 CONT * Niesiecki, Kasper, “Herbarz Polski”, 18th century. 
2 CONT * Petrov, P. N., “History of the Families of the Russian Nobility”. 
2 CONT * “Słownik Geograficzny Królestwa Polskiego” (“Geographical Dictionary of the Kingdom of Poland”), between 1880 and 1902. 
2 CONT * Wolff, Józef Ludwik, “Kniaziowie litewsko-ruscy od końca czternastego wieku” (“Lithuanian-Ruthenian Princes from the End of the Fourteenth Century”), Warszawa, 1895. — pg 469, 544, 545. 
2 CONT * Долгоруков, Петр, “Российская родословная книга” (“Russian Genealogical Book”), Karl Vingeber's Printing House in St. Petersburg, between 1854 and 1857. 
2 CONT * Трубецкой, Сергей Петрович, “Записки князя С. П. Трубецкого” (“Notes of Prince S. P. Troubetzkoy”), before 1923. 
2 CONT * Чарняўскі Ф. В., “Крыштоф Валадковіч: факты з жыцця і дзейнасці” (“Krzysztof Wołodkowicz: facts from his life and work”), Архіварыус — Дзяржкамархіў, Нацыянальны гістарычны архіў Беларусі, 2009. — issue 7. — pg. 238, 240. 
2 CONT
