1 NAME Alexander I, King of Georgia
1 NAME Александр Константинович // I
2 GIVN Александр Константинович
2 SURN Багратион
2 NSFX I
1 NAME Александр I Великий, царь Грузии
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1386
1 DEAT
2 DATE 7 MAR 1446
1 OCCU KING OF GEORGIA, MONK, Великий; 1412-42, царь Грузии
1 TITL King
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Alexander I, “the Great” (Georgian: ალექსანდრე I დიდი, Aleksandre I Didi) (1386 – between August 26, 1445 and March 7, 1446), of the Bagrationi house, was king of Georgia from 1412 to 1442. Despite his efforts to r
2 CONC estore the country from the ruins left by the Turco-Mongol warlord Timur Leng’s invasions, Georgia never recovered and faced the inevitable fragmentation that was followed by a long period of stagnation. In 1442, he abdicated the 
2 CONC throne and retired to a monastery.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Life
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Marriages and children
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 References
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 See also
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander was the eldest son of Constantine I of Georgia and his wife Natia, daughter of the Georgian diplomat prince Kutsna Amirejibi. He was brought up by his grandmother (Natia’s mother) Rusa (died 1413), an educated and religi
2 CONC ous noblewoman, who greatly influenced the future king’s preoccupations and his enthusiasm for religious building.
2 CONT
2 CONT With his ascend to the throne (1412), Alexander moved to western Georgia and mediated a peace between his vassals, the rival princes of Mingrelia and Abkhazia. Then he, in 1414, met the rebellious prince Atabeg Ivane Jakeli of Sam
2 CONC tskhe on battlefield and forced him into submission. Having dealt with these powerful feudal lords, he, aided by Catholicos Patriarch Shio II, began a program the restoration of major Georgian fortresses and churches. He imposed a
2 CONC  temporary building tax on his subjects from 1425 to 1440, but despite the king’s efforts many towns and villages, once flourished, were left in ruin and overgrown by forest.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1431, he re-conquered Lorri, a Georgian marchland occupied by the Kara Koyunlu Turkoman tribesmen of Persia who had frequently raided the southern Georgian marches from there and had even sacked Akhaltsikhe in 1416.[1] Around 1
2 CONC 434/5, Alexander encouraged the Armenian prince Beshken II Orbelian to attack the Kara Koyunlu clansmen in Syunik (Siunia) and, for his victory, granted him Lorri under terms of vassalage. In 1440, Alexander refused to pay tribute
2 CONC  to Jahan Shah of the Kara Kouynlu. In March, Jahan Shah surged into Georgia with 20,000 troops, destroyed the city of Samshvilde and sacked the capital city Tbilisi. He massacred thousands of Christians, put heavy indemnity on Ge
2 CONC orgia, and returned to Tabriz.
2 CONT
2 CONT In order to reduce the power of frequently rebellious aristocracy, he opposed them by appointing his sons – Vakhtang, Demetre, and George – as his co-rulers in Kakheti, Imereti and Kartli, respectively. This, however, proved to be
2 CONC  even dangerous to the kingdom’s integrity and the fragile unity kept by Alexander would soon disappear under his sons. For this reason, Alexander the Great is frequently claimed to have disintegrated Georgia and said not to deser
2 CONC ve his epithet "the Great" his people bestowed on him.[2] This appellation dates almost from his own day, however, and as the modern Georgian historian Ivane Javakhishvili presumes, might have been related to the large-scale resto
2 CONC ration projects launched by the king and his initial success in the struggle with the Turkmen nomads[3].
2 CONT
2 CONT As worldly problems overwhelmed his kingdom, Alexander abdicated the throne in 1442 and retired to a monastery under the name of Athanasius.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Marriages and children
2 CONT
2 CONT He married c. 1411 Dulandukht, daughter of Beshken II Orbelian, by whom he had two sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT Vakhtang IV, King of Georgia
2 CONT
2 CONT Demetre (1413–1452), co-ruler in Imereti; father of Constantine II
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander’s second marriage with Tamar (died 1455), daughter of prince Alexander I of Imereti, took place around 1414. Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT George VIII, the last king of a united Georgia and the first king of independent Kakheti
2 CONT
2 CONT David (1417 – 1471), Catholicos Patriarch of Georgia
2 CONT
2 CONT Zaal (1425 – c. 1442)
2 CONT
2 CONT Bagrationi (1415 – c. 1438) who married, 1425, the emperor John IV of Trebizond
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ According to the 15th-century Armenian historian Thomas of Metsoph (T’ovma Metsobets’i), the Kara Kouynlu leader Kara Yusuf invaded Samtskhe and pillaged its capital Akhaltsikhe in 1416 in response to the profanation inflicted b
2 CONC y the local Christian Georgians and Armenians on a mosque.
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Suny (1994), page 45
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Ivane Javakhishvili (1982), page 243
2 CONT
