1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Yusuf I was the son of Beka Jaqeli, Prince of Samtskhe. He obtained the Sublime Porte without problems. Yusuf I was a determined Muslim who governed in the total dependence of the Ottoman government represented loc
2 CONC ally by the Erzurum Pasha.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bearing a great hatred of Christianity, he promoted the development of Islam, so much so that Christians gradually disappeared from Samtskhe. Part of the rural population fiercely resisted this Islamization, though many Meskhetian
2 CONC s converted to Islam or migrated to Imereti . Now almost entirely Muslim, the Samtskhe enjoyed the special protection of Ottoman rulers who used the hereditary governors of the province for their interventions in Imereti and Easte
2 CONC rn Georgia (Kartli).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1636, Yusuf Jaqeli granted the right of passage and the hospitality of its states to Rostom Khan, King of Kartli and vassal of Iran, so he could marry his wife Mary of Mingrelia, sister of Dadian Levan II and daughter of Mamuka
2 CONC  I Dadiani.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon his death in 1647, Yusuf I Jaqeli was succeeded by his son Rostom I Jaqeli.
