1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Regina Wisniowiecka (also known as Mohilianka[1] or Mohylianka; her first name is rendered as Raina or Irina) was a Polish noble lady originally from Moldavia. She was the wife of Michał Wiśniowiecki and patron of 
2 CONC Eastern Orthodox Christianity in the Polish–Lituanian Commonwealth.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the daughter of Moldavian hospodar Ieremia Movilă and Elisabeta Movilă, and she was a cousin of Metropolite of Kiev Peter Mohyla. Due to the conflict with Michael the Brave, 1600 Ieremia was forced to move his family to Ga
2 CONC licia, according to the Polish Biographical Dictionary, to the town of Ustia (today it is a village of Ustia-Zelene in Chortkiv Raion).
2 CONT
2 CONT At age 14 Regina married Polish magnate of Ruthenian origin Michał Wiśniowiecki in 1603 in the city of Suceava, Moldavia.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Regina was married only to Michał Wiśniowiecki and had at least four children with him.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Jeremi Wiśniowiecki (1612-1651)
2 CONT * Alexander Wiśniowiecki (?-1629)
2 CONT * Jerzy Wiśniowiecki (?-1629)
2 CONT * Anna (?-1648), married Zbigniew Firlej
