0 @N6000000002188002122@ NOTE Kulan
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Kulan or Khulan, was an empress of Genghis Khan and head of the second Court of Genghis Khan. Her status in the Mongol Empire was second only to Grand Empress Borte.
1 CONT
1 CONT Kulan was a daughter of a Merkit chief. She was offered to Genghis Khan as a gift after the chief's surrender. Genghis Khan was enamored of Kulan and following his coronation as Khan, installed her as an empress. She had a son, Ge
1 CONC lejian, with Genghis Khan, and Gelejian's status was second only to Borte's four sons as he grew up. Genghis Khan was very fond of Kulan, and most of the time she was the only empress accompanying him on many campaigns, notably th
1 CONC e western campaign against the Khwarezmid Empire. She continued to travel with Genghis Khan until she died during one of Genghis Khan's campaign against India. She was buried under thick ice.
1 CONT
1 CONT As one of many wives of Genghis Khan, Kulan held a special place both in the Mongol Empire and in Genghis Khan's heart.
0 @N6000000002188002149@ NOTE Yesugei
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Yesugei Baghatur or Yesugei The Brave (11XX–1180) (Yesügei, Yesükhei) was a Mongol Khan (or ruler). He was the son of Bartan-baghatur, who was the son of Qabul, who was recognized as a Khagan by the Jin Dynasty. Qabul Khagan was, 
1 CONC in turn, the grandson of the Mongol chief Qaidu, the first to try to unite all of the Mongols.
1 CONT
1 CONT Yesugei was chief of the Kiyad (possibly the Taichiut or Yakka) khanate between approximately 1153 and 1180. He had four sons and one daughter by his chief wife, Hoelun: Temujin (son, later known as Genghis Khan), Kasar (son), Kha
1 CONC ji'un (son), Temuga (son), and Temulin (daughter). He also had two sons by a lesser wife: Bektor and Beigutai. He was poisoned by Tartars while sharing a meal on the way home from finding his son Temujin (Genghis Khan) a wife.
1 CONT
1 CONT Yesugei's wife was Oyelun (Oelun, Ho’elun, Hoelun), a daughter of the Olkunut forest tribe. Oyelun was abducted by Yesugei and his brothers from her newlywed husband of the Merkit tribe as they were traveling back to the Merkit ca
1 CONC mp. Yesugei then made Oyelun his chief wife, a rare honor since Khans had several wives, and only one could bear his heirs.
0 @N6000000002188002161@ NOTE Hoelun
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Hoelun was the mother of Mongol Khan Genghis Khan and the grandmother of Ogedei Khan, first emperor of Yuan Dynasty. She was the wife of Yesükhei, the chief of the Kiyad clan. After Yesükhei's death, his clan abandoned Hoelun and 
1 CONC his sons including Genghis Khan (Temujin by birth). Therefore Genghis Khan was raised in the harsh environment of the Mongolian steppes. According to Mongolian legend, Hoelun taught Genghis and his brothers the basics of unity and
1 CONC  support for one another, even though Temujin killed his brother in a disagreement over hunting spoils.
1 CONT
1 CONT Hoelun was born to the Olkunut tribe. She later married a member of the Merkit tribe, but was abducted by Yesugei on her way back to the Merkit camp. Yesugei made Hoelun his chief wife. This was an honor, since only the chief wife
1 CONC  could give birth to his heirs.
0 @N6000000002188002170@ NOTE Jöchi Khasar
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Jöchi Khasar (or Qasar) was one of Chinggis Khan's three full brothers. According to the Jami al-Tawarikh, his given name was Jöchi and he got the nickname Khasar after his distinguished bravery. He was also called Khabutu Khasar 
1 CONC (Khasar the Archer) because he was a skillful archer.
1 CONT
1 CONT Being given the people and territories by the khan, Chinggis Khan's full brothers Khasar, Khachiun and Temüge Odchigin formed the Left Wing of the Mongol Empire in the eastern edge of Greater Mongolia while Chinggis Khan's three s
1 CONC ons Jochi, Chaghatai and Ögödei made up the Right Wing in the western edge. The Right Wing saw a significant expansion to the west but the Left Wing did not have so much land to be conquered.
1 CONT
1 CONT Unlike the Right Wing where properties were equally divided, Odchigin was favored over Khasar and Khachiun in the Left Wing. Khasar's ulus (people and secondarily, territory) was significantly smaller than Odchigin's. His original
1 CONC  territory was located to the west of the Khingan Mountains and was surrounded by the Argun (Ergüne) and Khayilar Rivers, and the Külün Mountain. After the conquest of China, Khasarid princes had at least two additional territorti
1 CONC es in Shandong and Jiangxi respectively.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Descendants
1 CONT The princely houses of Khasar, Khachiun and Odchigin tended to coordinate with the five powerful clans Jalayir, Khunggirad, Ikires, Uruud and Mangghud. They were usually led by princes from Odchigin's house. At Arigh Bukha's rebel
1 CONC lion, the three princely houses supported Khubilai under leadership of Odchigin's grandson Taghachar.
1 CONT
1 CONT Among Khasarid princes, the third family head Yesüngge is probably the most famous. He was a son of Khasar and succeeded his brother Yegü. He is the hero of the Yesüngge Inscription (formerly known as the Chinggis Stone). The prin
1 CONC cely house was succeeded by Yesüngge's son Esen Emügen and then Emügen's son Shigdür. Although Shigdür joined the rebellion against Khubilai led by Odchigin's great-great-grandson Nayan, the princely house survived without confusi
1 CONC on. The sixth head Babusha was given the title of Qi Wang (??) by Khayishan Külüg Khan in 1307. It seems that Khasarid princes continued to hold the title even after the empire retreated from China.
1 CONT
1 CONT It is unknown what happened to Khasarid princes from the late Yuan Dynasty to the middle 15th century because of the confusion caused by the Mongols' withdrawal from China. Mongolian chronicles compiled from the 17th century to th
1 CONC e early 18th century contain some records on Khasar's descendants but they are considered mostly unhistorical by historians. In particular, Altan Tobchi by Mergen Gegen (not to be confused with Lubsandanjin's Altan Tobchi) exagger
1 CONC ated the influence of Khasarid princes as the author himself descended from Khasar.
1 CONT
1 CONT It is widely accepted that Khasar's descendant Bolunai was a historical figure since his existence is confirmed in contemporary Chinese sources of 1463, 1467 and 1470. Mongolian chronicles say that Bolunai's brother Ünebalad kille
1 CONC d Muulikhai of the Ongliud, a descendant of Chinggis's half brother Belgütei. Another famouns story about Ünebalad tells that he proposed to Mandukhai Khatun, a widow of Manduulun Khan but that she chose the Chinggisid infant Batu
1 CONC  Möngke (Dayan Khan) over him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Bolunai led the Khorchin Mongols. His descendants ruled the Khorchin, Jalayid, Dörbed and Ghorlus of the Jirim League, the Aru Khorchin of the Juu Uda League and the Dörben Keüked, Muu Mingghan and Urad of the Ulaanchab League in 
1 CONC the Manchu Qing Empire's administration. Among them, Khorchin princes established matrimonial relationship with the imperial family of Aisin Gioro at the early stage of the Manchu rise to power, and held top-ranking princely title
1 CONC s (hošoi cin wang) throughout the Qing Dynasty.
0 @N6000000002187840123@ NOTE Sorghaghtani Beki
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT  Sorghaghtani BekiSorghaghtani Beki (died 1252) was the mother of four of the great figures in Mongol history, especially Möngke Khan, Kublai Khan, and Hulagu Khan; she had eleven sons with Tolui, the youngest son of Genghis Khan.
1 CONC  A Kerait and a Nestorian Christian, William of Rubruck claimed she was the niece of Prester John. She was the eldest daughter-in-law of Genghis Khan and known as a ruthless, brilliant politician who pushed her children and their 
1 CONC interests into rule of the Mongol Empire. Both Muslim and Christian historians speak highly of her: “If I were to see among the race of women another woman like this, I should say that the race of women was far superior to that of
1 CONC  men”[citation needed]; “Among the Tartars this lady is... more powerful than anyone else except the khan of the Golden Horde”[citation needed]; “She was extremely intelligent and able and towered above all the women in the world.
1 CONC ”[citation needed] Sorghaghtani died in 1252, but she was given the title of “empress” in 1310 in a ceremony that included a Nestorian mass.
1 CONT
1 CONT The descendants of Sorghaghtani and Tolui are alive to this day, including many Chinese Christians in South-East Asia.
0 @N6000000002187576439@ NOTE Möngke Khan
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Möngke Khan (1208-1259, also transliterated as Mongke, Mongka, Möngka, Mangu) was the fourth Great Khan of the Mongol Empire. He was the son of Tolui and Sorghaghtani Beki, brother of Hulagu, and a grandson of Genghis Khan. He was
1 CONC  the Great Khan of the Mongol Empire from 1251 to 1259.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Möngke is noted as participating in the European campaign of 1236-1242, in the campaign against the steppe peoples to the southeast of the Russian principalities, the destruction of Kiev, and the assault of Hungary. In the summer 
1 CONC of 1241, before the premature end of the campaign, Möngke returned home.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the death of the third Great Khan, Güyük, Möngke found himself the champion of the factions of Genghis' descendants who aimed to supplant the branch of Ögedei. Batu, the senior male of the family, had almost come to open war
1 CONC fare with Güyük in 1248, the khan's early death precluding this. Batu joined forces with Tolui's widow to outmaneuver the regent, Ögedei's widow Oghul Ghaimish. Batu called a kuriltai in Siberia in 1250, which was protested as not
1 CONC  being in Mongolia proper. However, Batu ignored the opposition, had his brother Berke call a kuriltai within Mongolia, and elected Möngke khan in 1251.
1 CONT
1 CONT Realizing they had been outmaneuvered, the Ögedeiid faction attempted to overthrow Möngke under the pretext of paying him homage, but their conspiracy was clumsy and easily avoided. Oghul Ghaimish was sewn up into a sack and drown
1 CONC ed.
1 CONT
1 CONT Möngke, as khan, seemed to take much more seriously the legacy of world conquest he had inherited than did Güyük. He concerned himself more with the war in China, outflanking the Song Dynasty through the conquest of Yunnan in 1254
1 CONC  and an invasion of Indochina, which allowed the Mongols to invade from north, west, and south. Taking command personally late in the decade, he captured many of the fortified cities along the northern front. These actions ultimat
1 CONC ely rendered the conquest a matter of time. He dispatched his brother Hulagu to the southwest, an act which was to expand the Mongol Empire to the gates of Egypt. European conquest was neglected due to the primacy of the other two
1 CONC  theaters, but Möngke's friendliness with Batu ensured the unity of empire.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, while conducting the war in China at Fishing town in Chongqing, Möngke was shot to death by cannon (in 1259), which aborted Hülegü's campaign in Syria and Egypt, staved off defeat for the Song, and caused a civil war that
1 CONC  destroyed the unity and invincibility of the Mongol Empire.
1 CONT
1 CONT In some texts Möngke is said to be killed by a rock falling onto his head while attacking Fishing town, while other texts said Mongke died of an illness or injury when attacking Fishing town.
1 CONT
1 CONT In Jinyong's novel The Return of the Condor Heroes, Mongke is said to have been killed by Yang Guo, who hurled a rock with qi-enhanced power and shattered the khan's internal organs.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Güyük Khan Great Khan of Mongol Empire
1 CONT 1251–1259 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Kublai Khan 
0 @N6000000002187576417@ NOTE Kublai Khan
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Khubilai Khan 
1 CONT Birth and death: Sept. 23, 1215–
1 CONT Feb. 18, 1294 
1 CONT Clan name (obogh): Borjigin1 (????????)
1 CONT Bei'erzhijin2 (????) or
1 CONT Bo'erjijite3 (?????) 
1 CONT Sublineage name4:
1 CONT (yasun) Khiyad5 (????)
1 CONT Qiwowen6 (???)
1 CONT or Qiyan (??) 
1 CONT Given name: Khubilai (???????)
1 CONT Hubilie (???) 
1 CONT Khan of the Mongols 
1 CONT Dates of reign: May 5, 1260–Dec. 17, 1271 
1 CONT Emperor of Yuan China 
1 CONT Dates of reign: Dec. 18, 12717–Feb. 18, 1294 
1 CONT Era Names: Zhongtong, Zhiyuan 
1 CONT Dynasty: Ön8, now Yüanh 9 (????)
1 CONT Yuan (?) 
1 CONT Khan name: Setsen Khan (????? ????)
1 CONT Xuechan Han (???) 
1 CONT Temple name: (Mongolian name to be added)
1 CONT Shizu (??) 
1 CONT Posthumous name:
1 CONT (short) Never used short 
1 CONT Posthumous name:
1 CONT (full) (Mongolian name to be added)
1 CONT Emperor Shengde
1 CONT Shengong Wenwu
1 CONT (????????) 
1 CONT General note: Names given in Mongolian, then in Chinese.
1 CONT See Notes 
1 CONT
1 CONT Kublai Khan, Khubilai Khan or "the last of the Great Khans" (September 23, 1215 - February 18, 1294) (Mongolian: ??????? ????, Chinese: ???, also spelled as Kubilay Han in Turkic), was a Mongol military leader. He was Khagan (1260
1 CONC –1294) of the Mongol Empire as well as the founder and the first Emperor (1271–1294) of the Chinese Yuan Dynasty.
1 CONT
1 CONT Born the second son of Tolui and Sorghaghtani Beki and the grandson of Genghis Khan, he succeeded his brother Möngke in 1260. Kublai Khan's brother, Hulagu, conquered Persia and founded the Ilkhanate. Kublai also had a cousin name
1 CONC d Kaidu, who died in 1301.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Early Years 
1 CONT 2 Mongol Empire 
1 CONT 3 Emperor of Yuan 
1 CONT 3.1 Invasions of Japan 
1 CONT 4 Dadu 
1 CONT 5 Later Life 
1 CONT 6 Notes 
1 CONT 7 Popular culture 
1 CONT 8 External links 
1 CONT 9 References 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Early Years
1 CONT Kublai was the grandson of Genghis Khan. As a youth, he studied Chinese culture and became enamored with it. In 1251, his elder brother Mongke became Khan of the Mongol Empire, and Kublai became the governor of Southern territorie
1 CONC s of Mongol Empire. During his years as governor, Kublai managed his territory well, boosting the agricultural output of Henan and increasing social welfare spendings after receiving Xi'an. These acts received great acclaim from C
1 CONC hinese warlords and were essential to the building of Yuan Dynasty.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1253, Kublai was ordered to attack Yunnan. He eventually attacked and destroyed the Kingdom of Dali. In 1258, Möngke put Kublai in command of the Eastern Army and summoned him to assist with the attacks on Sichuan and Yunnan. B
1 CONC efore Kublai could arrive in 1259, words had reached him that Möngke had died. Despite his brother's death, Kublai continued to attack Wuhan. Soon thereafter, Kublai received news that his younger brother had usurped power. Kublai
1 CONC  quickly reached a peace agreement with Song troops and rode back north to the Mongolian plains.
1 CONT
1 CONT Both his brother and Kublai crowned themselves Khan in 1260, and the two brothers battled for three years before Kublai finally won. However, during this civil war, Yizhou governor Li revolted against Mongol rule. The revolt was s
1 CONC wiftly crushed by Kublai, but this incident instilled in him a strong distrust of ethnic Hans. After he became emperor, Kublai instituted several anti-Han laws, such as banning the titles of and tithes to Han Chinese warlords.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Mongol Empire
1 CONT The empire was separated into four khanates, each ruled by a separate khan and overseen by the Great Khan. The Kipchak Khanate (also called the Golden Horde) ruled Russia; the Ilkhanate ruled the Middle East, the Chagatai Khanate 
1 CONC ruled over western Asia, and the Great Khanate controlled Mongolia and eventually the whole of China. The empire reached its greatest extent under Kublai with his conquest of Song Dynasty which was completed by his final victory i
1 CONC n 1279.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Emperor of Yuan
1 CONT As emperor of Yuan Dynasty, Kublai Khan worked to minimize the influences of regional lords who had held immense power before and during the Song Dynasty. His mistrust of ethnic Han Chinese caused him to hire other ethnic group me
1 CONC mbers as officials more often than Han Chinese.
1 CONT
1 CONT At the Eighth Year of Zhiyuan (1271), Kublai Khan officially declared the creation of the Yuan Dynasty, and proclaimed the capital to be at Dadu (Beijing, China) in the following year. To unify China, Kublai Khan began a massive o
1 CONC ffensive against the remnants of Southern Song Dynasty in the 11th year of Zhiyuan, and finally destroyed the Song Dynasty in the 16th year of Zhiyuan, unifying the country at last.
1 CONT
1 CONT He ruled better than his predecessors, promoting economic growth with the rebuilding of the Grand Canal, repairing public buildings, and extending highways. However, Kublai Khan's domestic policy also included some aspects of the 
1 CONC old Mongol living traditions, and as Kublai Khan continued his reign, these traditions would clash more and more frequently with traditional Chinese economical and social culture.
1 CONT
1 CONT He also introduced paper currency although eventually a lack of fiscal discipline and inflation turned this into an economic disaster. He encouraged Chinese arts and demonstrated religious tolerance, except in regards to Taoism. H
1 CONC is capital was at Beijing (then Cambuluc or Dadu ?? lit. big capital). The empire was visited by several Europeans, notably Marco Polo in the 1270s who may have seen the summer capital in Shangdu (?? lit. upper capital or Xanadu).
1 CONT
1 CONT He conquered Dali (Yunnan) and Goryeo (Korea). Under pressure from his Mongolian advisors, Kublai attempted to conquer Japan, Myanmar, Vietnam and Java. All these failed attempts, costly expeditions, along with the introduction of
1 CONC  paper currency caused inflation. However, Kublai Khan also forced warlords from the Northwest and Northeast to capitulate, ensuring stability for those regions. Kublai Khan died in the 31st year of Zhiyuan. (1294)
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Invasions of Japan
1 CONT Main article: Mongol invasions of Japan
1 CONT Kublai Khan twice attempted to invade Japan in search of gold; however, both times the samurai resisted greatly and the weather tore the fleets apart. The first attempted invasion was in 1274 with a fleet of 900 ships. The second 
1 CONC invasion was in 1281 with a fleet of over 1,170 large war junks, each close to 240 feet long. Japanese were prepared for this invasion and they had built a wall several feet high on the island where Mongols were predicted to land 
1 CONC to prevent horses from coming ashore easily. The Campaign was badly synchronized as the Korean fleet reached Japan much ahead of the Chinese fleet. Japanese samurai fought with great valor and defeated the largely Chinese and Kore
1 CONC an army of Mongols. Dr. Kenzo Hayashida, the marine archaeologist who discovered the wreckage of the second invading fleet off the western coast of Takashima, headed their excavation. The findings strongly indicate that Kublai Kha
1 CONC n rushed to conquer Japan and attempted to construct his enormous fleet in only one year (a task that should have taken up to 5 years), which forced the Chinese to use any available ships, particularly river boats, as the basis fo
1 CONC r Khan's fleet in order to achieve readiness on time. Most importantly, the Koreans, then under his control, were forced to build countless ships to contribute to the fleet in both of the invasions. Had Kublai used ocean-going shi
1 CONC ps, which have a curved keel to prevent capsizing, his navy may have survived the journey to and from Japan and may have even conquered it as originally intended.
1 CONT
1 CONT John Pearson, author of Kublai Khan (2005), writes, "The cost of these defeats led the Khan to devalue the central currency, further exacerbating growing inflation. He also increased tax assessments. These economic problems lead t
1 CONC o growing resentment of the Mongols, who paid no taxes, among the Chinese populace." David Nicole writes in The Mongol Conquerors that "these disastrous defeats shattered the myth of Mongol invincibility throughout Asia." He also 
1 CONC wrote that Kublai Khan was determined to mount a third invasion, despite the horrendous cost to the economy and to his and Mongol prestige of the first two defeats, and only his death prevented such a third attempt, despite the un
1 CONC animous agreement of his advisors against such an attempt."
1 CONT
1 CONT In early 2006, marine archaeologists determined that previous theories that Kublai's fleet was made up entirely of ships that came from river boats was found to be weakened as evidence of keel usage began to show up in recent disc
1 CONC overies. One current theory that has yet to be found false is the idea that the new Mongol technology of explosives (grenade-like weapons) may have backfired, potentially from inexperienced engineering, when an attack on Japan wou
1 CONC ld have begun.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Dadu
1 CONT Kublai, a grandson of the Mongol leader Genghis Khan began leading further Mongol advances in the latter years of the 1250s. On 5 May 1260 Kublai was elected Khan at his residence in Shangdu and he began to organize the country. Z
1 CONC hang Wenqian, who was a friend of Guo and like him was a central government official, was sent by Kublai Khan in 1260 to Daming where unrest had been reported in the local population. Guo accompanied Zhang on his mission. Guo was 
1 CONC not only interested in engineering, but he was also an expert astronomer. In particular he was a skilled instrument maker and understood that good astronomical observations depended on expertly made instruments. He now began to co
1 CONC nstruct astronomical instruments, including water clocks for accurate timing and armillary spheres which represent the celestial globe.
1 CONT
1 CONT Zhang advised Kublai Khan that his friend Guo was a leading expert in hydraulic engineering. Kublai knew the importance of water management, for irrigation, transport of grain, and flood control, and he asked Guo to look at these 
1 CONC aspects in the area between Dadu (now Beijing or Peking) and the Yellow River. To provide Dadu with a new supply of water, Guo found the Baifu spring in the Shenshan Mountain and had a 30 km channel built to bring the water to Dad
1 CONC u. He proposed connecting the water supply across different river basins, built new canals with many sluices to control the water level, and achieved great success with the improvements which he was able to make. This pleased Kubl
1 CONC ai Khan and led to Guo being asked to undertake similar projects in other parts of the country. In 1264 he was asked to go to Gansu province to repair the damage that had been caused to the irrigation systems by the years of war d
1 CONC uring the Mongul advance through the region. Guo travelled extensively along with his friend Zhang taking notes of the work which needed to be done to unblock damaged parts of the system and to make improvements to its efficiency.
1 CONC  He sent his report directly to Kublai Khan.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Later Life
1 CONT Kublai, in the later part of his life developed severe gout. He also put on a lot of weight due to excessive eating. This ultimately led to his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Notes
1 CONT General note: Dates given here are in the Julian calendar. They are not in the proleptic Gregorian calendar.
1 CONT
1 CONT Note 1: This is the singular. The plural is Borjigi. 
1 CONT Note 2: This is the most frequent Chinese version of the clan name nowadays. 
1 CONT Note 3: This Chinese version of the clan name was the most frequent during the Qing Dynasty. 
1 CONT Note 4: The Cambridge History of China thinks that Khiyad was a sublineage inside the larger Borjigin clan, but other scholars disagree and think that Borjigin was a sublineage inside the larger Khiyad clan, while there are those 
1 CONC who think that Khiyad and Borjigin were both used interchangeably. 
1 CONT Note 5: This is the plural. The singular is Khiyan. 
1 CONT Note 6: This Chinese version of Khiyad is the one that appears in the Chinese history of the Yuan Dynasty. 
1 CONT Note 7: Founded the Yuan Dynasty on that day. However, was not in control of southern China until February 1276, when the Southern Song emperor was captured and the imperial seal was relinquished to the Yuan. The last pockets of r
1 CONC esistance in southern China fell in 1279. 
1 CONT The Yuan dynasty was the first to think the world was round, but thought that the other parts had horrible devils and spirits in them.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Popular culture
1 CONT Kubla Khan is a poem by Samuel Taylor Coleridge 
1 CONT Kublai Khan is one of the characters in the 1964 Doctor Who serial Marco Polo. 
1 CONT Kublai Khan is featured in the PC video game Civilization IV. In the game, Kublai Khan is a civilization leader of the Mongolian Empire, alongside Genghis Khan. Kublai Khan possesses the traits "Creative" and "Aggressive". 
1 CONT Kublai is mentioned in a Family Guy episode, when Stewie is offered pancakes "from Flappy himself" he replies with "I don't care if they're from Kublai filthy wretched Khan" 
1 CONT Kublai Khan is one of the primary characters in the Italo Calvino novel "Invisible Cities," in which Marco Polo tells him of his travels to different cities. 
1 CONT The poem Kubla Khan is namechecked in the Rush song Xanadu. "To stand within the Pleasure Dome / Decreed by Kubla Khan" 
1 CONT Reference is made in the song What Would Brian Boitano Do? from the film South Park: Bigger, Longer & Uncut written by DVDA (Trey Parker and Matt Stone's band) in which figure skater Brian Boitano "beat up Kublai Khan". 
1 CONT The Kublai Khan is further mentioned in the lyrics of the Frankie Goes To Hollywood song Welcome to the Pleasure Dome. "In Xanadu the Kubla Khan a Pleasure Dome Erect" 
1 CONT Kublai Khan is an often mentioned name in songs by Jedi Mind Tricks 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT External links
